(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3a.8354cdf21c.js"]={"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":1,"stem":"Which groove in the wall of the third ventricle divides the diencephalon into a superiorly located dorsal thalamus and an inferiorly located hypothalamus?","choices":{"A":"The sulcus limitans","B":"The corpus callosum sulcus","C":"The hypothalamic sulcus","D":"The choroidal fissure groove","E":"The collateral sulcus line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hypothalamic sulcus is a shallow groove that runs along the wall of the third ventricle from the cerebral aqueduct to the interventricular foramen, splitting the alar plate derivative into the superior dorsal thalamus and inferior hypothalamus. The sulcus limitans instead divides the brainstem ventricular floor into medial motor and lateral sensory zones, an unrelated landmark. The corpus callosum sulcus and collateral sulcus are telencephalic cortical folds, not diencephalic ventricular boundaries. The choroidal fissure marks where choroid plexus attaches, not the thalamus-hypothalamus divide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The hypothalamic sulcus is the classic embryologic and adult landmark separating somatic thalamic function above from visceral hypothalamic function below. COMLEX: Recognizing this sulcus on axial or coronal imaging helps localize a lesion as thalamic versus hypothalamic before deficits are even described.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — the embryonic brain vesicle-to-adult-ventricle map, now applied to how the diencephalon's four subdivisions form around the third ventricle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":1,"stem":"The diencephalon is classically described as having how many principal subdivisions, and what are they?","choices":{"A":"Three: dorsal thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus","B":"Four: dorsal thalamus, hypothalamus, ventral thalamus, and epithalamus","C":"Five: dorsal thalamus, hypothalamus, subthalamus, epithalamus, and pineal","D":"Two: the dorsal thalamus and the hypothalamus","E":"Six: thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, tectum, tegmentum, and peduncle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The diencephalon has four principal subdivisions: the dorsal thalamus, the largest component; the hypothalamus, involved in visceromotor and endocrine control; the ventral thalamus or subthalamus, containing the subthalamic nucleus and zona incerta; and the epithalamus, containing the pineal gland and habenular nuclei. Omitting the ventral thalamus undercounts the true four-part scheme. Listing the pineal as a separate fifth subdivision double-counts it, since the pineal is part of the epithalamus. Reducing the scheme to two subdivisions ignores the ventral thalamus and epithalamus entirely. Tectum, tegmentum, and peduncle are midbrain terms, not diencephalic subdivisions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize all four subdivisions together, since exam distractors frequently drop the smaller ventral thalamus and epithalamus. COMLEX: Each subdivision has a distinct clinical signature, so knowing all four sharpens localization on integrated brainstem-forebrain vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — the embryonic brain vesicle-to-adult-ventricle map, now applied to how the diencephalon's four subdivisions form around the third ventricle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":1,"stem":"The rostral boundary of the diencephalon, marking the transition to the telencephalon, is formed by which thin midline membrane?","choices":{"A":"The septum pellucidum","B":"The tela choroidea layer","C":"The interventricular septum","D":"The lamina terminalis","E":"The velum interpositum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lamina terminalis is a thin membrane extending ventrally from the anterior commissure to the optic chiasm and forms the rostral wall of the third ventricle, marking the diencephalon-telencephalon border. The septum pellucidum instead separates the two lateral ventricles, a purely telencephalic structure. The tela choroidea is the vascular pial-ependymal layer that gives rise to choroid plexus, not a ventricular boundary wall. There is no structure formally termed the interventricular septum in standard neuroanatomy. The velum interpositum is a triangular space above the third ventricle roof, not its rostral wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lamina terminalis also houses the organum vasculosum, a circumventricular organ lacking a blood-brain barrier that senses plasma osmolality. COMLEX: Its thinness makes it a classic site for endoscopic third ventriculostomy access in obstructive hydrocephalus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — the embryonic brain vesicle-to-adult-ventricle map, now applied to how the diencephalon's four subdivisions form around the third ventricle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of thalamic nuclei conveys somatosensory information, one relaying input from the body and the other from the face, to the primary somatosensory cortex?","choices":{"A":"The ventral anterior and ventral lateral motor nuclei","B":"The ventral posterolateral and ventral posteromedial nuclei","C":"The medial and lateral geniculate primary sensory nuclei","D":"The anterior and dorsomedial limbic association nuclei","E":"The lateral dorsal and lateral posterior parietal nuclei"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ventral posterolateral nucleus receives medial lemniscus and spinothalamic fibers carrying body sensation, while the ventral posteromedial nucleus receives trigeminothalamic fibers carrying facial sensation, and both project to the postcentral gyrus. The ventral anterior and ventral lateral nuclei instead relay motor-related input from the basal nuclei and cerebellum. The geniculate nuclei relay vision and audition, not somatic sensation. The anterior and dorsomedial nuclei are limbic and association nuclei. The lateral dorsal and lateral posterior nuclei connect with the cingulate gyrus and parietal association cortex rather than primary somatosensory cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VPL handles the body and VPM handles the face, a pairing tested repeatedly alongside somatotopic organization within VPL itself. First Aid mnemonic for VPM: very pretty makeup goes on the face. COMLEX: A thalamic stroke sparing the face but affecting the body points to isolated VPL, not VPM, involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — primary motor and primary sensory cortex, the thalamocortical destination that VL, VPL, and VPM each project onward to","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which two thalamic nuclei receive input predominantly from the basal nuclei and cerebellum and project to the frontal motor cortex to shape voluntary movement?","choices":{"A":"The ventral anterior and ventral lateral nuclei","B":"The ventral posterolateral and ventral posteromedial nuclei","C":"The medial and lateral geniculate nuclei","D":"The pulvinar and dorsomedial nuclei","E":"The anterior and midline nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral anterior nucleus receives pallidal and nigral input while the ventral lateral nucleus receives pallidal and contralateral cerebellar input, and both relay to the precentral gyrus and adjacent frontal cortex to influence motor execution. The ventral posterior nuclei instead carry somatosensory rather than motor information. The geniculate nuclei are dedicated to vision and hearing. The pulvinar and dorsomedial nuclei are association nuclei tied to visual and limbic-frontal circuits, not direct motor relay. The anterior and midline nuclei belong to the limbic system, not the motor system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VA and VL sit at the crossroads of basal ganglia and cerebellar motor loops, a concept tested when explaining how a thalamic lesion can mimic a movement disorder. First Aid mnemonic: Venus astronauts vow to love moving, for ventral anterior and ventral lateral motor relay. COMLEX: Deep brain stimulation targets often lie functionally adjacent to these thalamic motor relay nuclei.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — primary motor and primary sensory cortex, the thalamocortical destination that VL, VPL, and VPM each project onward to","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which thalamic nucleus receives dense limbic input via the mammillothalamic tract and the fornix and serves as a key relay station in the Papez circuit for memory and emotion?","choices":{"A":"The dorsomedial nucleus","B":"The ventral lateral nucleus","C":"The anterior nucleus","D":"The pulvinar nucleus","E":"The reticular nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior nucleus receives mammillary body input via the mammillothalamic tract and hippocampal input via the fornix, then projects to the cingulate gyrus, making it a defining node of the Papez circuit for emotion and memory. The dorsomedial nucleus is also limbic-related but connects mainly with the frontal lobe, temporal lobe, and amygdala rather than the mammillary bodies and fornix directly. The ventral lateral nucleus is a motor relay nucleus. The pulvinar is a visual association nucleus. The reticular nucleus modulates other thalamic nuclei but has no cortical projections of its own.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior nucleus, mammillary bodies, fornix, and cingulate gyrus together form the Papez circuit, a near-guaranteed limbic anatomy question. COMLEX: Wernicke encephalopathy classically damages the mammillary bodies, disrupting this same circuit and producing anterograde amnesia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — primary motor and primary sensory cortex, the thalamocortical destination that VL, VPL, and VPM each project onward to","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which single sensory modality is the recognized exception to the rule that ascending sensory pathways must relay through the thalamus before reaching the cerebral cortex?","choices":{"A":"Vision","B":"Audition","C":"Proprioception","D":"Nociception","E":"Olfaction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olfactory fibers project directly from the olfactory bulb to the primary olfactory cortex without an obligatory thalamic relay first, making olfaction the sole exception among the major sensory modalities. Vision relays through the lateral geniculate body, audition through the medial geniculate body, proprioception through the ventral posterolateral nucleus, and nociception through the ventral posterolateral nucleus and intralaminar nuclei, so all four require a thalamic synapse before cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This olfaction exception is a favorite single best answer trap, especially paired with anosmia-sparing questions after thalamic strokes. COMLEX: Uncinate seizures arising near primary olfactory cortex can produce hallucinated smells without any thalamic-level involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 7 — somatic, visceral, general, and special functional sensory-motor categories, now unified by the single thalamic relay rule that governs nearly all of them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Because the thalamus is the obligatory synaptic gateway for nearly every ascending sensory pathway, the dorsal thalamus as a whole is functionally best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"The processing gateway linking ascending pathways to the cerebral cortex","B":"A passive relay station that forwards signals unchanged to cortex","C":"A purely motor structure uninvolved in any sensory transmission process","D":"An exclusively limbic structure unrelated to primary sensory processing","E":"A structure entirely bypassed by all cortical afferent sensory fibers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The dorsal thalamus actively processes incoming sensory, motor, and limbic information through reciprocal thalamocortical and corticothalamic connections before relaying it onward, earning its description as the functional gateway to the cerebral cortex. Calling it a passive relay ignores the substantial neural processing that occurs at each thalamic synapse. Calling it purely motor ignores its dominant sensory relay role. Calling it purely limbic ignores VPL, VPM, LGB, and MGB, its primary sensory relay nuclei. Describing it as bypassed by cortical afferents directly contradicts its central relay position for nearly all ascending input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Understanding the thalamus as an active processor rather than a passive wire explains why thalamic lesions can produce both sensory loss and later hyperpathia. COMLEX: The gating concept also explains how attention and arousal can modulate perceived sensory intensity at the thalamic level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 7 — somatic, visceral, general, and special functional sensory-motor categories, now unified by the single thalamic relay rule that governs nearly all of them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"A thalamic nucleus that receives input predominantly from a single modality-specific source, such as one ascending sensory pathway, and sends that processed information to a localized cortical target is best classified as which type of nucleus?","choices":{"A":"An association nucleus","B":"A specific relay nucleus","C":"A nonspecific nucleus","D":"A commissural nucleus","E":"A reticular formation nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Relay nuclei such as VPL, VPM, LGB, and MGB receive predominantly single-source, modality-specific input and forward it to a localized region of sensory, motor, or limbic cortex, which is why nearly every sensory pathway depends on one to reach the cortex. Association nuclei instead receive convergent input from multiple structures and project diffusely to association cortex rather than primary cortex. Nonspecific nuclei produce widespread, delayed, bilateral cortical activation rather than sharply localized relay. There is no thalamic category termed a commissural nucleus. Reticular formation nuclei are brainstem structures distinct from the thalamic reticular nucleus and from relay nuclei entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing relay from association from nonspecific nuclei clarifies why VPL lesions cause focal deficits while intralaminar lesions cause diffuse arousal changes. COMLEX: This classification also underlies why some thalamic strokes cause crisp sensory syndromes and others cause vague, poorly localized symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 7 — somatic, visceral, general, and special functional sensory-motor categories, now unified by the single thalamic relay rule that governs nearly all of them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hypothalamic nucleus receives direct retinohypothalamic input from retinal ganglion cells and functions as the master pacemaker of circadian rhythm?","choices":{"A":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus","B":"The paraventricular nucleus","C":"The ventromedial nucleus","D":"The preoptic hypothalamic nucleus","E":"The arcuate hypothalamic nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus lies just superior to the optic chiasm and receives direct light-dark information from retinal ganglion cells via the retinohypothalamic tract, entraining the endogenous circadian clock to the environmental light cycle. The paraventricular nucleus instead governs oxytocin, vasopressin, and autonomic outflow. The ventromedial nucleus is the satiety center for feeding behavior. The preoptic nucleus governs thermoregulation and some hormone release. The arcuate nucleus produces releasing and inhibiting factors for the anterior pituitary, unrelated to light-driven rhythm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Suprachiasmatic nucleus equals circadian pacemaker is a near-guaranteed single fact recall question. First Aid mnemonic: the SCN is a sun-censing nucleus. COMLEX: Melatonin secretion by the pineal gland is downstream of suprachiasmatic nucleus output relayed through sympathetic pathways, tying two diencephalic structures together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — ventricular system components and their neuroanatomic relationships, now extended to the hypothalamic nuclei that line the third ventricle's floor and walls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of hypothalamic nuclei synthesizes antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin for transport along the supraopticohypophysial tract to the posterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"The arcuate and periventricular hypothalamic nuclei","B":"The ventromedial and lateral hypothalamic nuclei","C":"The preoptic and anterior hypothalamic nuclei","D":"The supraoptic and paraventricular hypothalamic nuclei","E":"The dorsomedial and posterior hypothalamic nuclei"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei synthesize antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin, which travel via axonal transport down the supraopticohypophysial tract for release from the posterior pituitary, making them the principal water-balance and reflex-related hormone sources. The arcuate and periventricular nuclei instead produce hypophysiotropic releasing and inhibiting factors delivered to the anterior pituitary through the portal system. The ventromedial and lateral nuclei govern satiety and hunger. The preoptic and anterior nuclei govern temperature and cardiovascular regulation. The dorsomedial and posterior nuclei relate to defensive behavior and blood pressure elevation, not posterior pituitary hormone synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei producing ADH and oxytocin for posterior pituitary release is a cornerstone endocrine fact tested repeatedly. First Aid mnemonic: SAD POX, supraoptic makes ADH and paraventricular makes oxytocin. COMLEX: Damage to this axis from trauma or tumor produces central diabetes insipidus with polyuria and hypernatremia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — ventricular system components and their neuroanatomic relationships, now extended to the hypothalamic nuclei that line the third ventricle's floor and walls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":1,"stem":"Bilateral destructive lesions of which hypothalamic nucleus classically produce hyperphagia and resultant obesity because it functions as the satiety center?","choices":{"A":"The lateral hypothalamic area","B":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"The supraoptic nucleus","D":"The paraventricular nucleus","E":"The ventromedial nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ventromedial nucleus, located in the tuberal region, functions as the satiety center, so bilateral lesions here remove the signal to stop eating, producing hyperphagia and obesity. The lateral hypothalamic area instead acts as the feeding or hunger center, and its destruction reduces food intake rather than increasing it. The suprachiasmatic nucleus governs circadian timing, not appetite. The supraoptic nucleus produces posterior pituitary hormones. The paraventricular nucleus contributes to autonomic and endocrine output but is not the classic satiety nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ventromedial equals satiety equals lesion causes hyperphagia, contrasted directly with lateral hypothalamic equals hunger equals lesion causes anorexia, is a paired fact frequently tested together. First Aid mnemonic: ventromedial injury makes you very massive, while lateral injury makes you lean. COMLEX: Craniopharyngioma extension into the ventromedial region is a classic pediatric cause of hypothalamic obesity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — ventricular system components and their neuroanatomic relationships, now extended to the hypothalamic nuclei that line the third ventricle's floor and walls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":2,"stem":"A radiology resident reviewing an axial T2 MRI at the level of the interventricular foramen notes a band of tissue crossing the midline space that separates the two thalami in most patients. The attending explains this bridge is present in about 80% of individuals and forms when the two dorsal thalami partially fuse. Which structure is being described?","choices":{"A":"The habenular commissure connecting both epithalamic regions","B":"The anterior commissure connecting olfactory and temporal structures","C":"The interthalamic adhesion, also called the massa intermedia","D":"The posterior commissure marking the diencephalon-midbrain junction","E":"The splenium of the corpus callosum connecting occipital lobes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The interthalamic adhesion, or massa intermedia, forms when the two growing dorsal thalami partially fuse across the third ventricle midline in roughly 80% of individuals, and it is a normal variant visible on midline imaging rather than a fiber tract. The habenular commissure instead connects the two habenular nuclei near the epithalamus. The anterior commissure connects olfactory and temporal lobe structures. The posterior commissure marks the diencephalon-midbrain junction and mediates pupillary light reflex consensual response. The corpus callosum splenium is the posterior telencephalic commissure connecting occipital lobes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Absence of the massa intermedia is a normal variant seen in roughly one-fifth of patients and should not be mistaken for a ventricular abnormality. COMLEX: Its presence or absence is frequently noted incidentally on routine MRI reports without clinical significance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Third ventricle formation from the diencephalic vesicle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with a pineal region tumor undergoes MRI, which shows the mass abutting a posterior evagination of the third ventricle situated just anterior to the pineal gland itself. Which third ventricular recess is being compressed?","choices":{"A":"The infundibular recess","B":"The pineal recess","C":"The supraoptic recess","D":"The suprapineal recess","E":"The lateral recess"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pineal recess is the small posterior evagination of the third ventricle that projects directly into the stalk of the pineal gland, so a pineal region mass most directly compresses this recess along with the adjacent suprapineal recess. The infundibular recess instead projects inferiorly into the pituitary stalk, far from the pineal region. The supraoptic recess sits anteriorly just above the optic chiasm. The suprapineal recess lies just superior to the pineal recess and can also be compressed, but the question specifically describes the recess immediately anterior to the pineal gland stalk itself, which is the pineal recess. There is no third ventricular recess formally termed the lateral recess; that term instead describes a fourth ventricle landmark.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pineal region masses can obstruct the cerebral aqueduct just below these posterior recesses, producing noncommunicating hydrocephalus alongside Parinaud syndrome. COMLEX: Recognizing which third ventricular recess is involved helps predict whether a mass will compress the aqueduct or spare it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Third ventricle formation from the diencephalic vesicle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":2,"stem":"A neurosurgery resident is asked to define the caudal boundary that separates the diencephalon from the midbrain on a midsagittal specimen. Which landmark correctly marks this transition?","choices":{"A":"A line from the anterior commissure to the optic chiasm","B":"A line from the interventricular foramen to the fornix column","C":"A line from the pineal recess to the habenular commissure","D":"A line from the posterior commissure to the mammillary body's edge","E":"A line from the optic chiasm to the infundibulum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The diencephalon-midbrain junction runs along a line extending from the posterior commissure to the caudal edge of the mammillary body on the medial hemisphere surface, with the caudal edge of the optic tract marking the same boundary externally. A line from the anterior commissure to the optic chiasm instead describes the lamina terminalis, the rostral boundary. A line from the interventricular foramen to the fornix does not define either boundary. A line from the pineal recess to the habenular commissure describes epithalamic landmarks, not the caudal diencephalic border. A line from the optic chiasm to the infundibulum stays entirely within the hypothalamus and does not reach the midbrain junction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Confusing the rostral lamina terminalis boundary with the caudal posterior commissure-mammillary body boundary is a common exam trap. COMLEX: This caudal landmark also matters surgically, since lesions just posterior to it behave more like midbrain than thalamic pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Third ventricle formation from the diencephalic vesicle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman develops a right thalamogeniculate artery infarct. She has near-complete loss of position sense, vibration, pain, and temperature on the contralateral body but intact vision and hearing. Which large thalamic nucleus nearby functions primarily as a visual association hub rather than a primary sensory relay?","choices":{"A":"The pulvinar association nucleus","B":"The ventral posterolateral nucleus","C":"The centromedian intralaminar nucleus","D":"The medial geniculate nucleus","E":"The lateral geniculate nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pulvinar is the largest thalamic nucleus, functioning as an association nucleus that connects extensively to parietal, temporal, and occipital association cortices for visual processing and eye movement rather than serving as a primary sensory relay. The ventral posterolateral nucleus was in fact the structure damaged, producing her described deficits. The centromedian intralaminar nucleus is a nonspecific arousal-related nucleus, not primarily a visual association hub. The medial geniculate nucleus relays audition, which was intact but is a primary relay rather than an association hub. The lateral geniculate nucleus relays vision directly to primary visual cortex, again a primary relay rather than the association function described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pulvinar's designation as an association nucleus, distinct from the primary relay nuclei like LGB and MGB, is a subtle but testable distinction. COMLEX: Pulvinar involvement in some seizure syndromes and in visual neglect makes it clinically relevant beyond pure anatomy recall.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Primary sensory cortex as the thalamocortical relay target","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient presents after a small posterior thalamic infarct with a contralateral homonymous visual field defect but entirely normal hearing and normal body sensation. Which thalamic nucleus was most likely infarcted?","choices":{"A":"The medial geniculate nucleus","B":"The lateral geniculate nucleus","C":"The ventral posterolateral nucleus","D":"The ventral posteromedial nucleus","E":"The anterior nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lateral geniculate nucleus receives visual input from the retina via the optic tract and projects to the primary visual cortex, so an isolated lesion here produces a contralateral homonymous visual field defect while sparing hearing and somatic sensation. The medial geniculate nucleus instead relays audition, which would cause a hearing deficit rather than a visual one. The ventral posterolateral and ventral posteromedial nuclei relay body and facial sensation respectively, neither of which is affected in this patient. The anterior nucleus is a limbic relay unrelated to any primary sensory modality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral geniculate lesions produce a contralateral homonymous hemianopia, the same field cut pattern as an optic tract lesion, a distinction tested by checking whether the pupillary light reflex is spared. First Aid mnemonic: lateral equals light, for the lateral geniculate nucleus's visual role. COMLEX: The lateral geniculate is supplied by both the anterior choroidal and thalamogeniculate arteries, giving it some collateral protection compared with more vascularly isolated nuclei.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Primary sensory cortex as the thalamocortical relay target","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient recovering from a severe traumatic brain injury remains persistently lethargic and difficult to arouse despite an otherwise structurally intact cortex. Imaging reveals bilateral small lesions within the discontinuous nuclear groups embedded in the internal medullary lamina that project diffusely to the cortex and striatum. Which thalamic nuclei are most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"The ventral posterolateral and posteromedial nuclei","B":"The lateral and medial geniculate nuclei","C":"The anterior and dorsomedial nuclei","D":"The lateral dorsal and lateral posterior nuclei","E":"The intralaminar and centromedian nuclei"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The intralaminar nuclei, embedded within the internal medullary lamina and including the prominent centromedian nucleus, project diffusely to the cerebral cortex and neostriatum and are classified as nonspecific nuclei that help maintain cortical arousal, so bilateral damage here produces persistent lethargy despite an intact cortex. The ventral posterior nuclei carry somatosensory information and would cause sensory loss, not impaired arousal. The geniculate nuclei carry vision and hearing, unrelated to arousal state. The anterior and dorsomedial nuclei are limbic association nuclei, and damage there produces memory and behavioral change rather than global lethargy. The lateral dorsal and lateral posterior nuclei connect to the cingulate and parietal cortex and do not mediate diffuse cortical arousal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intralaminar and nonspecific thalamic nuclei are part of the pathway maintaining cortical arousal, so bilateral damage produces coma or severe lethargy out of proportion to focal cortical injury. COMLEX: This arousal circuit overlaps conceptually with the ascending reticular activating system tested in altered mental status vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Primary sensory cortex as the thalamocortical relay target","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man suffers a small infarct isolated to the right dorsal thalamus. He develops profound numbness of the entire left body, with normal strength, normal vision, and normal hearing, and he reports no change in his sense of smell. Which explanation best accounts for the pattern of preserved and lost sensory function?","choices":{"A":"All sensory modalities require thalamic relay to reach cortex, except olfaction","B":"The thalamus relays motor but not sensory information to the cortex","C":"Olfaction, like somatic sensation, requires an obligatory thalamic relay","D":"The infarct spared all thalamic nuclei responsible for sensory relay","E":"Somatic sensory loss after thalamic infarct is typically transient and reversible"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because nearly every ascending sensory pathway, including touch, vision, and hearing, must synapse in the thalamus before reaching cortex, a thalamic infarct can abolish somatic sensation while sparing olfaction, which bypasses the thalamus and projects directly to primary olfactory cortex. Saying the thalamus relays only motor information ignores its dominant sensory relay role. Claiming olfaction requires thalamic relay contradicts the well-established exception. Claiming the infarct spared all sensory relay nuclei contradicts his profound hemibody numbness. Describing thalamic sensory loss as typically transient is inaccurate; such deficits are often persistent and can evolve into chronic thalamic pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This exact clinical pattern, sensory loss with preserved smell after a thalamic lesion, is a classic single best answer question testing the olfactory exception. COMLEX: Recognizing isolated thalamic sensory syndromes helps distinguish stroke localization from peripheral neuropathy in an elderly patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — Somatic, visceral, general, and special sensory categories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student asks why a patient with a large right frontal lobe tumor near the olfactory bulb develops anosmia without any accompanying loss of touch, vision, or hearing on either side. Which feature distinguishes the olfactory pathway injured here from the pathways for touch, vision, and hearing?","choices":{"A":"Olfactory fibers share the thalamic relay nucleus used by vision and hearing","B":"Olfactory pathways reach their primary cortex directly, without a thalamic relay","C":"The frontal lobe holds the thalamic relay nuclei for every sensory modality","D":"Anosmia only occurs after bilateral thalamic, not olfactory bulb, injury","E":"Touch and vision pathways also bypass the thalamus near the frontal lobe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mass causes anosmia because it compresses the olfactory bulb and tract on the floor of the anterior cranial fossa. Olfactory receptor neurons project via the olfactory bulb and tract directly to the primary olfactory, or piriform, cortex without an obligatory thalamic synapse first, so a lesion along this route selectively impairs smell while leaving vision, hearing, and touch, all of which do require thalamic relay through different dedicated nuclei located far from the frontal olfactory bulb, completely unaffected. Claiming olfactory fibers share a thalamic nucleus with vision and hearing is false, since each of those modalities uses its own dedicated relay nucleus and olfaction uses none. The frontal lobe does not contain the thalamic relay nuclei for other modalities; those reside in the diencephalon. Anosmia classically follows olfactory bulb or tract injury, not exclusively bilateral thalamic injury. Touch and vision pathways do not bypass the thalamus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anosmia from a frontal lobe mass, such as an olfactory groove meningioma, is a classic vignette that hinges on knowing olfaction skips the thalamus. COMLEX: Foster Kennedy syndrome pairs this same anosmia with ipsilateral optic atrophy and contralateral papilledema from a frontal mass.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — Somatic, visceral, general, and special sensory categories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher records evoked cortical potentials after focal electrical stimulation of a thalamic relay nucleus and separately after stimulation of a nonspecific intralaminar nucleus. Relay nucleus stimulation produces a rapid, sharply localized response in one hemisphere, while intralaminar stimulation produces a slower, widespread, bilateral response. Which conclusion about thalamic processing does this experiment best support?","choices":{"A":"Only nonspecific nuclei are capable of any cortical activation","B":"Relay and nonspecific nuclei produce functionally identical cortical responses","C":"Relay nuclei project focally, while nonspecific nuclei project diffusely","D":"Relay nuclei are incapable of producing any measurable cortical response","E":"Nonspecific nuclei alone participate in modality-specific sensory relay"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rapid, sharply localized response shows that a relay nucleus projects to a discrete cortical target, while the slower, widespread, bilateral response shows that intralaminar nuclei project diffusely to modulate arousal. Saying only nonspecific nuclei activate cortex contradicts the relay nucleus response described. Saying the two nucleus types produce identical responses contradicts the described contrast in timing and distribution. Saying relay nuclei cannot produce a cortical response contradicts the described rapid localized activation. Saying nonspecific nuclei alone participate in modality-specific relay reverses the correct assignment of function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The specific-versus-nonspecific thalamic nucleus distinction, tested by stimulation response pattern, occasionally appears in physiology-heavy vignette stems. COMLEX: This same processing concept underlies why thalamic gating can modulate how intensely a peripheral stimulus is consciously perceived.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — Somatic, visceral, general, and special sensory categories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman develops severe polyuria and polydipsia after resection of a large craniopharyngioma that damaged the floor of the third ventricle. Serum sodium is elevated, and urine remains dilute despite significant dehydration. Damage to which pair of hypothalamic nuclei best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The supraoptic and paraventricular hypothalamic nuclei","B":"The ventromedial and lateral hypothalamic nuclei","C":"The suprachiasmatic and preoptic hypothalamic nuclei","D":"The arcuate and dorsomedial hypothalamic nuclei","E":"The anterior and posterior hypothalamic nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei synthesize antidiuretic hormone for release from the posterior pituitary, and their destruction produces central diabetes insipidus, presenting with dilute urine despite hypernatremia and dehydration exactly as described. The ventromedial and lateral nuclei govern satiety and hunger, unrelated to water balance. The suprachiasmatic and preoptic nuclei govern circadian rhythm and temperature, not antidiuretic hormone synthesis. The arcuate and dorsomedial nuclei relate to anterior pituitary releasing factors and behavioral regulation, not posterior pituitary hormone production. The anterior and posterior nuclei govern cardiovascular and sympathetic-related functions rather than water balance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Craniopharyngioma is the classic pediatric and young adult tumor causing central diabetes insipidus by damaging the supraoptic-paraventricular-posterior pituitary axis. First Aid mnemonic: SAD POX, supraoptic makes ADH and paraventricular makes oxytocin. COMLEX: Distinguishing central diabetes insipidus, which responds to desmopressin, from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, which does not, is a frequently tested clinical distinction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Ventricular system components bordering the third ventricle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old runner collapses during a marathon on an unusually hot day. On arrival he is confused, his skin is hot and dry, and his core temperature is 41.5 degrees Celsius. His hypothalamic thermoregulatory center is failing to trigger adequate heat-dissipation responses such as sweating and vasodilation. Which hypothalamic nucleus normally coordinates this heat-loss response?","choices":{"A":"The ventromedial nucleus","B":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"The arcuate nucleus","D":"The mammillary nucleus","E":"The preoptic nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The preoptic nucleus, located in the chiasmatic region of the medial hypothalamic zone, contains warm-sensitive neurons that trigger heat-dissipation responses such as cutaneous vasodilation and sweating, and its failure or overwhelm, as in heat stroke, allows core temperature to rise dangerously. The ventromedial nucleus governs satiety, not temperature. The suprachiasmatic nucleus governs circadian timing, not acute thermoregulation. The arcuate nucleus governs anterior pituitary releasing factors. The mammillary nuclei are involved in feeding reflexes and memory circuitry, not temperature control.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The preoptic nucleus as the body's thermostat, with warm-sensitive neurons driving heat loss, is a classic physiology fact tied directly to heat stroke vignettes. COMLEX: Exertional heat stroke with hot dry skin and altered mental status is a heat-related emergency distinguished from heat exhaustion by the presence of central nervous system dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Ventricular system components bordering the third ventricle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with chronic, poorly controlled stress develops persistently elevated cortisol levels. Investigation reveals excessive corticotropin-releasing hormone output that drives the anterior pituitary to oversecrete adrenocorticotropic hormone. Which hypothalamic nucleus is the primary source of the corticotropin-releasing hormone driving this axis?","choices":{"A":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus, together with the preoptic area","B":"The paraventricular nucleus, through its parvocellular neurons","C":"The ventromedial nucleus, together with the lateral region","D":"The arcuate nucleus, together with the periventricular region","E":"The posterior nucleus, together with the mammillary region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parvocellular neurons of the paraventricular nucleus synthesize corticotropin-releasing hormone and release it into the hypophysial portal system at the median eminence, driving anterior pituitary ACTH secretion and, in turn, adrenal cortisol output. The arcuate nucleus and adjacent periventricular region also deliver releasing factors to the portal vessels, but the arcuate products are GnRH, GHRH and dopamine rather than CRH. The suprachiasmatic and preoptic nuclei govern circadian timing and temperature, the ventromedial and lateral nuclei govern feeding, and the posterior and mammillary nuclei govern autonomic and memory-related functions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypophysiotropic hormones delivered via the portal system are a cornerstone endocrine fact, with CRH from the paraventricular nucleus and GnRH and dopamine from the arcuate nucleus. First Aid frames the hypothalamus's whole functional repertoire with the mnemonic TAN HATS, Thirst, Adenohypophysis and neurohypophysis control, Hunger, Autonomic regulation, Temperature, and Sexual urges. COMLEX: Chronic hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis activation from ongoing stress is a recurring theme in osteopathic discussions of the mind-body stress response.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Ventricular system components bordering the third ventricle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy develops upward gaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus, and light-near dissociation of the pupils. MRI shows a mass in the posterior diencephalon compressing the adjacent midbrain tectum. This mass most likely arises from which diencephalic subdivision?","choices":{"A":"Epithalamus","B":"Dorsal thalamus","C":"Hypothalamus","D":"Subthalamus","E":"Ventral thalamus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The epithalamus, comprising the pineal gland and habenular nuclei, sits at the posterior roof of the third ventricle immediately adjacent to the midbrain tectum, so pineal region masses arising here classically compress the superior colliculus and pretectal area to produce this dorsal midbrain picture. The dorsal thalamus, hypothalamus, subthalamus, and ventral thalamus all sit elsewhere along the third ventricle and do not directly abut the tectum in this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a pineal region mass causing upward gaze palsy and light-near dissociation is a classic dorsal midbrain compression vignette; anchor the mass itself to the epithalamus even though the physical exam findings localize to the adjacent tectum. COMLEX: pineal region tumors in children raise germ cell tumor and pineoblastoma on the differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the four diencephalic subdivisions and their third-ventricle relationships, tested here as a board-style lesion-localization vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":3,"stem":"A structure forms the lateral walls of the third ventricle immediately superior to the hypothalamic sulcus and contains nuclei that project diffusely to the cerebral cortex through thalamocortical radiations. Which diencephalic subdivision is being described?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamus","B":"Epithalamus","C":"Dorsal thalamus","D":"Ventral thalamus","E":"Midbrain tegmentum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The dorsal thalamus makes up the bulk of the diencephalon, forms the lateral walls of the third ventricle above the hypothalamic sulcus, and is the source of the widespread thalamocortical radiations that relay sensory, motor, limbic, and association information to the cortex. The hypothalamus lies below the hypothalamic sulcus and forms the floor and inferior walls, the epithalamus caps the posterior roof, the ventral thalamus, also called the subthalamus, lies inferolateral to the dorsal thalamus, and the midbrain tegmentum is a brainstem structure entirely outside the diencephalon despite its similar name.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hypothalamic sulcus is the key landmark separating the dorsal thalamus above from the hypothalamus below on a coronal section. COMLEX: recognizing this boundary helps localize a lesion described purely by its position relative to the third ventricle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the four diencephalic subdivisions and their third-ventricle relationships, tested here as a board-style lesion-localization vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the boundaries and major subdivisions of the diencephalon and relate them to the third ventricle.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a craniopharyngioma develops bitemporal hemianopia, central diabetes insipidus, and a disrupted sleep-wake cycle. Imaging shows the mass centered on the floor of the third ventricle. Which diencephalic subdivision is primarily compressed?","choices":{"A":"Epithalamus","B":"Hypothalamus","C":"Dorsal thalamus","D":"Subthalamus","E":"Reticular nucleus of the thalamus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hypothalamus forms the floor and inferior walls of the third ventricle and contains the optic chiasm at its anterior margin, the infundibulum leading to the posterior pituitary, and the suprachiasmatic nucleus governing circadian rhythm, so a lesion here produces exactly this triad of visual field loss, diabetes insipidus, and disrupted sleep-wake cycling. The epithalamus, dorsal thalamus, subthalamus, and thalamic reticular nucleus are all diencephalic structures but none sits at the third ventricle floor or governs these specific functions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: craniopharyngioma is the classic pediatric and bimodal-onset suprasellar mass compressing the optic chiasm and hypothalamic floor, producing this exact triad. COMLEX: central diabetes insipidus from hypothalamic or infundibular injury is distinguished from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by its response to desmopressin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the four diencephalic subdivisions and their third-ventricle relationships, tested here as a board-style lesion-localization vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A stroke involving the posterolateral thalamus produces contralateral loss of pain, temperature, and proprioceptive sensation over the trunk and limbs, with the face fully spared. Which thalamic nucleus was most likely infarcted?","choices":{"A":"Ventral posteromedial nucleus","B":"Ventral anterior nucleus of the thalamus","C":"Pulvinar nucleus of the thalamus","D":"Ventral lateral nucleus of the thalamus","E":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The ventral posterolateral nucleus relays somatosensory input from the body, carried by both the dorsal column-medial lemniscus and anterolateral pathways, so its infarction produces exactly this contralateral trunk and limb sensory loss while sparing the face. The ventral posteromedial nucleus instead relays facial and gustatory input and would spare the body, the ventral anterior and ventral lateral nuclei are motor relay nuclei from the basal nuclei and cerebellum rather than sensory relays, and the pulvinar is a posterior association nucleus without a primary somatosensory relay role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VPL takes the body, VPM takes the face, a simple pairing that resolves almost any thalamic sensory-relay question. COMLEX: thalamic strokes affecting VPL can also produce delayed central post-stroke pain (thalamic pain syndrome) weeks to months later.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — major thalamic nuclei and their functions, tested here as a board-style vignette requiring derivation of the nucleus from a deficit pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a large posterior thalamic infarct has difficulty naming objects and integrating complex visual scenes, without any primary visual field cut on formal perimetry. Which thalamic nucleus is most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"Lateral geniculate body","B":"Medial geniculate body","C":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus","D":"Pulvinar nucleus","E":"Anterior nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pulvinar is the largest and most posterior thalamic nucleus, with extensive reciprocal connections to visual, parietal, and temporal association cortex rather than a primary sensory relay role, so its damage impairs higher-order visual integration and language association without producing a field cut. The lateral geniculate body is the primary visual relay and would cause a field cut if damaged, the medial geniculate body relays hearing, the ventral posterolateral nucleus relays body somatosensation, and the anterior nucleus is a limbic relay in the Papez circuit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish the pulvinar, an association nucleus with no primary sensory field, from the lateral and medial geniculate bodies, which are true primary sensory relays with corresponding field defects when damaged. COMLEX: pulvinar strokes are an underrecognized cause of higher-order visual-perceptual complaints that can be mistaken for a primary ophthalmologic problem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — major thalamic nuclei and their functions, tested here as a board-style vignette requiring derivation of the nucleus from a deficit pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify major thalamic nuclei and connect them to sensory, motor, limbic, and association functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with chronic alcohol use disorder and untreated thiamine deficiency develops severe anterograde amnesia and confabulation. Classic pathologic studies of this disorder show damage concentrated in the mammillary bodies together with which thalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Anterior nucleus","B":"Pulvinar","C":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus","D":"Ventral anterior nucleus","E":"Dorsomedial nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome classically damages the mammillary bodies together with the dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus, both key limbic relay stations, producing the profound anterograde amnesia and confabulation characteristic of Korsakoff psychosis. The anterior nucleus is also a Papez circuit relay but is not the specific nucleus classically paired with the mammillary bodies in this disorder, and the ventral posterolateral, ventral anterior, and pulvinar nuclei serve somatosensory, motor, and visual association roles unrelated to this memory circuit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mammillary bodies plus dorsomedial thalamic nucleus is the classic Wernicke-Korsakoff pairing tested on boards; thiamine repletion before glucose administration is the key management point in Wernicke encephalopathy. COMLEX: screen for thiamine deficiency risk in any alcohol use disorder patient before giving IV dextrose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — major thalamic nuclei and their functions, tested here as a board-style vignette requiring derivation of the nucleus from a deficit pattern","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with an olfactory groove meningioma has isolated anosmia. Unlike every other primary sensory modality, this pathway reaches its primary cortex without first synapsing in which structure?","choices":{"A":"Brainstem reticular formation","B":"Hypothalamic relay nucleus","C":"Basal forebrain nucleus","D":"Amygdala nucleus","E":"Thalamic relay nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olfaction is the one sensory modality that bypasses an obligate thalamic relay, projecting directly from the olfactory bulb to primary olfactory cortex, whereas vision, hearing, touch, and even taste all synapse in a dedicated thalamic relay nucleus before reaching their primary cortical areas. The brainstem reticular formation, hypothalamus, basal forebrain, and amygdala all receive some olfactory input but none of them is the structure that every other sensory modality relays through on its way to primary cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 'olfaction skips the thalamus' is a frequently tested exception worth memorizing as a standalone fact. COMLEX: this explains why strong smells can trigger such immediate, unfiltered emotional and memory responses through direct limbic connections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — the near-universal thalamic relay rule and its lone exception, tested here as a board-style application vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A small thalamic infarct produces contralateral loss of facial sensation and taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, with normal sensation over the trunk and limbs. Which thalamic nucleus was most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Lateral geniculate nucleus","B":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus","C":"Medial geniculate nucleus","D":"Ventral posteromedial nucleus","E":"Ventral lateral nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ventral posteromedial nucleus relays trigeminal general sensation from the face along with gustatory input traveling in the solitary tract, so an isolated lesion here produces facial sensory loss and taste loss while sparing the limbs and trunk. The ventral posterolateral nucleus instead relays body sensation, the medial geniculate body relays hearing, the lateral geniculate body relays vision, and the ventral lateral nucleus is a motor relay nucleus, none of which explains this face-and-taste-selective deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VPM carries both facial touch/pain (trigeminothalamic fibers) and taste (fibers ascending from the solitary nucleus), so one small VPM lesion can affect both. COMLEX: an isolated facial sensory complaint with taste change should raise a central rather than a purely peripheral trigeminal or facial nerve process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — the near-universal thalamic relay rule and its lone exception, tested here as a board-style application vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain why most sensory pathways, except olfaction, relay through the thalamus before reaching the cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man develops isolated hearing loss following a small thalamic infarct, with no visual field cut and no somatosensory deficit on exam. Which thalamic nucleus was most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Lateral geniculate body","B":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus","C":"Ventral posteromedial nucleus","D":"Medial geniculate body","E":"Pulvinar"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The medial geniculate body is the obligate thalamic relay for the auditory pathway on its way to primary auditory cortex, so an isolated lesion here selectively impairs hearing without affecting vision or somatosensation, which relay through separate thalamic nuclei. The lateral geniculate body relays vision and would cause a field cut, the ventral posterolateral and posteromedial nuclei relay body and facial somatosensation respectively, and the pulvinar is a visual and language association nucleus without a primary auditory relay role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair each special sense with its thalamic relay, lateral geniculate for vision and medial geniculate for hearing, to resolve any isolated single-modality thalamic lesion question. COMLEX: central (thalamic or cortical) hearing loss from a discrete lesion is rare and should prompt a search for a vascular cause rather than a peripheral otologic one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — the near-universal thalamic relay rule and its lone exception, tested here as a board-style application vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a small lesion in the caudal, mammillary region of the hypothalamus. In a cold room he fails to shiver or conserve body heat, and his core temperature drifts downward. The nucleus whose neurons normally drive shivering, heat conservation, blood pressure elevation, and pupillary dilation is the ___","choices":{"A":"preoptic nucleus","B":"suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"posterior nucleus","D":"ventromedial nucleus","E":"arcuate nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At caudal levels, the mammillary region of the hypothalamus contains the posterior nucleus and the mammillary nuclei; neurons of the posterior nucleus are involved in elevation of blood pressure, pupillary dilation, and shivering or body heat conservation, so its damage impairs defense against cold. The preoptic nucleus in the chiasmatic region handles heat-loss mechanisms such as sweating and vasodilation. The suprachiasmatic nucleus sets circadian rhythm, the ventromedial nucleus is the satiety center, and the arcuate nucleus delivers releasing and inhibiting factors to the portal vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior (preoptic) hypothalamus cools the body; posterior hypothalamus conserves and generates heat. COMLEX: Posterior hypothalamic lesions can leave a patient unable to maintain temperature in a cold environment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — hypothalamic nuclei and their autonomic, endocrine, feeding, and temperature functions, tested here as a board-style vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":3,"stem":"A tuberal-region hypothalamic nucleus delivers peptides into the portal vessels of the median eminence; some of these peptides increase and others inhibit the secretion of specific anterior pituitary hormones. A small lesion confined to this nucleus would most directly disrupt the hypothalamic control of anterior pituitary secretion. This nucleus is the ___","choices":{"A":"supraoptic nucleus","B":"suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"mammillary nucleus","D":"arcuate nucleus","E":"posterior nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The tuberal region contains the dorsomedial, ventromedial, and arcuate nuclei. Cells of the arcuate nucleus deliver peptides to the portal vessels and, through these channels, to the anterior pituitary; some are releasing factors that increase the secretion of specific pituitary hormones and some are inhibiting factors that suppress it. The supraoptic nucleus sends vasopressin and oxytocin axons directly to the posterior pituitary. The suprachiasmatic nucleus is the circadian pacemaker. The mammillary nuclei relate to memory and feeding reflexes, and the posterior nucleus drives heat conservation and sympathetic responses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior pituitary hormones are made by supraoptic and paraventricular neurons and travel by axons; anterior pituitary control uses releasing and inhibiting factors carried by the hypophysial portal vessels, many from the arcuate nucleus. COMLEX: Stalk compression interrupts these inhibiting factors, so prolactin can rise while other anterior pituitary hormones fall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — hypothalamic nuclei and their endocrine functions, tested here as a board-style vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl25_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe key hypothalamic functions related to autonomic regulation, endocrine control, temperature, feeding, water balance, circadian rhythm, and stress.","batch":3,"stem":"The mammillary region contains a large medial and a small lateral mammillary nucleus. Both receive input from the hippocampus through the fornix, but only one projects through the mammillothalamic tract, which crosses the internal medullary lamina to reach the anterior thalamic nucleus. The nucleus with this projection is the ___","choices":{"A":"lateral mammillary nucleus","B":"posterior hypothalamic nucleus","C":"medial mammillary nucleus","D":"ventromedial nucleus","E":"dorsomedial thalamic nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In humans, the mammillary nuclei consist of a large medial nucleus and a small lateral nucleus. Both receive input through the fornix, but only the medial nucleus projects to the anterior thalamic nucleus through the mammillothalamic tract, which traverses the internal medullary lamina; the mammillary nuclei take part in feeding-related reflexes and memory formation. The lateral nucleus does not give rise to the mammillothalamic tract. The posterior hypothalamic nucleus drives heat conservation and sympathetic responses. The ventromedial nucleus is the satiety center, and the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus is an association nucleus of the dorsal thalamus, not a mammillary nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Papez-type limbic loop: hippocampus to fornix to medial mammillary nucleus to mammillothalamic tract to anterior thalamic nucleus to cingulate cortex. COMLEX: Damage to the mammillary bodies in thiamine deficiency explains the memory loss of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — hypothalamic nuclei and their functions, tested here through the mammillary projection to the thalamus","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":1,"stem":"The subthalamic nucleus is best described anatomically as a lens-shaped cell group located in which position relative to surrounding structures?","choices":{"A":"Directly lateral to the internal capsule and caudal to the red nucleus","B":"Directly posterior to the pulvinar and superior to the geniculate nuclei","C":"Directly anterior to the mammillary bodies and inferior to the fornix","D":"Medial to the internal capsule and rostral to the substantia nigra","E":"Directly superior to the habenular nuclei and medial to the pineal gland"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus sits within the ventral thalamus, medial to the internal capsule and rostral to the substantia nigra, immediately inferior to the lenticular fasciculus. Placing it lateral to the internal capsule and caudal to the red nucleus misplaces both landmarks. The pulvinar and geniculate nuclei are dorsal thalamic structures, unrelated to the subthalamic nucleus's position. The mammillary bodies and fornix are hypothalamic and limbic landmarks, not subthalamic ones. The habenular nuclei and pineal gland belong to the epithalamus, a different diencephalic subdivision entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Precise subthalamic nucleus localization, medial to the internal capsule and rostral to the substantia nigra, matters because vascular lesions in this exact territory produce the classic hemiballismus syndrome. COMLEX: This same location makes the subthalamic nucleus a modern deep brain stimulation target for advanced Parkinson disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the ventral thalamus, or subthalamus, one of the four diencephalic subdivisions bordering the third ventricle, now examined for its internal nucleus and circuitry","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":1,"stem":"In the indirect basal ganglia pathway, the subthalamic nucleus sends excitatory glutamatergic fibers to which structure?","choices":{"A":"The lateral, or external, segment of the globus pallidus","B":"The putamen, part of the dorsal striatum complex","C":"The medial, or internal, segment of the globus pallidus","D":"The substantia nigra, specifically its pars compacta","E":"The ventral anterior nucleus of the thalamus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cortical excitation of the striatum inhibits the lateral globus pallidus, which disinhibits the subthalamic nucleus, allowing it to send excitatory subthalamopallidal fibers to the medial globus pallidus, whose output heavily inhibits the thalamus. The lateral globus pallidus is instead upstream of the subthalamic nucleus in this circuit, not its target. The putamen is the striatal input structure that begins the indirect pathway, not the subthalamic nucleus's output target. The substantia nigra pars compacta modulates the striatum via dopamine but is not the direct recipient of subthalamopallidal fibers. The ventral anterior nucleus is a downstream thalamic relay affected by pallidal output, not a direct subthalamic target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing that the subthalamic nucleus excites the medial globus pallidus, not the lateral segment, is the single fact that explains why subthalamic destruction paradoxically increases movement. COMLEX: This circuit-level understanding also explains why subthalamic deep brain stimulation can mimic a controlled, reversible lesion effect in Parkinson disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the ventral thalamus, or subthalamus, one of the four diencephalic subdivisions bordering the third ventricle, now examined for its internal nucleus and circuitry","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":1,"stem":"Destruction of the subthalamic nucleus by a vascular lesion classically produces which involuntary movement disorder?","choices":{"A":"Hemiballismus, with violent flailing of the contralateral limbs","B":"Resting tremor with cogwheel rigidity of the ipsilateral limbs","C":"Chorea with slow, writhing, athetoid movements occurring bilaterally","D":"Intention tremor with dysmetria affecting the ipsilateral limb","E":"Myoclonus with brief, bilateral, symmetric muscle jerking"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of the subthalamic nucleus removes its excitatory drive to the medial globus pallidus, reducing thalamic inhibition and disinhibiting motor cortex, which produces hemiballismus, violent and forceful flailing movements typically involving the contralateral upper extremity. Resting tremor with cogwheel rigidity instead reflects nigrostriatal dopamine loss in Parkinson disease, a different basal ganglia circuit defect. Chorea with athetoid writhing classically reflects striatal, not subthalamic, pathology such as in Huntington disease. Intention tremor with dysmetria localizes to the cerebellum, not the basal ganglia. Myoclonus arises from diverse cortical, subcortical, or spinal generators rather than isolated subthalamic nucleus destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemiballismus from contralateral subthalamic nucleus damage is a classic single best answer pairing, frequently tested alongside lacunar stroke risk factors like uncontrolled hypertension. First Aid mnemonic: in hemiballismus, half of the body is going ballistic. COMLEX: The violent, involuntary, and embarrassing nature of hemiballismus movements can also be a source of significant psychosocial distress worth addressing in patient care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the ventral thalamus, or subthalamus, one of the four diencephalic subdivisions bordering the third ventricle, now examined for its internal nucleus and circuitry","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":1,"stem":"The thalamogeniculate arteries, branches of the P2 segment of the posterior cerebral artery, primarily supply which portion of the thalamus?","choices":{"A":"The rostral thalamus, including the anterior and dorsomedial nuclei","B":"The caudal thalamus, including the pulvinar and geniculate nuclei","C":"The medial thalamus bordering the third ventricle","D":"The entire internal capsule and adjacent basal nuclei","E":"The hypothalamus and its anteromedial perforator territory"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thalamogeniculate arteries arise from the P2 segment and irrigate the caudal thalamus, including the pulvinar and the medial and lateral geniculate nuclei, and their occlusion produces classic thalamic sensory syndromes. The rostral and medial thalamus, including the anterior and dorsomedial nuclei, is instead supplied by the thalamoperforating arteries from P1, a different named vessel group. The medial thalamus bordering the third ventricle is supplied by the medial posterior choroidal artery, not the thalamogeniculate arteries. The internal capsule and basal nuclei receive their own lenticulostriate supply. The hypothalamus is supplied by anteromedial and posteromedial perforator groups, unrelated to the thalamogeniculate arteries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The thalamogeniculate artery to caudal thalamus is the vessel behind Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome, a repeatedly tested vascular-to-syndrome pairing. COMLEX: Recognizing this territory also aids in predicting which sensory modalities, VPL and VPM carried, are most affected by a given thalamic stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — the ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic, now extended to the smaller perforating branches that irrigate the thalamus and hypothalamus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":1,"stem":"Bilateral occlusion of the thalamoperforating arteries arising from the P1 segment, or occlusion of a single azygos stem supplying both sides, most characteristically produces which clinical picture?","choices":{"A":"Isolated contralateral face and body numbness without weakness","B":"Pure contralateral hemiplegia without any sensory involvement","C":"A rapid resting tremor confined to the contralateral hand","D":"Severe lethargy, somnolence, stupor, or coma from impaired arousal","E":"Violent unilateral flailing of the contralateral extremities"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The thalamoperforating arteries supply the rostral and medial thalamus, including the intralaminar nuclei that sustain cortical arousal, so bilateral occlusion or occlusion of a shared azygos trunk disrupts cortical arousal and produces severe lethargy, somnolence, stupor, or coma. Isolated contralateral sensory loss instead reflects the caudal, thalamogeniculate-supplied thalamus, not the rostral thalamoperforating territory. Pure hemiplegia without sensory involvement points to an internal capsule or corticospinal tract lesion, not this perforator territory. A resting tremor reflects nigrostriatal dopaminergic pathology, unrelated to thalamoperforating occlusion. Violent flailing movements reflect subthalamic nucleus destruction, a separate vascular territory and circuit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An azygos thalamoperforating trunk supplying both sides, sometimes called the artery of Percheron in clinical parlance, is a classic cause of bilateral thalamic infarction presenting with unexplained coma. COMLEX: This presentation is a frequently missed stroke mimic, since standard CT often appears deceptively normal early in the course.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — the ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic, now extended to the smaller perforating branches that irrigate the thalamus and hypothalamus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":1,"stem":"The anterior portion of the hypothalamus receives its blood supply predominantly from central branches of which arterial group?","choices":{"A":"The posteromedial group, from the posterior communicating artery and P1","B":"The lenticulostriate group, from the M1 segment of the middle cerebral artery","C":"The anteromedial group, from the anterior communicating artery and A1","D":"The thalamogeniculate group, from the P2 segment of the posterior cerebral artery","E":"The anterior choroidal group, from the cerebral internal carotid artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior hypothalamus is served by central, anteromedial-group branches arising from the anterior communicating artery and the A1 segment of the anterior cerebral artery, along with contributions from the proximal posterior communicating artery. The posteromedial group instead supplies the caudal hypothalamus and ventral thalamus, not the anterior hypothalamus. The lenticulostriate group supplies the basal nuclei and internal capsule. The thalamogeniculate group supplies the caudal dorsal thalamus. The anterior choroidal artery supplies the optic tract, inferior internal capsule, and medial temporal structures, not the anterior hypothalamus specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anteromedial group from ACom and A1 supplying the anterior hypothalamus, contrasted with the posteromedial group supplying the caudal hypothalamus, is a paired fact worth memorizing together. COMLEX: Anterior communicating artery aneurysm rupture can therefore produce hypothalamic dysfunction alongside its more commonly tested subarachnoid hemorrhage presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — the ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic, now extended to the smaller perforating branches that irrigate the thalamus and hypothalamus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":1,"stem":"A thalamic sensory syndrome is most clinically distinguished from other diencephalic syndromes by which characteristic evolution of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Pure sensory loss that evolves into severe, burning neuropathic pain","B":"Sudden, wild, uncontrollable flinging movements of one entire side","C":"Sudden-onset polyuria and polydipsia with persistently dilute urine","D":"Paralysis of upward gaze together with light-near pupillary dissociation","E":"Progressive weight gain from unchecked, uncontrolled hyperphagia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thalamic sensory syndrome classically begins with contralateral hemianesthesia or dissociated sensory loss from thalamogeniculate territory infarction and later evolves into intense, persistent, burning pain known as thalamic pain or Dejerine-Roussy syndrome, a distinctive time course that helps confirm the diagnosis. Wild flinging movements instead point to subthalamic nucleus destruction and hemiballismus. Polyuria and polydipsia with dilute urine point to hypothalamic diabetes insipidus from supraoptic or paraventricular damage. Upward gaze palsy with light-near dissociation points to Parinaud syndrome from pineal or dorsal midbrain compression. Progressive hyperphagia points to ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The delayed onset of burning central pain after initial sensory loss is the single most testable clue that distinguishes thalamic pain syndrome from a purely peripheral neuropathic process. COMLEX: This pain is often refractory to standard analgesics and may require agents such as anticonvulsants or tricyclic antidepressants targeting neuropathic pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — integrated board-style midbrain application, the same dorsal midbrain compression mechanism now reframed from the adjacent pineal/epithalamic vantage point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":1,"stem":"A pineal or dorsal midbrain compressive lesion is most clinically distinguished from other diencephalic syndromes by which combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral hemianesthesia evolving into burning pain","B":"Violent flailing movements limited to one side of the body","C":"Severe polyuria with persistently dilute urine despite dehydration","D":"Sudden onset of profound coma without a large hemispheric lesion","E":"Upward gaze palsy with light-near pupillary dissociation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A pineal region or dorsal midbrain compressive lesion produces Parinaud syndrome, characterized by paralysis of upward gaze, light-near pupillary dissociation, and convergence-retraction nystagmus, from direct pressure on the superior colliculi and pretectal area adjacent to the pineal gland. Contralateral hemianesthesia evolving into burning pain instead describes thalamic pain syndrome from thalamogeniculate territory infarction. Violent unilateral flailing describes hemiballismus from subthalamic nucleus destruction. Severe polyuria with dilute urine describes hypothalamic diabetes insipidus. Sudden coma without a large hemispheric lesion describes bilateral thalamoperforating artery occlusion affecting the ascending reticular activating system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Parinaud syndrome from a pineal region mass, most classically a pineal germinoma in a young patient, is a high-yield triad tested repeatedly on exams. COMLEX: The same compressive mechanism can also produce noncommunicating hydrocephalus by obstructing the adjacent cerebral aqueduct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — integrated board-style midbrain application, the same dorsal midbrain compression mechanism now reframed from the adjacent pineal/epithalamic vantage point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":1,"stem":"A hypothalamic autonomic disorder producing an ipsilateral Horner syndrome, characterized by ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis, is best explained by disruption of which descending pathway?","choices":{"A":"The mammillothalamic tract carrying limbic memory circuit input","B":"Descending hypothalamospinal sympathetic fibers","C":"The supraopticohypophysial tract carrying antidiuretic hormone","D":"The tuberoinfundibular tract carrying pituitary releasing factors","E":"The medial forebrain bundle carrying reward-related dopamine fibers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Descending hypothalamospinal sympathetic fibers originate in the posterior and lateral hypothalamus and travel through the brainstem to preganglionic sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord, and their disruption produces an ipsilateral Horner syndrome with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis. The mammillothalamic tract instead carries limbic input to the anterior thalamic nucleus and has no role in sympathetic outflow. The supraopticohypophysial tract carries antidiuretic hormone to the posterior pituitary, producing diabetes insipidus when disrupted rather than Horner syndrome. The tuberoinfundibular tract delivers releasing factors to the anterior pituitary via the portal system, unrelated to sympathetic tone. The medial forebrain bundle carries reward and motivational fibers, not the descending sympathetic pathway responsible for pupillary and lid function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothalamic Horner syndrome from disrupted descending sympathetic fibers is a less commonly recalled cause compared with the lateral medullary Wallenberg pattern, making it a useful distractor-busting fact. COMLEX: Recognizing the full three-neuron oculosympathetic pathway helps localize a Horner syndrome to hypothalamus, brainstem, spinal cord, or a peripheral lesion such as a Pancoast tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — integrated board-style midbrain application, the same dorsal midbrain compression mechanism now reframed from the adjacent pineal/epithalamic vantage point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":2,"stem":"A neurosurgeon plans a deep brain stimulation lead aimed at the subthalamic nucleus. Descending from above, the trajectory passes through the thalamic fasciculus and then the zona incerta before reaching the nucleus. Which myelinated fiber bundle lies immediately superior to the subthalamic nucleus, separating it from the zona incerta?","choices":{"A":"The stria medullaris thalami","B":"The mammillothalamic tract","C":"The internal medullary lamina","D":"The medial lemniscus","E":"The lenticular fasciculus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus is a lens-shaped cell group situated immediately inferior to a distinct myelinated bundle, the lenticular fasciculus (Forel's field H2); the zona incerta lies superior to that bundle, and above the zona incerta are the myelinated axons of the thalamic fasciculus (Forel's field H1). Fibers of the lenticular fasciculus pass through the prerubral area, Forel's field H, to enter the thalamic fasciculus. The subthalamic nucleus receives input from motor cortex, projects to the substantia nigra, and is reciprocally connected with the globus pallidus. The stria medullaris thalami belongs to the epithalamus and ends in the habenular nuclei. The mammillothalamic tract runs to the anterior thalamic nucleus, the internal medullary lamina divides the dorsal thalamic nuclei, and the medial lemniscus ascends to the ventral posterolateral nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ventral thalamus layers from above: thalamic fasciculus (H1), zona incerta, lenticular fasciculus (H2), subthalamic nucleus; Forel's field H is the prerubral area. COMLEX: Damage to the subthalamic nucleus causes contralateral hemiballismus, and stimulation of it is used to treat Parkinson disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — The ventral thalamus, or subthalamus, as one of the four diencephalic subdivisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and small-vessel disease develops sudden onset of continuous, forceful, uncontrollable flailing of her right arm that resolves substantially over several weeks with medical management. Vascular imaging most likely reveals a small infarct affecting posteromedial branches of which artery?","choices":{"A":"The anterior choroidal artery arising from the internal carotid artery","B":"The middle cerebral artery lenticulostriate branches","C":"The anterior cerebral artery A1 segment perforators","D":"The posterior cerebral or posterior communicating artery","E":"The superior cerebellar artery circumferential branches"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus is affected by vascular lesions involving posteromedial branches of the posterior cerebral or posterior communicating artery, and infarction here produces hemiballismus, which often improves over weeks as edema resolves and partial functional compensation occurs. The anterior choroidal artery instead supplies the optic tract and inferior internal capsule, producing visual field loss and hemiplegia rather than isolated ballistic movements. The middle cerebral artery lenticulostriate branches supply the basal nuclei and internal capsule, more typically causing weakness. The anterior cerebral artery A1 perforators supply the anterior hypothalamus, not the subthalamic nucleus. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the cerebellum and would produce ataxia rather than hemiballismus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small-vessel ischemic disease from diabetes and hypertension is a common real-world cause of subthalamic nucleus infarction and resultant hemiballismus, not just hemorrhage. COMLEX: Gradual symptomatic improvement over weeks is typical as perilesional edema resolves, an important reassurance point for patients experiencing this dramatic and frightening presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — The ventral thalamus, or subthalamus, as one of the four diencephalic subdivisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man develops sudden violent flinging movements of his left leg. A neurology resident wants to confirm the lesion is subthalamic rather than striatal before ordering imaging. Which additional clinical feature would most strongly support a subthalamic, rather than striatal, localization?","choices":{"A":"Movements are slow, writhing, and largely confined to the fingers and facial muscles","B":"Movements are symmetric and affect both sides of the body equally","C":"Movements are accompanied by a resting tremor and cogwheel rigidity","D":"Movements worsen dramatically with any voluntary attempts at conscious relaxation","E":"Movements are large-amplitude, proximal, and forceful rather than fine and distal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hemiballismus from subthalamic nucleus damage classically produces large-amplitude, proximal, forceful, ballistic flinging movements, in contrast to the slower, smaller-amplitude, distal writhing chorea and athetosis typically associated with striatal pathology such as Huntington disease. Slow writhing movements confined to fingers and face instead describe chorea and athetosis, the striatal pattern. Symmetric bilateral involvement is atypical, since hemiballismus is classically unilateral and contralateral to the lesion. Resting tremor with cogwheel rigidity describes Parkinson disease from nigrostriatal dopamine loss, a different circuit entirely. Worsening specifically with attempted relaxation is not a recognized distinguishing feature of either movement disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The large-amplitude, proximal, ballistic quality of hemiballismus versus the smaller, distal, writhing quality of chorea is a classic bedside distinguishing feature tested on movement disorder vignettes. COMLEX: Recognizing this distinction at the bedside can guide urgency of imaging and helps counsel patients on expected clinical course, since hemiballismus often has a more self-limited trajectory than progressive chorea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — The ventral thalamus, or subthalamus, as one of the four diencephalic subdivisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman develops sudden contralateral hemianesthesia after an ischemic stroke. Over the following weeks, the numb region begins to develop severe, spontaneous, burning pain that is poorly responsive to standard analgesics. Occlusion of which artery most likely explains this evolving presentation?","choices":{"A":"The thalamogeniculate artery, arising from the P2 segment of the PCA","B":"The thalamoperforating artery from the P1 segment","C":"The anterior choroidal artery from the internal carotid artery","D":"The anteromedial perforator group from the anterior communicating artery","E":"The medial posterior choroidal artery to the third ventricle wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thalamogeniculate artery supplies the caudal thalamus, and its occlusion classically produces total or dissociated sensory loss that evolves into thalamic pain, or Dejerine-Roussy syndrome, exactly as described. The thalamoperforating artery instead supplies the rostral and medial thalamus important for arousal, and its occlusion causes lethargy or coma rather than an evolving pain syndrome. The anterior choroidal artery supplies the optic tract and internal capsule, producing visual field loss and hemiplegia rather than the described sensory-to-pain evolution. The anteromedial perforator group supplies the anterior hypothalamus, unrelated to thalamic sensory pathways. The medial posterior choroidal artery supplies a narrow strip of medial thalamus bordering the third ventricle and choroid plexus, not the broad sensory territory described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome following thalamogeniculate artery infarction is one of the most reliably tested vascular-syndrome pairings in diencephalic anatomy. COMLEX: The delayed, treatment-resistant nature of central post-stroke pain makes early patient counseling about this possible complication clinically important.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is found unresponsive at home. On arrival he is comatose with intact brainstem reflexes and no focal hemiparesis. CT imaging is initially unremarkable, but MRI later reveals bilateral paramedian thalamic infarcts. A single anomalous vessel supplying both thalami from one side was most likely occluded. Which artery territory does this anomalous vessel represent?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral thalamogeniculate arteries from separate P2 segments","B":"A shared thalamoperforating trunk arising from a single P1 segment","C":"The anterior choroidal artery supplying both internal capsules","D":"Bilateral lenticulostriate arteries from separate M1 segments","E":"The medial posterior choroidal artery to the third ventricle wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A single azygos thalamoperforating trunk can arise from one P1 segment and supply the rostral and medial thalamus bilaterally, including the medially placed intralaminar nuclei that sustain cortical arousal, so its occlusion produces sudden bilateral thalamic infarction with profound coma or lethargy despite an initially normal-appearing CT and absence of hemiparesis. Bilateral thalamogeniculate arteries from separate P2 segments would require two separate occlusions and typically produce sensory syndromes rather than isolated coma. The anterior choroidal artery supplies the internal capsule and optic tract, producing hemiplegia and visual field loss, not this pattern. Bilateral lenticulostriate occlusions would require two separate strokes and typically cause motor deficits from basal nuclei and internal capsule involvement. The medial posterior choroidal artery supplies only a narrow strip of tissue, not the broad bilateral rostral thalamic territory described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A shared thalamoperforating trunk supplying both sides is the classic explanation for bilateral thalamic infarction presenting as unexplained coma with a deceptively normal early CT scan. COMLEX: This presentation should prompt urgent MRI when a comatose patient lacks focal hemiparesis or a clear large-vessel occlusion pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes clipping of an internal carotid artery aneurysm. Intraoperatively, a small branch arising from the cerebral portion of the internal carotid artery and running caudolaterally along the optic tract is inadvertently sacrificed. Besides the optic tract and the inferior posterior limb of the internal capsule, which medial temporal structures are also at risk from loss of this vessel?","choices":{"A":"The mammillary bodies and the anterior thalamic nucleus","B":"The pulvinar and the medial and lateral geniculate nuclei","C":"The caudal hypothalamus and the subthalamic nucleus","D":"The amygdala and large parts of the hippocampal formation","E":"The rostral thalamus, including the intralaminar nuclei"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The vessel described is the anterior choroidal artery, which arises from the cerebral portion of the internal carotid artery and follows the optic tract. Besides the optic tract and the genu and inferior posterior limb of the internal capsule, it supplies inferior parts of the lenticular nucleus, the choroid plexus of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle, much of the amygdala, the retrolenticular limb, and large parts of the hippocampal formation. The mammillary bodies lie in the caudal hypothalamus, which, together with the anterior thalamus, is fed by perforating branches of the circle of Willis rather than this vessel. The pulvinar and geniculate nuclei belong to the thalamogeniculate territory from the P2 segment. The caudal hypothalamus and subthalamic nucleus are supplied by the posteromedial central branches from the posterior communicating artery and P1 segment. The rostral thalamus, including the intralaminar nuclei, is the thalamoperforating territory from P1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior choroidal artery is an internal carotid branch whose classic syndrome is contralateral hemiplegia, hemianesthesia, and homonymous hemianopia, but its territory also reaches the amygdala, hippocampus, and choroid plexus of the temporal horn. COMLEX: Recognize this territory when an aneurysm clip or temporal lobe operation near the carotid terminus is followed by a contralateral motor deficit plus a field cut.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy is evaluated for delayed puberty. He has small testes, sparse pubic hair, and low gonadotropin levels for his age. MRI shows a mass in the posterior wall of the third ventricle just behind the habenular nuclei, and biopsy shows a tumor composed of large numbers of pinealocytes. Which mechanism best explains his endocrine finding?","choices":{"A":"Destruction of the ventromedial nucleus removing the satiety signal","B":"Compression of the supraoptic nucleus reducing antidiuretic hormone","C":"Compression of the pretectal area interrupting the light reflex","D":"Excess pineal secretory output inhibiting gonadal maturation","E":"Interruption of hypothalamospinal fibers to the lateral horn"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Haines notes that pinealomas, tumors with large numbers of pinealocytes, are accompanied by depressed gonadal function and delayed puberty, whereas lesions that destroy pineal cells are associated with precocious puberty, indicating that pineal secretory products normally inhibit gonadal development. Ventromedial nucleus destruction produces hyperphagia and obesity, not hypogonadism. Supraoptic damage causes central diabetes insipidus with dilute urine and rising sodium. Pretectal compression explains light-near dissociation in Parinaud syndrome but has no endocrine effect. Interrupting hypothalamospinal fibers produces an ipsilateral Horner syndrome rather than delayed puberty. First Aid adds that most pineal region tumors are germ cell tumors that present with obstructive hydrocephalus and Parinaud syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pineal region masses classically cause Parinaud syndrome and aqueductal hydrocephalus; the course text adds that pinealocyte tumors depress gonadal function and delay puberty, while pineal cell loss is associated with precocious puberty. COMLEX: In an adolescent with a pineal region mass, check pubertal staging and gonadotropins along with vertical gaze and pupils.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — Integrated board-style midbrain application, including Parinaud syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a long history of severe alcohol use disorder is brought in confused, with profound anterograde amnesia and a tendency to confabulate detailed but inaccurate answers when questioned about recent events. His eye movements and gait are otherwise unremarkable after acute treatment. Damage to which diencephalic structure most directly explains his memory deficit?","choices":{"A":"The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus","B":"The mammillary nuclei of the posterior hypothalamus","C":"The subthalamic nucleus of the ventral thalamus","D":"The superior colliculi and pretectal area of the dorsal midbrain","E":"The thalamogeniculate perforator territory of the caudal thalamus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic thiamine deficiency in alcohol use disorder classically damages the mammillary nuclei, which receive fornix input from the hippocampus and project to the anterior thalamic nucleus via the mammillothalamic tract as part of the Papez circuit, and their destruction produces the profound anterograde amnesia and confabulation characteristic of Korsakoff psychosis. The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei instead govern water balance through antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin, producing diabetes insipidus when damaged, not amnesia. The subthalamic nucleus governs movement, and its damage produces hemiballismus. The superior colliculi and pretectal area govern eye movement and pupillary reflexes, producing Parinaud syndrome findings absent here. The thalamogeniculate territory governs sensory relay, producing sensory loss and thalamic pain rather than isolated amnesia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mammillary body damage causing Korsakoff amnesia and confabulation after Wernicke encephalopathy is a classic sequence tested repeatedly, especially the thiamine deficiency mechanism. COMLEX: Prompt thiamine repletion before glucose administration in at-risk patients can prevent this often-irreversible memory deficit from developing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — Integrated board-style midbrain application, including Parinaud syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":2,"stem":"An emergency physician is evaluating four patients with distinct new-onset neurologic presentations: one with sudden violent unilateral limb flailing, one with progressive polyuria and dilute urine despite dehydration, one with upward gaze palsy and light-near pupillary dissociation, and one with contralateral numbness evolving into burning pain. Which single localizing principle best organizes how to distinguish these four diencephalic presentations?","choices":{"A":"Each syndrome localizes to a distinct diencephalic structure with its own signature","B":"All four presentations reflect diffuse, nonlocalizing diencephalic dysfunction","C":"All four presentations require an identical, immediate urgent surgical intervention","D":"Only pupillary examination findings reliably distinguish among these four syndromes","E":"Vascular territory alone determines symptoms, regardless of the structure involved"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Violent flailing localizes to the subthalamic nucleus, hemiballismus; polyuria with dilute urine localizes to the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, diabetes insipidus; upward gaze palsy with pupillary dissociation localizes to the pineal region and dorsal midbrain, Parinaud syndrome; and numbness evolving into burning pain localizes to the caudal, thalamogeniculate-supplied thalamus, Dejerine-Roussy syndrome, so each presentation maps to a distinct diencephalic structure with its own recognizable clinical signature rather than reflecting one diffuse process. Describing all four as diffuse nonlocalizing dysfunction ignores their sharply distinct, well-localized mechanisms. Claiming identical urgent surgery is required ignores that management differs entirely by etiology and structure. Relying solely on pupillary findings would miss the other three syndromes, which have no pupillary component. Attributing symptoms to vascular territory alone ignores that the specific structure damaged, not merely the vessel, determines the clinical picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Building a mental map linking each named diencephalic syndrome to its precise structure is the single most efficient strategy for rapidly answering multi-syndrome comparison questions. COMLEX: This same structure-first localizing approach mirrors the osteopathic emphasis on identifying the specific anatomic lesion before selecting a targeted management plan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — Integrated board-style midbrain application, including Parinaud syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops sudden onset of violent, large-amplitude flinging movements of the contralateral arm and leg following a small lacunar stroke. Which structure was most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Globus pallidus internus","B":"Putamen","C":"Subthalamic nucleus","D":"Caudate nucleus","E":"Substantia nigra pars compacta"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus normally provides excitatory drive to the globus pallidus internus as part of the indirect pathway, and its destruction removes this braking influence, producing disinhibited, violent flinging movements of the contralateral limbs known as hemiballismus. Damage to the globus pallidus internus, putamen, or caudate produces other movement disorder patterns rather than ballismus, and substantia nigra pars compacta loss produces parkinsonism from dopamine depletion, not ballismus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemiballismus is one of the most reliably tested single-structure movement disorder localizations on boards, essentially always pointing to the contralateral subthalamic nucleus. COMLEX: small vessel lacunar disease is the classic mechanism, so control of hypertension and diabetes is central to both treatment and prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — subthalamic nucleus anatomy and indirect-pathway circuitry, tested here as a board-style hemiballismus localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":3,"stem":"In the indirect basal ganglia pathway, the subthalamic nucleus normally exerts which effect on its target output nuclei, the globus pallidus internus and substantia nigra pars reticulata?","choices":{"A":"Inhibitory GABAergic input","B":"Excitatory dopaminergic input","C":"Inhibitory glycinergic input","D":"No direct synaptic connection","E":"Excitatory glutamatergic input"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus is the one excitatory, glutamatergic relay within the otherwise largely inhibitory basal ganglia circuitry, driving the globus pallidus internus and substantia nigra pars reticulata to increase their inhibitory output to the thalamus and thereby suppress movement. Its input is not inhibitory GABAergic or glycinergic, since those transmitters dominate the striatal and pallidal projections elsewhere in the circuit, it is not dopaminergic, since dopamine signaling in this circuit comes from the substantia nigra pars compacta, and it is a direct monosynaptic projection rather than absent input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the subthalamic nucleus is the lone glutamatergic, excitatory node in an otherwise GABA-dominated basal ganglia circuit, a detail that resolves many indirect-pathway mechanism questions. COMLEX: deep brain stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus is a major treatment target in advanced Parkinson disease precisely because of this pivotal excitatory role.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — subthalamic nucleus anatomy and indirect-pathway circuitry, tested here as a board-style hemiballismus localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply subthalamic nucleus anatomy to basal ganglia circuitry and hemiballismus.","batch":3,"stem":"On a coronal section through the caudal diencephalon, the subthalamic nucleus is separated from the zona incerta lying above it by a distinct myelinated fiber bundle. Which bundle is this?","choices":{"A":"Thalamic fasciculus","B":"Mammillothalamic tract","C":"Lenticular fasciculus","D":"Stria medullaris thalami","E":"Fasciculus retroflexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus sits immediately inferior to the lenticular fasciculus, and the zona incerta lies above that bundle, so the lenticular fasciculus separates the two; its fibers then pass through the prerubral area (Forel field H) to join the thalamic fasciculus. The thalamic fasciculus lies one level higher, superior to the zona incerta. The mammillothalamic tract runs from the mammillary body to the anterior thalamic nucleus, well medial to this region. The stria medullaris thalami arches over the medial dorsal thalamus to reach the habenular nuclei. The fasciculus retroflexus carries habenular output to the interpeduncular nucleus of the midbrain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The ventral thalamus (subthalamus) contains the subthalamic nucleus, zona incerta, and prerubral field, stacked in order with the lenticular and thalamic fasciculi carrying pallidal output toward the ventral lateral and ventral anterior thalamus. COMLEX: These layered landmarks guide deep brain stimulation electrode placement into the subthalamic nucleus for Parkinson disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 1 — subthalamic nucleus position below the zona incerta, identified among the basal nuclei and allied structures","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has an initially painless sensory stroke, then develops severe burning contralateral hemibody pain and allodynia weeks later. Which vascular territory was most likely originally infarcted?","choices":{"A":"Thalamoperforating artery","B":"Anterior choroidal artery","C":"Thalamogeniculate artery","D":"Lenticulostriate artery","E":"Anterior spinal artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The thalamogeniculate arteries, branches of the P2 segment of the posterior cerebral artery, supply the ventral posterior thalamic nuclei, and their infarction is the classic cause of Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome, in which an initial sensory deficit is later followed by delayed, severe, treatment-resistant central pain. The thalamoperforating arteries instead supply the rostromedial thalamus and hypothalamus, the anterior choroidal artery supplies the posterior limb of the internal capsule and optic tract, the lenticulostriate arteries supply the basal nuclei and internal capsule, and the anterior spinal artery supplies the spinal cord rather than the thalamus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed-onset burning central pain following a sensory stroke is the classic Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome vignette, localizing to the thalamogeniculate artery territory. COMLEX: this pain is often poorly responsive to standard analgesics and instead requires neuropathic pain agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — diencephalic vascular territories and their stroke syndromes, tested here as a board-style vessel-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has sudden bilateral loss of consciousness, vertical gaze palsy, and memory impairment. Angiography shows a single anomalous perforating vessel supplying both medial thalami from a common trunk. Occlusion of which vessel best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Thalamogeniculate artery","B":"Artery of Percheron","C":"Posterior communicating artery","D":"Basilar bifurcation perforator","E":"Anterior choroidal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The artery of Percheron is an anatomic variant in which a single dominant thalamoperforating trunk supplies both medial thalami and the rostral midbrain bilaterally, so its occlusion produces this classic combination of bilateral consciousness impairment, vertical gaze palsy, and memory disturbance from simultaneous bilateral paramedian thalamic infarction. The thalamogeniculate artery supplies only the lateral, ventral posterior thalamus unilaterally, the anterior choroidal artery supplies the internal capsule and optic tract, the posterior communicating artery connects anterior and posterior circulations without this specific bilateral thalamic distribution, and a generic basilar bifurcation perforator does not describe this specific anomalous single-trunk variant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral thalamic infarction with altered consciousness, vertical gaze palsy, and memory loss is the signature artery of Percheron presentation, a high-yield anatomic variant. COMLEX: this diagnosis is easily missed on initial imaging since early diffusion changes can be subtle despite dramatic clinical findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — diencephalic vascular territories and their stroke syndromes, tested here as a board-style vessel-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Correlate diencephalic blood supply with thalamic and hypothalamic stroke syndromes.","batch":3,"stem":"During resection of a tumor in the posterior third ventricle, a surgeon notes that the choroid plexus of the third ventricle and a narrow strip of caudal medial thalamus bordering the ventricle share one arterial source. Which artery supplies both?","choices":{"A":"Anterior choroidal artery","B":"Thalamogeniculate artery","C":"Thalamoperforating artery","D":"Lenticulostriate artery","E":"Medial posterior choroidal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The medial posterior choroidal artery, a branch of the posterior cerebral artery, supplies a narrow part of the caudal and medial thalamus bordering the third ventricle, and its branches also serve the choroid plexus of the third ventricle. The anterior choroidal artery, from the internal carotid, serves the choroid plexus of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle along with the optic tract and internal capsule. The thalamogeniculate arteries supply the caudal thalamus, including the pulvinar and geniculate nuclei. The thalamoperforating arteries from P1 penetrate deeply to supply rostral thalamus. The lenticulostriate arteries are MCA branches to the basal nuclei and internal capsule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Match each choroidal artery to its plexus: anterior choroidal (internal carotid) to the temporal horn, medial posterior choroidal (posterior cerebral) to the third ventricle. COMLEX: Posterior third ventricle and pineal region surgery places the medial posterior choroidal artery and the medial thalamus at risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — diencephalic vascular territories and their stroke syndromes, tested here as a board-style vessel-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has sudden contralateral loss of all sensory modalities over the face, arm, and leg, followed weeks later by spontaneous burning pain in the same distribution, with no motor weakness at any point. This pattern localizes to a lesion of which diencephalic structure?","choices":{"A":"Ventral posterior thalamus","B":"Subthalamic nucleus circuitry","C":"Pineal gland region","D":"Hypothalamic autonomic nuclei","E":"Posterior limb of the internal capsule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A pure sensory deficit later followed by delayed central pain, without any motor involvement, localizes to the thalamic sensory relay nuclei rather than the adjacent motor fibers of the internal capsule, matching the classic evolution of a thalamic sensory syndrome. The hypothalamus produces autonomic and endocrine disturbances rather than a discrete sensory-then-pain pattern, the subthalamic nucleus produces ballistic movement rather than sensory loss, the pineal gland produces dorsal midbrain compressive findings, and the internal capsule would be expected to also produce motor weakness, which is absent here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure sensory loss evolving into delayed central pain, without weakness, is the differentiating clue that separates a thalamic syndrome from an internal capsule lacunar syndrome. COMLEX: always check for accompanying motor findings, since their presence or absence is the fastest way to separate these two adjacent but distinct lesion sites.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 7 — clinical-clue differentiation across thalamic, hypothalamic, subthalamic, and pineal syndromes, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man develops a right Horner syndrome after a neck injury that damaged the cervical sympathetic chain, and he now reports poor nighttime sleep. Light information normally reaches the pineal gland by way of the suprachiasmatic nucleus and the intermediolateral cell column. Postganglionic fibers from which ganglion complete this route?","choices":{"A":"Ciliary ganglion","B":"Inferior cervical ganglion","C":"Otic ganglion","D":"Pterygopalatine ganglion","E":"Superior cervical ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Retinal ganglion cells project to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which influences preganglionic sympathetic neurons of the intermediolateral cell column; these project to the superior cervical ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers travel on branches of the internal carotid artery to innervate the pineal gland and drive nighttime melatonin synthesis. The same ganglion supplies the eye and face, so cervical sympathetic damage can produce Horner syndrome and loss of pineal sympathetic drive together. The ciliary ganglion is parasympathetic to the pupillary sphincter and ciliary muscle. The inferior cervical ganglion, often fused with the first thoracic ganglion as the stellate ganglion, sends sympathetic fibers to the heart and upper limb rather than the pineal. The otic ganglion is parasympathetic to the parotid gland. The pterygopalatine ganglion is parasympathetic to the lacrimal and nasal glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links the suprachiasmatic nucleus to norepinephrine release from the superior cervical ganglion, which controls melatonin release from the pineal gland. COMLEX: A cervical sympathetic lesion can affect both the pupil and the pineal circadian signal, so ask about sleep in a patient with Horner syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 7 — suprachiasmatic nucleus and the retinohypothalamic input that entrains circadian rhythm","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl26_bloom_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use clinical clues to distinguish thalamic sensory syndromes, hypothalamic endocrine/autonomic disorders, subthalamic movement disorders, and pineal/dorsal midbrain syndromes.","batch":3,"stem":"A night-shift nurse who works under bright overhead lighting has a markedly blunted nighttime melatonin level. Exposure to light during the dark period most directly suppresses which step in pinealocytes?","choices":{"A":"Tryptophan hydroxylation in raphe neurons","B":"Serotonin N-acetyltransferase activity","C":"Dopamine release from arcuate neurons","D":"Vasopressin release from supraoptic cells","E":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone output"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinealocytes synthesize melatonin from serotonin; activity of serotonin N-acetyltransferase rises during darkness, enhancing melatonin synthesis, and exposure to light turns this enzymatic activity off, so light at night blunts melatonin. Tryptophan hydroxylation in raphe neurons makes serotonin for brain transmission and is not the light-gated pineal step. Arcuate dopamine inhibits prolactin and is not the light-sensitive pineal enzyme. Supraoptic vasopressin regulates water balance. Corticotropin-releasing hormone from the paraventricular nucleus drives the stress axis rather than melatonin synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Melatonin is made from serotonin in the pineal gland, and its circadian rise at night depends on darkness-driven N-acetyltransferase activity. COMLEX: Light at night and shift work blunt melatonin, which is the basis for timed light avoidance and melatonin in circadian rhythm sleep disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — hypothalamic nuclei and the suprachiasmatic circadian pacemaker","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":1,"stem":"The telencephalon constitutes roughly what fraction of total human brain weight, making it the largest division of the central nervous system?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 45 percent","C":"About 65 percent","D":"About 85 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The telencephalon, comprising the two cerebral hemispheres, constitutes approximately 85 percent of total human brain weight, reflecting how dominant the cortex and its underlying white matter and basal nuclei are relative to the diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum combined. The lower percentages understate the sheer bulk of the hemispheres relative to the rest of the neuraxis. The 95 percent figure overstates it, since the diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum still make up a meaningful remainder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing the telencephalon dominates total brain mass helps frame why cortical strokes and mass lesions are so common clinically relative to isolated brainstem events. COMLEX: This proportion also explains why cortical atrophy patterns dominate structural imaging findings in dementia workups.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the lamina terminalis and third-ventricle boundaries that mark where the diencephalon ends and the telencephalon's own components begin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":1,"stem":"Which deep midline groove physically separates the two cerebral hemispheres of the telencephalon from one another?","choices":{"A":"The transverse cerebral fissure, above the cerebellum","B":"The longitudinal cerebral fissure","C":"The central sulcus groove","D":"The lateral sylvian fissure","E":"The parietooccipital fissure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The longitudinal cerebral fissure is the deep midline groove that separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres, with the falx cerebri projecting into it and the corpus callosum bridging across its floor. The transverse cerebral fissure instead separates the cerebrum from the cerebellum. The central sulcus divides the frontal from the parietal lobe within one hemisphere, not the two hemispheres from each other. The lateral sylvian fissure separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes, and the parietooccipital fissure separates the parietal from the occipital lobe, again both within a single hemisphere.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A falx cerebri meningioma classically grows within the longitudinal fissure and can compress both anterior paracentral gyri, producing bilateral leg weakness. COMLEX: Recognizing this fissure on axial imaging is the first step in orienting to hemispheric versus interhemispheric pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the lamina terminalis and third-ventricle boundaries that mark where the diencephalon ends and the telencephalon's own components begin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":1,"stem":"Besides the cerebral cortex and subcortical white matter, which structures are considered major components of the telencephalon proper?","choices":{"A":"The internal capsule, basal nuclei, amygdala, and olfactory nerve","B":"The pineal gland, mammillary bodies, and fornix column, all diencephalic","C":"The red nucleus, substantia nigra, and cerebral peduncle, all midbrain","D":"The inferior olive, pyramid, and gracile tubercle, all medullary structures","E":"The superior colliculus, tectum, and cerebral aqueduct, all midbrain structures"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Beyond the cortex and its white matter, the telencephalon includes the internal capsule as its major projection pathway, the basal nuclei and amygdaloid complex as deep gray matter aggregates, and the olfactory nerve as its dedicated cranial nerve. The pineal gland and mammillary bodies belong to the epithalamus and hypothalamus of the diencephalon, not the telencephalon. The red nucleus, substantia nigra, and cerebral peduncle are midbrain structures. The inferior olive, pyramid, and gracile tubercle are medullary landmarks, and the superior colliculus, tectum, and cerebral aqueduct belong to the midbrain as well.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sorting structures by embryologic origin, forebrain vesicle versus midbrain versus hindbrain, is a fast way to eliminate distractor structures on anatomy-heavy vignettes. COMLEX: The amygdala's telencephalic origin, despite lying deep within the temporal lobe, explains why temporal lobe lesions so often produce behavioral and autonomic symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — the lamina terminalis and third-ventricle boundaries that mark where the diencephalon ends and the telencephalon's own components begin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":1,"stem":"The insula is classified as a sixth cerebral lobe because it satisfies which defining criterion for what constitutes a lobe of the cerebral cortex?","choices":{"A":"It contains a unique six-layered cytoarchitecture found in no other lobe","B":"It is the only lobe that has its own dedicated cranial nerve pathway","C":"It is bordered by dural venous sinus attachments alone","D":"It is separated from adjacent cortex by a named sulcus, the circular sulcus","E":"It receives blood supply exclusively from the posterior circulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A defining characteristic of a cerebral lobe is separation from adjacent cortex by a named sulcus; the insula lies deep to the lateral sulcus and is bounded by the circular sulcus of the insula, satisfying this criterion despite being buried beneath the frontal, parietal, and temporal opercula. Six-layered cytoarchitecture is a general neocortical feature, not something unique to the insula. No lobe has a dedicated cranial nerve as a defining feature. Dural sinus attachments and exclusive posterior-circulation supply are not the anatomic criteria used to define a lobe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The insula is a favorite \"hidden lobe\" question because it tests whether students know the actual definition of a lobe rather than just memorizing the visible four. COMLEX: Insular involvement in temporal lobe epilepsy can produce visceral auras such as a rising epigastric sensation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — aphasia, visual field loss, and higher cortical signs that localize lesions to these same gyri and sulci","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":1,"stem":"Broca area, responsible for expressive language, is located within which specific gyrus of the dominant frontal lobe?","choices":{"A":"The pars orbitalis of the inferior frontal gyrus, its most anterior part","B":"The pars opercularis and pars triangularis of the inferior frontal gyrus","C":"The posterior third of the superior frontal gyrus, near the vertex","D":"The precentral gyrus at its most inferior extent near the sylvian fissure","E":"The middle frontal gyrus adjacent to the precentral sulcus rostrally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Broca area occupies the pars opercularis and pars triangularis subdivisions of the inferior frontal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere, corresponding to Brodmann areas 44 and 45, and lesions here produce nonfluent, effortful speech with relatively preserved comprehension. The pars orbitalis is the most anterior inferior frontal subdivision and is not part of Broca area proper. The superior frontal gyrus is unrelated to expressive language. The precentral gyrus just anterior to it is primary motor cortex controlling the face and mouth muscles used in speech, not the language-formulation area itself. The middle frontal gyrus near the precentral sulcus instead houses the frontal eye field.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Broca aphasia classically pairs nonfluent, telegraphic speech with intact comprehension and frustrated awareness of the deficit. First Aid mnemonic: Broca sounds like broken boca, boca meaning mouth in Spanish, for nonfluent output. COMLEX: Because Broca area sits adjacent to the face and arm motor cortex, a Broca aphasia is frequently accompanied by contralateral facial and arm weakness from MCA territory involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — aphasia, visual field loss, and higher cortical signs that localize lesions to these same gyri and sulci","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":1,"stem":"The primary visual cortex is located along the banks of which sulcus within the occipital lobe?","choices":{"A":"The calcarine sulcus","B":"The collateral sulcus","C":"The parietooccipital sulcus","D":"The occipitotemporal sulcus","E":"The lingual sulcus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The primary visual cortex, Brodmann area 17, lies along the upper and lower banks of the calcarine sulcus on the medial occipital lobe, with the cuneus forming the superior bank and the lingual gyrus forming the inferior bank. The collateral sulcus instead separates the parahippocampal and lingual gyri on the inferior temporal surface. The parietooccipital sulcus marks the boundary between the parietal and occipital lobes rather than housing the visual cortex itself. The occipitotemporal sulcus separates the fusiform gyrus from adjacent temporal and occipital gyri, and there is no standard structure termed the lingual sulcus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The calcarine sulcus location is essential for predicting visual field deficits, since superior retinal fibers (inferior visual field) end in the cuneus above the sulcus and inferior retinal fibers (superior visual field) end in the lingual gyrus below it. COMLEX: Calcarine cortex lesions from posterior cerebral artery occlusion classically spare the macula because of dual vascular supply at the occipital pole.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — aphasia, visual field loss, and higher cortical signs that localize lesions to these same gyri and sulci","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"Distal to the anterior communicating artery, the anterior cerebral artery follows a characteristic course to reach its cortical territory. Which description best matches that course and the posterior limit of its territory?","choices":{"A":"Along the lateral sulcus over the convexity, back to the angular gyrus","B":"Around the genu of the corpus callosum to the parietooccipital sulcus","C":"Around the splenium of the corpus callosum, back to the occipital pole","D":"Along the collateral sulcus, back to the inferior temporal gyrus","E":"Along the central sulcus, back to the preoccipital notch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Distal to the anterior communicating artery, the anterior cerebral artery arches superiorly around the genu of the corpus callosum and courses caudally to serve the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes to about the level of the parietooccipital sulcus, including the paracentral leg areas of motor and sensory cortex. A course along the lateral sulcus over the convexity to the angular gyrus describes middle cerebral branches. The occipital pole lies behind the parietooccipital sulcus in posterior cerebral territory, so the anterior cerebral artery does not reach it. The collateral sulcus and inferior temporal gyrus are served by temporal branches of the posterior cerebral artery. The central sulcus and preoccipital notch are lateral-surface landmarks that do not define the anterior cerebral territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior cerebral artery owns the medial frontal and parietal surface back to the parietooccipital sulcus, which is why its strokes hit the leg and foot areas of the paracentral lobule. COMLEX: On imaging, a medial frontoparietal wedge that stops at the parietooccipital sulcus localizes to the anterior cerebral artery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicted deficits of ACA, MCA, and PCA occlusion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a large territory infarct shows contralateral face and arm weakness that is worse than leg weakness, along with severe language dysfunction. Which artery territory is most likely involved, and in which hemisphere?","choices":{"A":"The anterior cerebral artery in the dominant hemisphere","B":"The posterior cerebral artery in the dominant hemisphere","C":"The middle cerebral artery in the dominant hemisphere","D":"The middle cerebral artery in the nondominant hemisphere","E":"The anterior choroidal artery in the dominant hemisphere"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The middle cerebral artery supplies the vast lateral convexity, including the face and arm portions of the motor and sensory homunculus along with Broca and Wernicke areas in the dominant hemisphere, so combined face-arm-predominant weakness and severe aphasia together localize to dominant MCA territory. The anterior cerebral artery instead produces leg-predominant deficits without this language pattern. The posterior cerebral artery produces visual field loss rather than motor weakness. A nondominant MCA infarct would produce the same motor pattern but neglect rather than aphasia. Anterior choroidal artery occlusion classically spares the language network while affecting the optic tract and posterior limb of the internal capsule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Language deficit plus face-and-arm weakness sparing the leg is the single most tested MCA stroke pattern on Step 1. COMLEX: A gaze preference toward the side of the lesion often accompanies large dominant MCA strokes from frontal eye field involvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 7 — the stroke-localization algorithm from deficit to vascular territory, applied here to ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical strokes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops sudden loss of vision in the same half of the visual field in both eyes but has entirely normal strength, sensation, and speech. Which artery territory best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The anterior cerebral artery, supplying the medial frontal lobe","B":"The middle cerebral artery, supplying the lateral convexity","C":"The anterior choroidal artery, supplying the optic tract and internal capsule","D":"The superior cerebellar artery, supplying the superior cerebellar surface","E":"The posterior cerebral artery, supplying the primary visual cortex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior cerebral artery supplies the inferior and medial temporal and occipital lobes, including the calcarine primary visual cortex, so an isolated contralateral homonymous hemianopia with no motor, sensory, or language deficit points squarely to PCA territory. The anterior and middle cerebral arteries instead produce motor and sensory deficits from their frontal and lateral convexity territories rather than isolated visual loss. The anterior choroidal artery would typically also compromise the adjacent posterior limb of the internal capsule, producing accompanying weakness. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the cerebellum and produces ataxia rather than a visual field cut.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An isolated homonymous hemianopia with everything else normal is the classic PCA stroke presentation, and macular sparing further suggests dual vascular supply at the occipital pole. COMLEX: PCA strokes near the thalamus can additionally produce contralateral sensory loss if thalamogeniculate branches are involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 7 — the stroke-localization algorithm from deficit to vascular territory, applied here to ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical strokes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion confined to the retrolenticular limb of the internal capsule, sparing the posterior limb proper, would most characteristically produce which deficit?","choices":{"A":"A contralateral hemianopia or quadrantanopia from optic radiation fibers","B":"Tinnitus and difficulty localizing sound from auditory radiation fibers","C":"Dense contralateral hemiplegia and hemianesthesia sparing the visual fields","D":"An ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy with contralateral limb weakness","E":"Contralateral lower facial weakness with sparing of the limbs and trunk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The retrolenticular limb of the internal capsule carries optic radiation fibers, so its lesions produce a contralateral visual deficit that, depending on extent, is a hemianopia or a quadrantanopia in each eye. Tinnitus and difficulty localizing sound result from damage to the sublenticular limb, which carries auditory radiations. Dense contralateral hemiplegia with hemianesthesia reflects posterior limb damage to corticospinal and thalamocortical fibers, which the stem spares. An ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy with contralateral limb weakness is a crossed brainstem pattern. Isolated contralateral lower facial weakness reflects corticonuclear fibers in the genu rather than the retrolenticular limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Map the internal capsule limbs: genu = corticonuclear, posterior limb = corticospinal plus thalamocortical, retrolenticular = optic radiation, sublenticular = auditory radiation. COMLEX: A capsular stroke with a contralateral field cut implies extension into the retrolenticular limb or anterior choroidal territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — optic radiation lesions and the contralateral hemianopia or quadrantanopia they produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding would most strongly suggest a brainstem lesion rather than a cortical or internal capsule lesion as the cause of a patient's contralateral limb weakness?","choices":{"A":"Accompanying expressive aphasia and word-finding difficulty in a right-handed patient","B":"Accompanying hemispatial neglect and inattention of the left side","C":"Equal, dense weakness of the face, arm, and leg","D":"A clearly demarcated horizontal sensory level along the trunk","E":"An accompanying ipsilateral cranial nerve deficit at the level of the lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brainstem lesions classically produce alternating, or crossed, signs, an ipsilateral cranial nerve deficit combined with a contralateral long-tract motor or sensory deficit, and the specific cranial nerve involved pinpoints the rostrocaudal level of the lesion. Expressive aphasia and hemispatial neglect are higher cortical signs pointing to a cortical lesion instead. Equal, dense weakness of face, arm, and leg together is the classic internal capsule pattern, not a brainstem one. A sharply demarcated sensory level on the trunk is the hallmark of a spinal cord lesion, not a brainstem lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Crossed\" or alternating signs are the single fastest way to localize a lesion to the brainstem on Step 1 vignettes. COMLEX: Identifying which cranial nerve is involved lets you localize the lesion to midbrain, pons, or medulla before ordering imaging.","crossRef":"📎 Reflex- and long-tract-based central versus peripheral localization, now extended into the full cortex-to-cord ladder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops a sharply demarcated horizontal sensory level on the trunk along with bilateral leg weakness but entirely normal cranial nerve and mental status findings. Which level of the neuraxis is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral cortex","B":"The internal capsule","C":"The spinal cord","D":"The thalamus","E":"The brainstem"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Deficits restricted strictly to the body, sparing the head entirely, with a distinct horizontal sensory level marking the lowest intact segment, are the hallmark of a spinal cord lesion. Cortical, internal capsule, and thalamic lesions all produce deficits that can involve the face along with the body because their fibers have not yet separated out into a purely spinal distribution. Brainstem lesions, by definition, involve cranial nerve territory and therefore the head, which this patient's normal cranial nerve exam argues against.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A horizontal sensory level sparing the head is the single most reliable localizer to the spinal cord on Step 1 vignettes. COMLEX: Bilateral leg weakness with a thoracic sensory level should immediately raise concern for cord compression requiring urgent imaging.","crossRef":"📎 Reflex- and long-tract-based central versus peripheral localization, now extended into the full cortex-to-cord ladder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is being counseled after imaging shows complete agenesis of the corpus callosum discovered incidentally. His neurologic exam is entirely normal and he has no seizure history. His physician explains that this commissural bundle normally connects which structures?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral cortex with the ipsilateral thalamus and basal nuclei","B":"The cerebellar hemispheres with the contralateral cerebral cortex","C":"The hippocampus with the contralateral amygdala complex","D":"The spinal cord with the ipsilateral brainstem nuclei","E":"Homologous cortical regions of the right and left cerebral hemispheres"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The corpus callosum is the largest commissural bundle of the telencephalon, connecting homologous cortical regions of the two cerebral hemispheres to coordinate interhemispheric processing, and some patients with agenesis remain remarkably asymptomatic because other commissures and ipsilateral pathways partially compensate. Connections between cortex and ipsilateral thalamus or basal nuclei describe projection fibers of the internal capsule, not a commissure. Cerebellar-to-cortical connections run through the cerebellar peduncles and thalamus, not the corpus callosum. The hippocampal commissure, a separate smaller bundle, links the hippocampal formations, not the amygdalae. Spinal cord-to-brainstem connections are unrelated to any telencephalic commissure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corpus callosum agenesis is a classic example of how developmental anomalies can be clinically silent if compensatory pathways exist, unlike acquired lesions of the same structure. COMLEX: Recognizing agenesis on MRI, often showing a \"racing car sign\" on axial imaging, avoids unnecessary workup for a symptomatic mass lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — the diencephalon's own boundary-defining membrane, the lamina terminalis, which is also the site where the earliest telencephalic commissure, the anterior commissure, first develops","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes MRI for chronic headaches. The radiologist notes normal cerebral hemispheres, normal basal nuclei, and a normal amygdala bilaterally, but comments that the olfactory bulbs and tracts appear thin. Which functional category of information is most likely impaired by this finding?","choices":{"A":"Smell, since the olfactory nerve is the telencephalon's dedicated cranial nerve","B":"Hearing, since auditory input first enters through the olfactory tract","C":"Vision, since the olfactory bulb relays retinal ganglion cell output","D":"Taste, since the olfactory tract carries all gustatory afferents directly to cortex","E":"Balance, since the olfactory bulb connects to the vestibular nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The olfactory nerve, cranial nerve I, is unique among cranial nerves in that its bulb and tract are considered part of the telencephalon itself rather than a peripherally entering nerve relaying through the brainstem, so thin olfactory bulbs and tracts point specifically to impaired smell. Hearing enters via the cochlear division of cranial nerve VIII into the brainstem, unrelated to the olfactory tract. Vision is carried by the optic nerve and retinal ganglion cell axons, an entirely separate pathway. Taste is carried mainly by cranial nerves VII, IX, and X, not the olfactory tract, even though smell and taste are perceptually intertwined. Balance depends on the vestibular division of cranial nerve VIII and brainstem vestibular nuclei, not the olfactory bulb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The olfactory nerve's telencephalic origin, unlike every other cranial nerve, is a frequently tested anatomic exception worth memorizing on its own. COMLEX: Anosmia from olfactory bulb or tract injury is a classic early finding in traumatic brain injury from shearing forces at the cribriform plate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — third-ventricle and diencephalic boundary structures, contrasted here with the telencephalon's own dedicated cranial nerve, which relays sensory input without first passing through the diencephalon or brainstem","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":2,"stem":"A neuroanatomy instructor points to a coronal section and asks students to identify the deep gray matter structure lying within the wall of the temporal horn of the lateral ventricle, whose axons coalesce into the fornix, stria terminalis, and amygdalofugal pathway. Which paired telencephalic structures best fit this description?","choices":{"A":"The caudate nucleus and the putamen","B":"The globus pallidus and the substantia nigra","C":"The thalamus and the subthalamic nucleus","D":"The hippocampal complex and the amygdala","E":"The claustrum and the external capsule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hippocampal complex and the amygdala both sit in the walls of the temporal horn of the lateral ventricle, and their efferent axons coalesce into the named output pathways described, the fornix from the hippocampus and the stria terminalis and amygdalofugal pathway from the amygdala. The caudate and putamen form the striatum, positioned along the lateral ventricle but not defined by these specific output tracts. The globus pallidus and substantia nigra are basal-nuclei-associated structures without this fornix/stria terminalis relationship. The thalamus and subthalamic nucleus are diencephalic, not telencephalic. The claustrum and external capsule lie between the putamen and insula and are unrelated to these named pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fornix, stria terminalis, and amygdalofugal pathway trio is a favorite way to test whether students can pair each output tract with its structure of origin. COMLEX: Mesial temporal sclerosis affecting this hippocampal-amygdalar region is the most common pathologic substrate of temporal lobe epilepsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — diencephalic boundary anatomy, now contrasted with these temporal-horn telencephalic structures that instead define the limbic output pathways","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a small cortical lesion that selectively impairs sound comprehension while leaving his ability to see, speak fluently, and move normally intact. Testing localizes the lesion to the gyri housing primary auditory cortex. Where should the lesion be found?","choices":{"A":"The superior temporal gyrus along its full lateral convexity surface","B":"The transverse temporal gyri, on the superior surface of the temporal lobe","C":"The angular gyrus of the inferior parietal lobule, posteriorly","D":"The postcentral gyrus along its most inferior lateral extent","E":"The lingual gyrus on the inferior occipital surface, medially"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary auditory cortex, Brodmann area 41, occupies the transverse temporal gyri, small gyri folded onto the superior surface of the temporal lobe within the lateral sulcus, and a discrete lesion there impairs basic sound processing while sparing adjacent association cortex needed for language comprehension and fluency. The superior temporal gyrus more broadly houses auditory association cortex and part of Wernicke area, a different functional level than primary sound processing. The angular gyrus is part of the language and multimodal association network, not primary auditory cortex. The postcentral gyrus houses somatosensory, not auditory, cortex. The lingual gyrus is visual association cortex on the occipital surface.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing primary auditory cortex, area 41, from the surrounding auditory association cortex, area 42, and Wernicke area is a common source of exam confusion worth clarifying explicitly. COMLEX: Isolated cortical deafness from bilateral transverse temporal gyri lesions is rare but classically tested as a distinct entity from aphasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — predicted deficits from named vessel territory lesions, here extended to the specific gyral landmark, the transverse temporal gyri, that middle cerebral artery branches must reach to produce a primary auditory deficit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old right-handed woman suffers a stroke and on exam struggles to repeat spoken phrases despite fluent, grammatically intact speech and largely preserved comprehension. Her lesion most likely involves white matter deep to which pair of gyri that jointly comprise the classic language network's receptive component?","choices":{"A":"The pars opercularis and pars triangularis of the inferior frontal gyrus","B":"The precentral and postcentral gyri along the central sulcus","C":"The superior and middle frontal gyri of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex","D":"The supramarginal and angular gyri of the inferior parietal lobule","E":"The precuneus and cingulate gyrus on the medial hemisphere surface"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The supramarginal gyrus, Brodmann area 40, and the angular gyrus, area 39, together make up the inferior parietal lobule and form the parietal component of Wernicke area, and a lesion in the arcuate fasciculus deep to this region classically produces conduction aphasia, with fluent speech, preserved comprehension, but poor repetition. The pars opercularis and pars triangularis instead form Broca area, whose lesions cause nonfluent rather than fluent aphasia. The precentral and postcentral gyri are primary motor and sensory cortex, unrelated to language repetition. The superior and middle frontal gyri house executive and oculomotor functions, not core language circuitry. The precuneus and cingulate gyrus are medial parietal and limbic structures without this direct role in repetition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conduction aphasia with intact fluency and comprehension but poor repetition localizes to the arcuate fasciculus connecting Broca and Wernicke areas, a frequently tested aphasia subtype. COMLEX: Because the supramarginal and angular gyri sit at the parietotemporal junction, lesions here can also produce components of Gerstmann syndrome when more extensive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 7 — the stroke-localization algorithm moving from deficit to likely vascular territory, applied here to a fluent-but-poor-repetition language pattern that localizes to the parietal language gyri rather than the frontal language gyri","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man develops sudden weakness affecting his contralateral hand and fingers far more than his shoulder or trunk. Cortical mapping studies show this discrepancy reflects disproportionate representation of the hand within which gyrus?","choices":{"A":"The postcentral gyrus, in the region corresponding to the medial third of the sensory homunculus","B":"The superior frontal gyrus, immediately adjacent to the paracentral lobule medially","C":"The anterior paracentral gyrus, on the medial surface of the hemisphere","D":"The supramarginal gyrus, within the inferior parietal lobule more laterally","E":"The precentral gyrus, in the region corresponding to the middle third of the motor homunculus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The precentral gyrus forms primary motor cortex, and within its middle third the hand and upper extremity occupy a disproportionately large cortical territory relative to the shoulder or trunk, consistent with the fine motor control the hand requires, so a lesion there produces hand-and-finger-predominant weakness. The postcentral gyrus is sensory rather than motor cortex, so it would not explain a primarily motor deficit. The superior frontal gyrus is not part of the primary motor homunculus. The anterior paracentral gyrus represents the lower extremity and foot on the medial surface, not the hand. The supramarginal gyrus is parietal association cortex involved in language and praxis, not primary motor output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The disproportionately large hand and face representations on the motor and sensory homunculi, the classic \"motor and sensory man\" drawings, reflect cortical magnification for fine control and discrimination. COMLEX: A small cortical lesion can therefore produce dramatic focal hand weakness even when the overall infarct volume is small.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — named vessel deficit prediction, here applied to the fine cortical somatotopy within a single artery's territory that explains why some MCA strokes are hand-predominant rather than diffusely weak","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man undergoing cardiac surgery experiences an episode of severe intraoperative hypotension. Postoperatively he has proximal weakness of both shoulders and upper arms, with the hands, face, and legs all relatively spared. Which vascular explanation best accounts for this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral complete middle cerebral artery territory infarction","B":"Bilateral complete anterior cerebral artery territory infarction","C":"Bilateral watershed infarction in the overlapping distal territory of the ACA and MCA","D":"Isolated unilateral posterior cerebral artery territory infarction","E":"Bilateral lenticulostriate artery territory infarction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Systemic hypotension can compromise perfusion at the watershed zone where the distal territories of the anterior and middle cerebral arteries overlap, and because this zone corresponds to the proximal upper extremity and shoulder region of the motor cortex, patients classically develop a \"man-in-the-barrel\" pattern of bilateral proximal arm weakness with sparing of the hands, face, and legs. Complete MCA or ACA territory infarcts would produce far more extensive deficits including the face or leg, not this selective proximal pattern. Posterior cerebral artery infarction produces visual field loss rather than proximal weakness. Lenticulostriate artery infarction affects the basal nuclei and internal capsule, producing dense hemiplegia rather than a bilateral proximal-only pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The man-in-the-barrel watershed pattern from global hypoperfusion, classically after cardiac surgery or severe hypotension, is a frequently tested exception to focal single-vessel stroke patterns; First Aid names this ACA-MCA border-zone infarct pattern man-in-a-barrel syndrome. COMLEX: Watershed infarcts can also occur in the spinal cord and cerebellum, so a global hypotensive episode should prompt consideration of multiple watershed territories at once.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 7 — the stroke-localization algorithm, applied here to a bilateral, hypoperfusion-driven pattern rather than a single occluded named vessel","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old right-handed woman suddenly develops left-sided weakness of the face and arm with leg sparing. She ignores objects and people on her left side, fails to draw the left half of a clock, and denies that anything is wrong with her left arm despite obvious weakness. Which vessel territory and hemisphere best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The left, dominant middle cerebral artery","B":"The right, nondominant middle cerebral artery","C":"The right, nondominant anterior cerebral artery","D":"The left, dominant posterior cerebral artery","E":"The right, nondominant posterior cerebral artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Left-sided face-and-arm-predominant weakness points to a right hemisphere middle cerebral artery territory lesion, and the accompanying left-sided neglect, constructional deficits, and anosognosia, denial of the deficit, are classic nondominant hemisphere findings rather than the aphasia seen with dominant hemisphere MCA strokes. A left, dominant MCA stroke would instead produce right-sided weakness and aphasia, not this presentation. Anterior cerebral artery involvement would produce leg-predominant rather than face-and-arm-predominant weakness. Posterior cerebral artery lesions in either hemisphere would produce visual field loss rather than this motor and neglect pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neglect and anosognosia without aphasia are the signature of a nondominant, usually right, MCA stroke, contrasted with aphasia from a dominant, usually left, MCA stroke of similar vascular extent. COMLEX: Anosognosia can delay recognition of the deficit by both the patient and untrained observers, making early neglect screening clinically important.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 7 — the stroke-localization algorithm, applied here to hemisphere-specific higher cortical signs that refine a same-artery, same-motor-pattern presentation into dominant versus nondominant localization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man reports that he can no longer read despite still being able to write a coherent letter by hand and speaking fluently with intact comprehension. Formal testing confirms a right homonymous hemianopia and alexia without agraphia. Which vessel territory most likely explains this unusual combination?","choices":{"A":"The left posterior cerebral artery, involving the occipital lobe and splenium","B":"The left middle cerebral artery, involving Wernicke area","C":"The right posterior cerebral artery, involving the occipital lobe alone","D":"The left anterior cerebral artery, involving the medial frontal lobe","E":"The right middle cerebral artery, involving the angular gyrus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A left posterior cerebral artery infarct destroys the left primary visual cortex, causing a right homonymous hemianopia, and can also involve the splenium of the corpus callosum, disconnecting visual information from the intact right occipital lobe from reaching the language-dominant left hemisphere, producing alexia without agraphia since writing itself is unaffected. A left MCA infarct involving Wernicke area would impair both reading and writing along with comprehension, not spare writing selectively. A right PCA infarct alone would cause a left, not right, visual field cut and would not disconnect language cortex the same way. Left ACA involvement would not explain the visual field cut or reading-specific deficit. A right MCA lesion involving the angular gyrus would not typically spare writing while eliminating reading in this disconnection pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alexia without agraphia from a dominant PCA infarct plus splenial involvement is a classic disconnection syndrome tested as a named entity distinct from Wernicke aphasia. COMLEX: The combination of a visual field cut with an isolated reading deficit but normal writing should prompt suspicion for this specific vascular pattern rather than a primary language cortex lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — named vessel deficit prediction, extended here to a disconnection syndrome arising from combined occipital and callosal PCA territory involvement rather than a single cortical area","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman develops sudden onset of severe burning, poorly localized pain over her entire left body several weeks after an initial stroke that caused transient left-sided numbness. Her exam now shows subtle residual sensory loss but no weakness. Which level of the neuraxis was most likely originally affected?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral cortex, consistent with a parietal sensory strip lesion","B":"The thalamus, consistent with Dejerine-Roussy syndrome","C":"The internal capsule, consistent with a lacunar sensory stroke","D":"The brainstem, consistent with a lateral medullary lesion","E":"The spinal cord, consistent with a dorsal column lesion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thalamic strokes, particularly those involving the thalamogeniculate branches of the posterior cerebral artery, can produce an initial contralateral sensory deficit followed weeks to months later by intense, burning, poorly localized neuropathic pain known as Dejerine-Roussy or thalamic pain syndrome as central sensitization develops during recovery. Cortical, internal capsule, and brainstem sensory strokes can all cause contralateral sensory loss acutely but are not classically associated with this specific delayed central pain syndrome. A dorsal column spinal cord lesion would cause loss of vibration and proprioception rather than this diffuse burning pain pattern, and would not fit a hemibody distribution from a single vascular event.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dejerine-Roussy syndrome is a high-yield example of central poststroke pain, distinguishing thalamic lesions from other sensory-pathway strokes by its delayed, burning, treatment-resistant quality. COMLEX: This syndrome often responds poorly to standard analgesics and may require neuropathic pain agents such as gabapentinoids or tricyclic antidepressants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — cranial nerve and brainstem lesion localization, contrasted here with a purely thalamic sensory syndrome that produces no cranial nerve findings at all","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man develops sudden weakness of the entire right side of his face, including the forehead, along with left-sided body weakness. On closer exam, his right eye cannot abduct past midline. Which level of the neuraxis best explains this alternating pattern of findings?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral cortex, given the combination of facial and limb weakness","B":"The internal capsule, given dense equal involvement of face and limb","C":"The brainstem, specifically the pons, given crossed cranial nerve and long-tract signs","D":"The thalamus, given contralateral sensory and motor findings","E":"The spinal cord, given a clear and distinct rostrocaudal level of involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An ipsilateral abducens palsy, impaired abduction of the right eye, combined with contralateral, left-sided body weakness is a classic alternating or crossed brainstem sign localizing to the pons, where the abducens nucleus and the corticospinal tract lie close together before the tract decussates more caudally; the forehead-involving right facial weakness is a lower motor neuron sign from the adjacent facial nucleus or fascicle, whose fibers loop around the abducens nucleus. A cortical lesion would produce weakness on one side of the body and face together without this specific crossed cranial nerve pattern. An internal capsule lesion causes dense, equal, purely contralateral hemiplegia without any cranial nerve involvement since cranial nerve nuclei lie caudal to the capsule. A thalamic lesion produces contralateral sensory loss, not this ipsilateral eye movement abnormality paired with contralateral weakness. A spinal cord lesion cannot produce a cranial nerve finding at all, since cranial nerve nuclei lie rostral to the cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crossed brainstem signs, an ipsilateral cranial nerve deficit with contralateral body weakness, are among the most reliably tested localization patterns on Step 1. COMLEX: The specific cranial nerve involved, here the abducens, pinpoints the pons as the level rather than the midbrain or medulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — interpreting cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to cortex, brainstem, or other levels, applied here to a textbook alternating pontine syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with sudden left-sided weakness. Examination shows the weakness and accompanying numbness affect the face, arm, and leg equally, without any aphasia, neglect, visual field cut, or crossed cranial nerve finding. Which level of the neuraxis is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The internal capsule, given dense, equal limb involvement without cortical signs","B":"The cerebral cortex, given the absence of aphasia, neglect, or other cortical signs","C":"The brainstem, given the absence of any crossed cranial nerve findings","D":"The spinal cord, given a distinct horizontal sensory level along the trunk","E":"The thalamus, given equal involvement of the face, arm, and leg"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dense, equal weakness and numbness of the face, arm, and leg without higher cortical signs, a visual field cut, or crossed cranial nerve findings is most consistent with a lesion in the internal capsule, where corticobulbar, corticospinal, and thalamocortical fibers are tightly packed together, so face, arm, and leg are involved together, and the lesion lacks any cortical or brainstem structures to produce those other signs. A cortical lesion of this size would typically still show at least a face-arm-leg gradient and often cortical signs such as aphasia or neglect, which this patient lacks entirely. A brainstem lesion would be expected to show a crossed cranial nerve finding, absent here. A spinal cord lesion would classically show a horizontal sensory level, which is not described. Thalamic lesions typically produce sensory loss out of proportion to motor loss rather than the combined dense motor and sensory deficit seen here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When a stroke vignette specifically excludes cortical signs, cranial nerve findings, and a spinal sensory level, the internal capsule becomes the most likely remaining answer by process of elimination. COMLEX: This lesion-by-exclusion reasoning mirrors how COMLEX vignettes are frequently built to test the full cortex-to-cord localization ladder in a single question.","crossRef":"📖 Using long-tract signs to support central versus peripheral localization, applied here as the capstone question requiring the full cortical, capsular, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal differential at once","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":3,"stem":"Which of the following structures is a telencephalic derivative rather than a diencephalic one?","choices":{"A":"Thalamus","B":"Hypothalamus","C":"Basal nuclei","D":"Subthalamic nucleus","E":"Epithalamus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The basal nuclei, including the caudate, putamen, and globus pallidus, arise from the telencephalon and lie deep to the cerebral cortex within each hemisphere, distinguishing them from the diencephalic structures. The thalamus, hypothalamus, subthalamic nucleus, and epithalamus are all diencephalic subdivisions surrounding the third ventricle rather than telencephalic components.","boardPrep":"Step 1: telencephalon gives rise to cortex, basal nuclei, and hippocampus/amygdala, while diencephalon gives rise to thalamus, hypothalamus, subthalamus, and epithalamus, a distinction worth memorizing as a clean pair. COMLEX: this embryologic split also predicts which structures share blood supply with the cerebral hemispheres versus the brainstem-adjacent perforators.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — telencephalic components and their relationship to the cerebral hemispheres, tested here as a board-style identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":3,"stem":"During development, cells arising from the diencephalon migrate across the developing internal capsule and settle medial to the putamen. Which adult basal nucleus do these cells form?","choices":{"A":"Caudate nucleus","B":"Claustrum","C":"Globus pallidus","D":"Amygdala","E":"Subthalamic nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Internal capsule axons bisect the embryonic corpus striatum into a medial caudate nucleus and a lateral putamen; meanwhile, diencephalic cells migrate across the developing internal capsule to lie medial to the putamen, becoming the globus pallidus, which joins the putamen to form the lenticular nucleus. The caudate nucleus arises from the corpus striatum medial to the capsule rather than from migrating diencephalic cells. The claustrum lies lateral to the putamen, beyond the external capsule. The amygdala develops in the wall of the temporal horn. The subthalamic nucleus remains in the diencephalon and does not cross the internal capsule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Caudate plus putamen form the striatum, putamen plus globus pallidus form the lenticular nucleus, and the pallidum has a diencephalic origin. COMLEX: These groupings matter for localizing lenticulostriate infarcts and basal nuclei movement disorders on imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — why caudate plus putamen equals striatum","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the major components of the telencephalon and relate them to the cerebral hemispheres.","batch":3,"stem":"In the developing telencephalon, three major interhemispheric commissures form in sequence. Which commissure is the first to appear, arising within the lamina terminalis?","choices":{"A":"Corpus callosum","B":"Hippocampal commissure","C":"Posterior commissure","D":"Habenular commissure","E":"Anterior commissure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The three major interhemispheric commissures are the anterior commissure, the hippocampal commissure, and the corpus callosum, and the first to appear is the anterior commissure, which arises within the lamina terminalis. The corpus callosum is the third to develop; it originates from the area of the lamina terminalis as an astrocytic framework and then enlarges caudally. The hippocampal commissure is the second, forming with the hippocampal primordium. The posterior commissure marks the diencephalon-midbrain junction rather than the telencephalic set. The habenular commissure is a small epithalamic bundle on the posterior commissure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Agenesis of the corpus callosum may be accompanied by absence of the anterior and hippocampal commissures, and some patients have few deficits. COMLEX: The lamina terminalis marks the adult position of the anterior neuropore, linking commissure development to anencephaly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — telencephalic components and their relationship to the cerebral hemispheres, tested here as a board-style identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops nonfluent speech with effortful, halting output but preserved comprehension after a stroke involving the posterior inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere. This region corresponds to which cortical area?","choices":{"A":"Broca speech area","B":"Primary motor cortex","C":"Wernicke area","D":"Primary visual cortex","E":"Primary auditory cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Broca area, located in the posterior inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere, governs the motor programming of speech production, so its damage produces exactly this nonfluent, effortful output with preserved comprehension. Wernicke area instead sits in the posterior superior temporal gyrus and produces fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension when damaged, primary motor cortex produces limb weakness rather than a language-specific deficit, and primary visual and auditory cortex govern basic sensory processing rather than expressive language.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Broca is broken speech, or nonfluent aphasia with intact comprehension, while Wernicke is wordy but senseless, or fluent aphasia with impaired comprehension, a classic paired mnemonic. COMLEX: repetition is impaired in both classic Broca and Wernicke aphasia, which is what separates them from the isolated transcortical aphasias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — cerebral lobes, sulci/gyri, and functional cortical areas, tested here as a board-style lesion-to-function vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":3,"stem":"Lesions of the precentral gyrus produce contralateral weakness following a predictable somatotopic map. Which body region is represented most medially, near the paracentral lobule?","choices":{"A":"Face and tongue","B":"Hand and fingers","C":"Trunk and shoulder","D":"Leg and foot","E":"Forearm and wrist"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The motor homunculus places the leg and foot representation most medially along the precentral gyrus, extending onto the paracentral lobule on the medial surface of the hemisphere, which explains why parasagittal lesions such as anterior cerebral artery infarcts weaken the leg while sparing the face and arm, whereas lateral-convexity middle cerebral artery infarcts spare the leg. The face, hand, trunk, and forearm all map more laterally along the convexity, progressively farther from the interhemispheric fissure than the leg representation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the motor and sensory homunculus runs leg-medial to face-lateral, directly explaining why ACA strokes spare the face and arm while MCA strokes spare the leg. COMLEX: this same somatotopic logic applies to the sensory homunculus along the postcentral gyrus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — cerebral lobes, sulci/gyri, and functional cortical areas, tested here as a board-style lesion-to-function vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe major cerebral lobes, important sulci/gyri, and high-yield functional cortical areas.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient loses the ability to recognize familiar faces despite normal visual acuity and intact recognition of other common objects, following a stroke in the occipitotemporal region. Which functional cortical area was most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Primary visual cortex","B":"Supplementary motor area","C":"Angular gyrus","D":"Wernicke area","E":"Fusiform gyrus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The fusiform gyrus, in the occipitotemporal region, contains specialized face-processing cortex, and its damage produces prosopagnosia, the selective inability to recognize familiar faces, while sparing general object recognition and visual acuity. Primary visual cortex damage would impair basic vision and produce a field cut rather than a selective face-recognition deficit, the angular gyrus governs reading and calculation, Wernicke area governs language comprehension, and the supplementary motor area governs complex movement planning, none of which produces prosopagnosia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prosopagnosia localizes specifically to the fusiform gyrus, a frequently tested example of highly specialized cortical function within the occipitotemporal region. COMLEX: bilateral fusiform damage produces the most severe and persistent prosopagnosia, while unilateral right-sided lesions are the more common clinical pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — cerebral lobes, sulci/gyri, and functional cortical areas, tested here as a board-style lesion-to-function vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman develops new difficulty retaining recent information. Strength, sensation, speech, and visual fields are normal. MRI shows an infarct of the parahippocampal gyrus and inferomedial temporal cortex, sparing the lateral convexity, insula, and internal capsule. Which artery's temporal branches were most likely occluded?","choices":{"A":"Middle cerebral artery","B":"Posterior cerebral artery","C":"Anterior choroidal artery","D":"Anterior cerebral artery","E":"Superior cerebellar artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Temporal branches arising from the P3 segment of the posterior cerebral artery supply the inferomedial temporal lobe, and the parahippocampal gyrus is listed among the telencephalic structures in the posterior cerebral domain, so this infarct reflects posterior cerebral temporal branch occlusion. The middle cerebral artery serves the lateral convexity and insula, both spared. The anterior choroidal artery reaches the hippocampus and amygdala but also the posterior limb of the internal capsule and optic tract, so it would add hemiparesis or a field cut. The anterior cerebral artery serves the medial frontal and parietal surface. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellum and dorsolateral rostral pons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior cerebral artery strokes are not just visual; temporal branches to the medial temporal lobe can impair memory. COMLEX: Consider a posterior circulation source when new memory loss accompanies an inferomedial temporal infarct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampal and parahippocampal regions in memory","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has an infarct on MRI involving the transverse temporal gyri and the adjacent inferior parietal lobule of one hemisphere, with the medial hemispheric surface and the occipital lobe spared. Which cerebral artery territory is most likely infarcted?","choices":{"A":"Anterior choroidal artery","B":"Anterior cerebral artery","C":"Posterior cerebral artery","D":"Middle cerebral artery","E":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Distal branches of the middle cerebral artery serve the trunk, arm, and head areas of the precentral and postcentral gyri, the auditory cortex in the transverse temporal gyri, the parietal association cortex of the parietal lobules, and much of the superior and lateral frontal lobe, so this lateral infarct is in middle cerebral territory. The anterior choroidal artery serves deep structures such as the optic tract, internal capsule, and hippocampus. The anterior cerebral artery serves the spared medial surface. The posterior cerebral artery serves the spared occipital lobe and the inferomedial temporal lobe. The posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the lateral medulla and inferior cerebellum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary auditory cortex lies in middle cerebral territory, but a unilateral lesion rarely causes deafness because the central auditory pathway is bilateral. COMLEX: A lateral temporoparietal infarct points to a middle cerebral source, so evaluate the ipsilateral carotid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 6 — why unilateral central auditory lesions rarely cause complete unilateral deafness","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories with common stroke presentations.","batch":3,"stem":"The middle cerebral artery passes laterally to the limen insulae and divides into superior and inferior trunks. Branches of which segment directly serve the insular lobe?","choices":{"A":"M1 segment","B":"A1 segment","C":"M4 segment","D":"P2 segment","E":"M2 segment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The initial M1 segment of the middle cerebral artery gives rise to the lenticulostriate arteries; the superior and inferior trunks and their branches form M2 through M4, and the insular lobe itself is served by branches of M2. As these branches pass onto the inner surface of the opercula they become M3, and on exiting the lateral sulcus they become M4, which serves the lateral convexity cortex. The A1 segment of the anterior cerebral artery sends branches to the optic chiasm and anterior hypothalamus. The P2 segment of the posterior cerebral artery gives the thalamogeniculate and posterior choroidal branches to the caudal diencephalon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Middle cerebral segments run M1 (horizontal, lenticulostriate), M2 (insular), M3 (opercular), M4 (cortical). COMLEX: An M1 occlusion adds deep capsular infarction from the lenticulostriate arteries, while an M2 branch occlusion produces a cortical syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 Obj 3 — ACA/MCA/PCA/anterior choroidal artery deficit-pattern logic","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has contralateral face, arm, and leg weakness that is roughly equal in severity, with no aphasia, neglect, or visual field cut. Which lesion location is most consistent with this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Posterior limb of the internal capsule","B":"Thalamus, ventral posterior nuclei","C":"Brainstem, medial lemniscus fascicles","D":"Spinal cord, lateral corticospinal tract","E":"Cerebral cortex, primary motor area"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The posterior limb of the internal capsule densely packs corticospinal fibers to the face, arm, and leg into a small territory before they fan out cortically, so a small lacunar infarct here produces equally severe weakness across all three regions without any cortical signs, since cortical association areas are untouched. A cortical lesion would instead be expected to produce disproportionate face/arm or leg weakness along with cortical signs such as aphasia or neglect depending on the territory, a thalamic lesion would produce sensory rather than pure motor loss, a lesion of the medial lemniscus fascicles in the brainstem would cost contralateral position and vibration sense rather than strength, and a spinal cord lesion would produce a sensory level and bowel/bladder involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: equal, dense contralateral face/arm/leg weakness without any cortical signs is the classic pure motor lacunar stroke localizing to the posterior limb of the internal capsule. COMLEX: this is the most common lacunar syndrome and reflects small-vessel disease from chronic hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — cortical versus internal capsule versus thalamic versus brainstem versus spinal cord localization, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has an ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy together with contralateral body weakness, a combination sometimes called a crossed finding. Which lesion location is most consistent with this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Cerebral cortex","B":"Internal capsule","C":"Brainstem","D":"Thalamus","E":"Spinal cord"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crossed findings, an ipsilateral cranial nerve deficit paired with contralateral body weakness, occur only in the brainstem, where a cranial nerve nucleus or fascicle exits on the same side as the lesion while the corticospinal tract has not yet decussated and therefore carries a contralateral deficit below the lesion. A cortical, internal capsule, or thalamic lesion lies above the level of any cranial nerve nucleus and cannot produce a crossed cranial nerve finding, and a spinal cord lesion lies below the level of the cranial nerve nuclei entirely and produces only body-level findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: any 'crossed' finding, ipsilateral face plus contralateral body, localizes the lesion to the brainstem and nowhere else in the neuraxis. COMLEX: identifying which specific cranial nerve is crossed further localizes the lesion to midbrain, pons, or medulla.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — cortical versus internal capsule versus thalamic versus brainstem versus spinal cord localization, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl27_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish cortical motor/sensory deficits from internal capsule, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has a discrete sensory level on the trunk, bilateral leg weakness, and new urinary retention, with no cranial nerve findings and no cortical signs. Which lesion location is most consistent with this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Cerebral cortex","B":"Internal capsule","C":"Thalamus","D":"Spinal cord","E":"Brainstem"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A discrete sensory level marking a specific spinal segment, combined with bilateral leg weakness and bowel or bladder dysfunction, is the hallmark localization to the spinal cord, since only a transverse cord lesion produces this exact combination of a sharp dermatomal boundary and bilateral involvement below it. A cortical, internal capsule, or thalamic lesion would produce unilateral rather than bilateral findings, and a brainstem lesion would be expected to add cranial nerve involvement, none of which is present here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a sharp bilateral sensory level plus bowel/bladder involvement is the fastest way to localize a lesion to the spinal cord rather than any level above it. COMLEX: transverse myelitis, cord compression, and spinal cord infarction should all be on the differential once this pattern is recognized.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — cortical versus internal capsule versus thalamic versus brainstem versus spinal cord localization, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":1,"stem":"Which category of subcortical white matter fibers connects adjacent or distant gyri within the same cerebral hemisphere, such as the arcuate fasciculus and the cingulum?","choices":{"A":"Association fibers","B":"Commissural fibers","C":"Projection fibers","D":"Corticofugal fibers","E":"Thalamocortical radiations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Association fibers link cortical regions within one hemisphere, ranging from short U-fibers joining adjacent gyri to long bundles such as the arcuate fasciculus, uncinate fasciculus, and cingulum linking distant lobes. Commissural fibers instead cross the midline to connect homologous areas of the two hemispheres. Projection fibers, including corticofugal and thalamocortical radiation fibers, run vertically between cortex and subcortical or spinal targets rather than horizontally within one hemisphere.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing arcuate fasciculus damage as an association fiber lesion, not a commissural or projection fiber lesion, is essential for correctly framing conduction aphasia mechanistically. COMLEX: Long association bundle injury from diffuse axonal injury after trauma often produces subtle cognitive-linguistic deficits without a discrete focal weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — the cortex-vs-internal-capsule-vs-thalamus-vs-brainstem-vs-cord localization ladder, now given the internal capsule's own detailed fiber anatomy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":1,"stem":"Callosal fibers that interconnect the two occipital lobes loop through one part of the corpus callosum, forming the major, or occipital, forceps. Through which part do these fibers cross?","choices":{"A":"Rostrum","B":"Genu","C":"Body","D":"Splenium","E":"Anterior commissure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The corpus callosum consists, from rostral to caudal, of a rostrum, genu, body (trunk), and splenium. Fibers interconnecting the occipital lobes loop through the splenium, forming the major (occipital) forceps, and the tapetum in the wall of the atrium and posterior horn is also made of fibers crossing in the splenium. The rostrum is the thin anterior-inferior end of the callosum. The genu carries frontal interconnections that arch rostrally as the minor (frontal) forceps. The body interconnects the large intermediate regions of the hemispheres. The anterior commissure is a separate, smaller bundle linking parts of the frontal and temporal lobes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The splenium carries interoccipital fibers, which is why a left PCA infarct involving the splenium produces alexia without agraphia. COMLEX: Callosotomy for severe generalized seizures sections the anterior 75% of the corpus callosum and largely spares the splenium, limiting visual disconnection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — left PCA infarct involving the occipital lobe and splenium, producing alexia without agraphia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":1,"stem":"The internal capsule is composed of which category of white matter fibers, carrying both corticofugal output such as the corticospinal tract and corticopetal input such as thalamocortical radiations?","choices":{"A":"Association fibers","B":"Commissural fibers","C":"Short U-fibers","D":"Arcuate fasciculus fibers","E":"Projection fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Projection fibers connect the cerebral cortex to subcortical, brainstem, and spinal targets, and the internal capsule is the densely packed projection fiber bundle carrying corticofugal fibers such as the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts downward and corticopetal fibers such as thalamocortical radiations upward to the cortex. Association fibers connect gyri within one hemisphere rather than cortex to subcortical structures. Commissural fibers cross between hemispheres. Short U-fibers and the arcuate fasciculus are both examples of association fibers, not projection fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The internal capsule is the single highest-yield example of projection fibers because of how much motor and sensory traffic funnels through such a small structure. COMLEX: This funneling explains why even a tiny lacunar infarct here can produce a deficit as severe as a much larger cortical stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — the cortex-vs-internal-capsule-vs-thalamus-vs-brainstem-vs-cord localization ladder, now given the internal capsule's own detailed fiber anatomy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":1,"stem":"The caudate nucleus and putamen together are developmentally and anatomically grouped as which basal nuclei structure?","choices":{"A":"The paleostriatum","B":"The neostriatum","C":"The lentiform nucleus alone","D":"The claustrum complex","E":"The subthalamic complex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The caudate nucleus and putamen share a common developmental origin and are grouped together as the neostriatum, the phylogenetically newer striatal component, while the globus pallidus forms the older paleostriatum. The lentiform nucleus is a shape-based term combining the putamen and globus pallidus together, not synonymous with neostriatum. The claustrum is a separate thin sheet of gray matter deep to the insula, unrelated to this grouping. The subthalamic complex is a diencephalic structure functionally allied with, but not part of, the striatum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Confusing neostriatum with paleostriatum is a common exam trap; caudate plus putamen equals neostriatum, while globus pallidus equals paleostriatum. First Aid mnemonic: striatum equals putamen for motor plus caudate nucleus for cognitive function, while the lentiform nucleus equals putamen plus globus pallidus. COMLEX: This distinction underlies why caudate-predominant disease, such as Huntington disease, produces a different clinical picture than pallidal-predominant disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — the subthalamic nucleus and hemiballismus, already introduced from the diencephalic vantage point, now placed within the full basal nuclei circuit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which two structures, though not part of the basal nuclei proper, are functionally allied with them and essential to normal basal nuclei circuitry?","choices":{"A":"The hippocampus and the amygdala, both limbic structures","B":"The thalamus and the internal capsule, both projection related","C":"The cingulate gyrus and the fornix, both limbic pathways","D":"The subthalamic nucleus and the substantia nigra","E":"The cerebellum and the red nucleus, both motor coordination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus, located in the diencephalon, and the substantia nigra, located in the midbrain, are both functionally integrated into basal nuclei motor circuitry despite lying outside the telencephalon proper, making them essential partners of the caudate, putamen, and globus pallidus. The hippocampus and amygdala are limbic structures with memory and emotional roles, not basal ganglia motor circuitry partners. The thalamus and internal capsule are relay and projection structures adjacent to, but not functionally allied components of, the basal nuclei circuit itself. The cerebellum and red nucleus belong to a separate motor coordination system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Remembering that the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra are diencephalic and mesencephalic, respectively, yet still count as basal ganglia partners, resolves a frequent source of confusion. COMLEX: Both structures are common targets of deep brain stimulation for movement disorders precisely because of this functional integration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — the subthalamic nucleus and hemiballismus, already introduced from the diencephalic vantage point, now placed within the full basal nuclei circuit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":1,"stem":"Huntington disease classically produces loss of neurons in which specific basal nuclei structure, correlating with an enlarged anterior horn of the lateral ventricle on imaging?","choices":{"A":"The putamen","B":"The globus pallidus internus","C":"The head of the caudate nucleus","D":"The substantia nigra pars compacta","E":"The subthalamic nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Huntington disease preferentially destroys neurons in the head of the caudate nucleus, and because the caudate head bulges into the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle, its atrophy produces the characteristic imaging finding of ventricular enlargement, or \"boxcar\" ventricles, at that level. The putamen can be involved but the caudate head degeneration is the classic imaging correlate described. The globus pallidus internus is affected in some hyperkinetic and hypokinetic disorders but is not the defining Huntington finding. Substantia nigra pars compacta loss is the hallmark of Parkinson disease, not Huntington disease. Subthalamic nucleus destruction produces hemiballismus, a different disorder entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The caudate head atrophy and secondary ventricular enlargement pairing is one of the most reliably tested basal ganglia imaging findings. COMLEX: Huntington disease also causes progressive dementia and psychiatric symptoms, so caudate atrophy on imaging should prompt genetic counseling discussions for the patient's family.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — the subthalamic nucleus and hemiballismus, already introduced from the diencephalic vantage point, now placed within the full basal nuclei circuit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":1,"stem":"What is considered the primary and most critical function of the hippocampus within the limbic system?","choices":{"A":"Generating the primary subjective experience of fear and rage","B":"Consolidating short-term memories into long-term declarative memory","C":"Producing the initial autonomic response to painful stimuli","D":"Executing skilled procedural motor sequences automatically","E":"Relaying primary olfactory sensation directly to the thalamus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hippocampus decides which experiences are important enough to commit to permanent storage and drives the consolidation of short-term memory into long-term declarative, or conscious factual, memory. Generating the primary subjective experience of fear and rage is more characteristic of the amygdala. Initiating autonomic responses to pain involves the hypothalamus and amygdala rather than the hippocampus directly. Procedural motor sequence execution depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, which the hippocampus is notably not required for. Primary olfactory relay does not pass through the hippocampus at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bilateral hippocampal damage sparing procedural learning is a classic demonstration that declarative and procedural memory are anatomically dissociable systems. First Aid sums up the whole limbic system, hippocampus included, with the famous 5 F's: feeding, fleeing, fighting, feeling, and sex. COMLEX: This dissociation is clinically relevant in early Alzheimer disease, where hippocampal-dependent declarative memory fails long before procedural skills are lost.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — named cortical behavioral syndromes such as Gerstmann and neglect, extended here into the archicortical hippocampus and amygdala behavioral syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":1,"stem":"Specialized hippocampal neurons known as place cells are primarily responsible for which cognitive function?","choices":{"A":"Generating the sensation of taste through gustatory cortex input","B":"Coordinating fine motor sequencing for skilled hand movements","C":"Regulating circadian sleep-wake timing across each day","D":"Producing the perception of pitch from auditory cortex signals","E":"Constructing cognitive maps for spatial orientation and navigation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Place cells within the hippocampus fire selectively when an animal or person occupies a specific location in space, together building an internal cognitive map essential for spatial orientation and navigation. Taste perception depends on gustatory cortex in the insula and frontal operculum, unrelated to place cells. Fine motor sequencing is a basal ganglia and cerebellar function, not a hippocampal one. Circadian timing is governed by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus. Pitch perception arises from tonotopic organization within auditory cortex, not the hippocampus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Place cells were the discovery underlying a Nobel Prize-recognized model of how the brain encodes spatial memory, a favorite integrative Step 1 fact. COMLEX: Patients with hippocampal pathology, such as early Alzheimer disease, often first present with getting lost in familiar environments, reflecting early place cell network dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — named cortical behavioral syndromes such as Gerstmann and neglect, extended here into the archicortical hippocampus and amygdala behavioral syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":1,"stem":"Bilateral destruction of the amygdala and adjacent anterior temporal cortex classically produces which behavioral syndrome, marked by placidity, hyperorality, and hypersexuality?","choices":{"A":"Kluver-Bucy syndrome","B":"Gerstmann syndrome","C":"Anton-Babinski syndrome","D":"Balint-Holmes syndrome","E":"Capgras delusion syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kluver-Bucy syndrome results from bilateral amygdala and anterior temporal cortex destruction and produces profound placidity, hypermetamorphosis, hyperorality, severe visual agnosia, and hypersexuality, reflecting loss of the amygdala's normal aversion and threat-appraisal function. Gerstmann syndrome, from dominant angular gyrus damage, involves agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right disorientation instead. Anton syndrome describes cortical blindness with denial of blindness from bilateral occipital damage. Balint syndrome involves simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, and ocular apraxia from bilateral parieto-occipital damage. Capgras syndrome is a delusional misidentification disorder, not a classic bilateral temporal lobe lesion syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kluver-Bucy syndrome's placidity-plus-hyperorality-plus-hypersexuality triad is a favorite way to test bilateral amygdala and anterior temporal lobe destruction specifically. COMLEX: Herpes simplex encephalitis, which preferentially targets the medial temporal lobes, is a classic real-world cause of Kluver-Bucy-like features.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — named cortical behavioral syndromes such as Gerstmann and neglect, extended here into the archicortical hippocampus and amygdala behavioral syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is brought in by his wife because over the past year he has become impulsive, socially disinhibited, and indifferent to her feelings, and he can no longer plan or organize household tasks. Strength, sensation, language, and visual fields are normal. Which region is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"The angular gyrus of the dominant parietal lobe","B":"The nondominant inferior parietal lobule","C":"The prefrontal cortex of the frontal lobes","D":"The calcarine cortex of the occipital lobe","E":"The transverse temporal gyri of Heschl"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid lists frontal lobe syndrome from prefrontal cortex lesions as disinhibition, hyperphagia, impulsivity, loss of empathy, and impaired executive function, as seen in frontotemporal dementia, which fits this progressive change in behavior and planning with intact primary functions. The dominant angular gyrus produces Gerstmann syndrome with agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right confusion. The nondominant inferior parietal lobule produces hemispatial neglect of the contralateral world. Calcarine cortex damage causes a contralateral homonymous field defect, and his fields are normal. The transverse temporal gyri form primary auditory cortex, and a unilateral lesion there rarely produces a clear deficit because auditory input is bilateral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prefrontal cortex lesions give frontal lobe syndrome, including disinhibition, impulsivity, loss of empathy, and executive dysfunction, classically in frontotemporal dementia. COMLEX: Personality change with preserved strength, language, and fields should prompt frontal imaging and formal executive-function testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — anterior cingulate motivational circuitry and akinetic mutism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Fluent, melodic speech that is largely meaningless, accompanied by poor comprehension, localizes a lesion to which dominant hemisphere region?","choices":{"A":"The posterior superior temporal gyrus, forming Wernicke area","B":"The pars opercularis and pars triangularis, forming Broca area","C":"The precentral gyrus, housing primary motor cortex fibers","D":"The calcarine sulcus, housing primary visual cortex fibers","E":"The anterior paracentral gyrus, the medial motor cortex region"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fluent but meaningless, paraphasic speech with poor auditory comprehension is the hallmark of Wernicke aphasia, localizing to the posterior superior temporal gyrus, Brodmann area 22, of the dominant hemisphere, with the adjacent supramarginal and angular gyri often also implicated in the broader language network. Broca area lesions instead produce nonfluent speech with preserved comprehension. Primary motor cortex lesions cause weakness rather than a language comprehension deficit. Primary visual cortex lesions cause visual field loss, not a language deficit. The anterior paracentral gyrus governs lower extremity motor function on the medial surface, unrelated to language.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wernicke aphasia patients often seem unaware of their errors and speak in a confident, fluent \"word salad,\" a classically tested contrast with Broca aphasia's frustrated, halting speech; this is First Aid's own phrasing, Wernicke is a word salad and makes no sense. COMLEX: Because Wernicke area borders the auditory association cortex, comprehension of both spoken and often written language is impaired together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — the Broca/Wernicke/conduction/global/transcortical/anomic aphasia comparison, applied here as a lesion-localization tool","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A contralateral superior quadrantanopia, described by patients as a \"pie in the sky\" visual field defect, localizes a lesion to which structure?","choices":{"A":"The primary visual cortex along the calcarine sulcus","B":"The temporal lobe optic radiations, via the Meyer-Archambault loop","C":"The optic chiasm at the level of crossing nasal retinal fibers","D":"The parietal lobe optic radiations traveling more directly posteriorly","E":"The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The inferior optic radiation fibers, carrying information from the superior visual field, loop anteriorly through the temporal lobe as the Meyer-Archambault loop before turning posteriorly toward the occipital cortex, so temporal lobe lesions disrupting this loop selectively produce a contralateral superior quadrantanopia, the \"pie in the sky\" defect. A primary visual cortex lesion along the calcarine sulcus more typically produces a denser, more complete homonymous hemianopia rather than an isolated superior quadrant defect. Optic chiasm lesions classically produce bitemporal hemianopia, not a unilateral quadrant defect. Parietal lobe optic radiation lesions instead produce an inferior, not superior, quadrantanopia, the \"pie in the floor\" pattern. Lateral geniculate nucleus lesions typically produce a more complete contralateral hemianopia rather than an isolated quadrant defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The temporal \"pie in the sky\" versus parietal \"pie on the floor\" quadrantanopia distinction is a classic high-yield visual pathway localization pairing. COMLEX: Temporal lobectomy for refractory epilepsy carries a recognized risk of iatrogenic superior quadrantanopia from Meyer loop disruption.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — the Broca/Wernicke/conduction/global/transcortical/anomic aphasia comparison, applied here as a lesion-localization tool","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension develops sudden dense contralateral hemiplegia and hemianesthesia affecting the face, arm, and leg equally. He has no aphasia, no neglect, and no visual field cut, and imaging shows a small lacunar infarct in a deep white matter structure. Why can such a tiny lesion produce such a severe, complete deficit?","choices":{"A":"Small cortical lesions always cause more severe deficits than large ones do","B":"The corpus callosum concentrates all motor output before it reaches the cortex","C":"Association fiber bundles carry a disproportionate share of motor output","D":"The internal capsule packs nearly all fibers into one narrow anatomic bottleneck","E":"The lesion size on imaging always underestimates true infarct volume"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The internal capsule packs the great majority of corticospinal, corticobulbar, and thalamocortical fibers destined for the entire contralateral body into an extremely narrow, compact space between the thalamus and basal nuclei, so even a small lacunar infarct there can sever motor and sensory pathways as completely as a much larger cortical lesion would, while sparing the cortical association areas responsible for aphasia, neglect, and visual field integrity. Small cortical lesions do not inherently cause more severe deficits than large ones; severity instead depends on which fibers are involved. The corpus callosum connects the two hemispheres and does not concentrate motor output. Association fibers connect cortical regions within one hemisphere and are not the structures responsible for this dense contralateral deficit. Imaging appropriately reflects the anatomically small size of a lacunar infarct rather than systematically underestimating it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The internal capsule bottleneck concept, explaining why tiny lacunar strokes there produce deficits equal in severity to massive cortical strokes, is one of the most reliably tested localization principles. COMLEX: Lacunar strokes here are strongly associated with chronic hypertension and lipohyalinosis of small penetrating arteries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — the cortex-vs-internal-capsule-vs-thalamus-vs-brainstem-vs-cord localization ladder, applied here to explain the underlying anatomic reason a capsular lesion lacks the cortical signs a cortical lesion of similar clinical severity would show","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has a small subcortical infarct and on exam has moderate contralateral weakness of the leg and foot, mild weakness of the arm, and only trace facial weakness, without any cortical signs. Which somatotopic explanation best accounts for this graded pattern within a single deep white matter structure?","choices":{"A":"Leg motor fibers travel exclusively through the anterior limb of the internal capsule","B":"Leg fibers lie more posteriorly than face and arm fibers in the internal capsule","C":"Facial motor fibers are entirely absent from the internal capsule at any level","D":"The corpus callosum somatotopically separates leg from face and arm fibers","E":"Association fiber bundles graded by limb are located just lateral to the capsule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Within the posterior limb of the internal capsule, corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers are somatotopically arranged from anterior to posterior, with face and arm fibers positioned more anteriorly near the genu and leg fibers positioned more posteriorly, so a lesion weighted toward the posterior portion produces leg-predominant weakness with lesser face and arm involvement. Leg motor fibers travel through the posterior limb, not the anterior limb, which instead carries frontopontine and other non-corticospinal fibers. Facial motor fibers are present in the genu and anterior posterior limb region, not absent from the capsule altogether. The corpus callosum is a commissural bundle unrelated to this somatotopic gradient. Association fibers connect cortical gyri and are not responsible for this graded limb weakness pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior-to-posterior somatotopic gradient within the posterior limb of the internal capsule, face and arm anteriorly and leg posteriorly, is a frequently tested refinement of basic internal capsule anatomy. COMLEX: Recognizing a graded rather than uniform limb weakness pattern can help localize a capsular lesion to a specific sub-region even before imaging is obtained.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — internal capsule deficits contrasted broadly with cortical, thalamic, brainstem, and spinal cord patterns, now refined with the internal somatotopic gradient that explains graded rather than uniform limb involvement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman with refractory epilepsy undergoes surgical division of a large midline white matter bundle to prevent seizure spread between hemispheres. Postoperatively, when a comb is placed in her left hand out of view, she can use it appropriately but cannot name it aloud, though naming succeeds instantly when the comb is placed in her right hand. Which category of fibers was surgically divided to produce this split-brain phenomenon?","choices":{"A":"Commissural fibers, specifically the corpus callosum","B":"Association fibers, specifically the superior longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Projection fibers, specifically the internal capsule","D":"Projection fibers, specifically the thalamocortical radiations","E":"Association fibers, specifically the cingulum bundle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Corpus callosotomy severs the primary commissural bundle connecting the two hemispheres, so tactile information from the left hand, processed by the right, typically nondominant hemisphere, can no longer cross to reach the left, language-dominant hemisphere for naming, even though the intact right hemisphere can still direct correct nonverbal use of the object. Right hand information reaches the left hemisphere directly, allowing normal naming. Association fiber bundles like the superior longitudinal fasciculus or cingulum connect regions within one hemisphere and would not by themselves prevent this interhemispheric transfer. The internal capsule and thalamocortical radiations are projection fibers linking cortex to subcortical structures, not the two hemispheres to each other, so dividing them would not produce this specific disconnection pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The split-brain naming deficit after corpus callosotomy is a classic demonstration of hemispheric lateralization and interhemispheric transfer, frequently tested with a left-hand object identification vignette. COMLEX: Corpus callosotomy is reserved for drop-attack or generalized tonic-clonic seizures resistant to medication, since it is a palliative rather than curative procedure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — the corpus callosum as the largest telencephalic commissure connecting homologous cortical regions, now shown producing a specific disconnection deficit when surgically divided","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman dies after many years of progressive memory loss and cognitive decline. At autopsy, besides cortical atrophy, there is an especially noticeable loss of large neurons in a basal forebrain cell group lying internal to the anterior perforated substance, inferior to the anterior commissure. Which structure is this?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus accumbens of the ventral striatum","B":"The head of the caudate nucleus in the lateral ventricle","C":"The substantia innominata, or basal nucleus of Meynert","D":"The pars compacta of the substantia nigra","E":"The internal segment of the globus pallidus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The substantia innominata, the basal nucleus of Meynert or ventral pallidum, lies internal to the anterior perforated substance in the area inferior to the anterior commissure, and although many regions are affected in Alzheimer disease, there is an especially noticeable loss of its large neurons. The nucleus accumbens lies more rostrally, where the putamen is continuous with the head of the caudate, and forms the ventral striatum. The head of the caudate bulges into the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle and atrophies in Huntington disease. Loss of pigmented dopaminergic pars compacta neurons causes Parkinson disease. The internal pallidal segment is a basal nuclei output nucleus, not the cell group described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alzheimer disease features loss of large neurons in the basal nucleus of Meynert, the cholinergic basal forebrain, which is the rationale for acetylcholinesterase inhibitors. COMLEX: Distinguish basal forebrain loss in Alzheimer disease from caudate atrophy in Huntington disease and nigral loss in Parkinson disease when matching dementia to anatomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — subthalamic nucleus anatomy and hemiballismus from the diencephalic vantage point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension develops sudden, violent, unpredictable flailing movements of his left arm and leg after an acute vascular event. Imaging reveals a lesion in a small diencephalic nucleus. Which mechanism best explains why loss of this specific structure produces such dramatic hyperkinetic movements rather than weakness or rigidity?","choices":{"A":"Destruction of the subthalamic nucleus directly severs the corticospinal tract fibers","B":"Destruction of the subthalamic nucleus releases the thalamus from basal ganglia inhibition","C":"Destruction of the subthalamic nucleus abolishes dopaminergic input to the striatum","D":"Destruction of the subthalamic nucleus causes compensatory overgrowth of the caudate nucleus","E":"Destruction of the subthalamic nucleus interrupts cerebellar output through the superior peduncle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus normally exerts excitatory drive on the globus pallidus internus, which in turn inhibits the thalamus, so vascular destruction of the subthalamic nucleus removes this excitatory drive and effectively releases the thalamus from basal ganglia inhibition, producing the violent, unpredictable, contralateral flailing movements known as hemiballismus. The corticospinal tract runs through the internal capsule and brainstem, not through the subthalamic nucleus itself, so this is not a direct tract transection. Dopaminergic input to the striatum arises from the substantia nigra pars compacta, a separate structure whose loss instead produces Parkinsonism, not hemiballismus. There is no compensatory caudate overgrowth mechanism at play here. Cerebellar output through the superior cerebellar peduncle is an entirely separate motor coordination pathway unrelated to subthalamic nucleus function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemiballismus is the classic example of a disinhibition mechanism in basal ganglia physiology, distinguishing it conceptually from primary dopaminergic or striatal degeneration disorders; First Aid frames the same circuit with the mnemonic D1 receptor equals D1rect pathway while the indirect, D2, pathway is inhibitory. COMLEX: Hemiballismus typically improves gradually over weeks to months as the basal ganglia circuit partially compensates, an important prognostic point for patient counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — subthalamic nucleus anatomy and hemiballismus, revisited here with the specific disinhibition circuitry mechanism explaining why its loss produces hyperkinetic rather than hypokinetic movement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with several months of progressively worsening involuntary, jerky, dance-like movements of his limbs and trunk, along with irritability and cognitive decline. His father had a similar illness that began in his fifties and worsened, and his paternal grandfather reportedly developed symptoms even later in life. Which genetic and anatomic pattern best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Autosomal recessive point mutation causing progressive substantia nigra loss","B":"X-linked deletion mutation causing progressive subthalamic nucleus degeneration","C":"Mitochondrial inheritance causing progressive globus pallidus degeneration","D":"Autosomal dominant CAG repeat expansion causing progressive caudate degeneration","E":"Sporadic prion-mediated destruction of the internal capsule posterior limb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Huntington disease is caused by an autosomal dominant CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion that produces progressive degeneration beginning in the head of the caudate nucleus, and the earlier symptom onset in successive generations described here reflects genetic anticipation, a hallmark of trinucleotide repeat disorders, along with the characteristic chorea, psychiatric changes, and cognitive decline. Autosomal recessive substantia nigra degeneration does not describe Huntington disease and better fits some rarer parkinsonian syndromes instead. X-linked and mitochondrial inheritance patterns are not characteristic of Huntington disease. Sporadic prion disease, such as Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, produces rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus rather than this chorea-predominant, clearly familial pattern with anticipation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Genetic anticipation, earlier and often more severe onset in successive generations, is a hallmark feature of CAG repeat disorders including Huntington disease that is frequently tested alongside the caudate degeneration finding. COMLEX: Genetic counseling is essential for at-risk family members given the autosomal dominant inheritance and the availability of predictive genetic testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — basal ganglia circuitry from the subthalamic and hemiballismus angle, contrasted here with a striatal degenerative disorder that instead produces chorea through primary caudate nucleus loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes bilateral medial temporal lobe surgery for severe refractory epilepsy. Postoperatively he can hold a normal conversation, recognize old friends, and learn a new physical skill such as mirror tracing with practice, but he cannot recall meeting his care team five minutes after they leave the room. Which memory system is selectively impaired?","choices":{"A":"Declarative memory, from bilateral hippocampal damage, with procedural memory spared","B":"Procedural memory, from bilateral hippocampal damage, with declarative memory spared","C":"Both declarative and procedural memory equally, from bilateral hippocampal damage","D":"Declarative memory, from bilateral amygdala damage, with procedural memory spared","E":"Short-term working memory alone, from bilateral prefrontal cortex damage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bilateral hippocampal destruction produces profound anterograde amnesia for declarative, conscious, factual memory, leaving the patient unable to form new verbal or symbolic memories, while procedural, skill-based memory encoded by the basal ganglia and cerebellum remains intact, explaining why this patient can still learn a new motor skill like mirror tracing despite having no memory of learning it. Procedural memory being impaired while declarative memory is spared is the reverse of the actual pattern seen with hippocampal damage. Both memory systems are not equally affected, since the dissociation between them is the defining clinical feature. Isolated bilateral amygdala damage would primarily blunt emotional tagging and fear responses rather than produce this declarative memory-specific amnesia. Working memory deficits from prefrontal damage present differently, with impaired attention and planning rather than this classic anterograde amnesia pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The declarative-versus-procedural memory dissociation after bilateral hippocampal damage is one of the most famous findings in clinical neuroscience and a near-certain Step 1 topic. COMLEX: Preserved procedural learning in amnesia patients demonstrates that rehabilitation strategies emphasizing repetitive skill practice can still succeed even when explicit recall is impossible.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 8 — named cortical behavioral syndromes such as Gerstmann and neglect, extended here into the archicortical hippocampus, where bilateral damage produces a memory-specific rather than a language- or attention-specific syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has recurrent episodes in which she stares blankly, smacks her lips repeatedly, and picks at her clothing for about ninety seconds, followed by confusion before returning to baseline. MRI shows volume loss and scarring in the medial temporal lobe. Which pathologic process and resulting seizure category best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Occipital cortex gliosis causing focal seizures with simple visual hallucinations","B":"Frontal lobe cortical dysplasia causing generalized tonic-clonic convulsions","C":"Mesial temporal sclerosis causing focal onset impaired awareness seizures with automatisms","D":"Parietal lobe infarction causing focal sensory seizures with a Jacksonian march","E":"Cerebellar vermis atrophy causing episodic truncal ataxia without altered awareness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mesial temporal sclerosis, scarring of the hippocampus and adjacent medial temporal structures, is the most common cause of focal onset impaired awareness seizures, formerly called complex partial or psychomotor seizures, which classically feature impaired awareness with automatisms such as lip-smacking or picking, exactly as described. Occipital gliosis produces simple visual hallucinations such as flashing lights without this automatism-and-impaired-awareness pattern. Frontal cortical dysplasia can cause various seizure types but is not specifically tied to this automatism-predominant medial temporal picture, and generalized tonic-clonic convulsions look clinically distinct. Parietal lobe seizures classically produce a Jacksonian sensory march rather than automatisms and impaired awareness. Cerebellar pathology does not produce seizures or altered awareness at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mesial temporal sclerosis as the leading cause of automatism-featuring focal impaired awareness seizures is a near-certain Step 1 association worth memorizing precisely. COMLEX: Because CA1 and CA4 hippocampal subfields are especially vulnerable to seizure-related and ischemic injury, recurrent seizures can progressively worsen the underlying sclerosis in a self-perpetuating cycle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 8 — named cortical behavioral syndromes, extended here to the hippocampus's own primitive three-layered allocortex, which explains its distinct vulnerability to seizure generation compared with six-layered neocortex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man suffers bilateral damage to the anterior cingulate gyrus after a rare vascular event affecting both anterior cerebral artery territories. He now lies motionless and silent, tracking examiners with his eyes but making no spontaneous movement or speech, though he is clearly not comatose. Which circuit and resulting syndrome best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Disruption of the corticospinal tract bilaterally, producing locked-in syndrome","B":"Disruption of the reticular activating system, producing a persistent vegetative state","C":"Disruption of the basal ganglia direct pathway, producing bilateral parkinsonism","D":"Disruption of the optic radiations bilaterally, producing cortical blindness with confabulation","E":"Disruption of anterior cingulate motivational-drive circuitry, producing akinetic mutism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anterior cingulate gyrus is a key limbic-frontal hub for motivational drive and volitional initiation of movement and speech, and bilateral anterior cingulate destruction produces akinetic mutism, a state of profound immobility and silence with preserved wakefulness and eye tracking rather than true coma. Locked-in syndrome results from bilateral ventral pontine corticospinal and corticobulbar tract destruction, sparing consciousness and vertical eye movements but not from cingulate damage. A persistent vegetative state reflects widespread cortical or reticular activating system failure without the preserved purposeful eye tracking described here. Bilateral basal ganglia direct pathway disruption would produce parkinsonian rigidity and bradykinesia rather than this specific behavioral flattening. Bilateral optic radiation damage causes cortical blindness, often with confabulation as in Anton syndrome, not this global akinesia and mutism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Akinetic mutism from bilateral anterior cingulate damage is a distinct entity from locked-in syndrome and vegetative state, and distinguishing the three by preserved wakefulness, eye tracking, and volitional capacity is a classic Step 1 discrimination. COMLEX: Because patients with akinetic mutism retain some capacity for purposeful behavior, subtle bedside testing of eye tracking and response to command is important before assuming a worse prognosis than is warranted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 8 — named cortical behavioral syndromes, extended here into the cingulate gyrus's role as the Papez circuit's cortical integration hub for emotion and memory, whose bilateral loss produces this profound behavioral flattening","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old right-handed woman has normal strength and sensation in her right hand but cannot pantomime how to use a pair of scissors or brush her teeth when asked, despite understanding the instruction and having no difficulty recognizing the objects themselves. Which higher cortical deficit does this describe, and to which region does it localize?","choices":{"A":"Agnosia, localizing to sensory association cortices","B":"Apraxia, localizing to motor and parietal association cortices","C":"Aphasia, localizing to the inferior frontal or superior temporal gyrus","D":"Neglect, localizing to nondominant parietal association cortex","E":"Alexia, localizing to the dominant occipital lobe and splenium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An inability to perform complex, goal-directed learned movements despite normal strength, sensation, and comprehension defines apraxia, which localizes to motor and parietal association cortices responsible for planning and sequencing skilled movement, distinct from primary motor or sensory deficits. Agnosia describes a failure to recognize sensory input despite intact primary sensation, which this patient does not have since she recognizes the objects. Aphasia describes a language production or comprehension deficit, not a deficit of skilled movement execution despite intact language. Neglect describes failure to attend to one side of space or body, not a general inability to pantomime learned actions. Alexia describes a selective reading deficit, unrelated to this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing apraxia, an inability to execute learned skilled movement, from simple weakness or from agnosia is a classic higher cortical function discrimination tested with pantomime tasks. COMLEX: Ideomotor apraxia is frequently screened for at the bedside by asking a patient to pantomime using a common tool, exactly as in this vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — the Broca/Wernicke/conduction/global/transcortical/anomic aphasia comparison, contrasted here with apraxia, a distinct higher cortical sign that is not itself a language deficit though it is often tested alongside aphasia for localization purposes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man reports recurrent brief episodes of seeing simple, unformed flashes of light and colors in his left visual field, occurring in stereotyped bursts lasting under a minute, without any preceding complex visual scene or aura evolving over many minutes. Where does this seizure semiology most likely localize?","choices":{"A":"The right temporal lobe, given complex, formed visual hallucinations with emotional content","B":"The left frontal eye fields, given forced deviation of gaze away from the lesion","C":"The right parietal lobe, given a contralateral sensory Jacksonian march","D":"The right amygdala and uncus, given an aura of foul olfactory hallucination","E":"The right occipital cortex, given brief, simple, unformed visual hallucinations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Occipital lobe seizures classically produce brief, simple, unformed visual hallucinations such as flashing lights or colors in the field contralateral to the seizure focus, distinguishing them from the more complex, formed hallucinations and evolving auras characteristic of temporal lobe seizures or the slowly marching, evolving quality of a migraine aura. Temporal lobe seizures instead tend to produce complex, formed visual or auditory hallucinations often carrying emotional or memory-like content, a different semiology. Frontal eye field seizures typically cause forced head and eye deviation rather than isolated visual hallucinations. Parietal lobe seizures produce a somatosensory Jacksonian march, not visual phenomena. Amygdala and uncal seizures classically produce foul olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, the uncinate fit, not simple flashing visual phenomena.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple, unformed visual hallucinations point to occipital cortex, while complex, formed hallucinations point to temporal cortex, a classic seizure semiology localization pairing. COMLEX: Because the visual field affected is contralateral to the seizure focus, careful history-taking about laterality can help direct presurgical localization in refractory epilepsy evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — aphasia subtype localization by speech pattern, applied here as an analogous principle in which a different modality of higher cortical dysfunction, seizure-induced visual hallucination character, localizes a lesion just as precisely","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old right-handed man has a stroke and on exam cannot write a coherent sentence, cannot perform simple subtraction, cannot identify his fingers when touched with his eyes closed, and consistently confuses his left and right sides on command, despite fluent speech, intact comprehension, and normal strength. Which named syndrome and localization best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Gerstmann syndrome, localizing to the dominant angular gyrus","B":"Balint syndrome, localizing to bilateral parieto-occipital cortex","C":"Anton syndrome, localizing to bilateral occipital cortex","D":"Kluver-Bucy syndrome, localizing to bilateral amygdala and anterior temporal cortex","E":"Neglect syndrome, localizing to the nondominant parietal lobe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The tetrad of agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right disorientation, occurring together with preserved fluency, comprehension, and strength, defines Gerstmann syndrome and localizes to the dominant, typically left, angular gyrus. Balint syndrome instead involves simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, and ocular apraxia from bilateral parieto-occipital damage, a different symptom cluster entirely. Anton syndrome describes cortical blindness with denial of the deficit from bilateral occipital lesions, not this tetrad. Kluver-Bucy syndrome produces placidity, hyperorality, and hypersexuality from bilateral amygdala and anterior temporal damage, unrelated to this presentation. Neglect syndrome from nondominant parietal damage causes inattention to the contralateral side of space and body, not this specific dominant-hemisphere symbolic and calculation deficit tetrad.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Gerstmann syndrome tetrad, agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right disorientation, localizing to the dominant angular gyrus, is a frequently tested named syndrome requiring precise recall of all four features together. COMLEX: Because the angular gyrus sits at the parietotemporal-occipital junction near Wernicke area, partial or evolving Gerstmann features can sometimes accompany early receptive aphasia from an adjacent, expanding lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — the aphasia subtype comparison, contrasted here with Gerstmann syndrome, a non-aphasic dominant parietal lesion pattern that must be distinguished from Wernicke aphasia despite both localizing to the temporoparietal junction region","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":3,"stem":"Diffusion tractography in a healthy volunteer traces a long association bundle that runs within one hemisphere to interconnect the temporal and occipital lobes. Which bundle is this?","choices":{"A":"Uncinate fasciculus","B":"Inferior longitudinal fasciculus","C":"Cingulum bundle","D":"Forceps major","E":"Superior longitudinal fasciculus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The inferior longitudinal fasciculus is a long association bundle that provides temporal-occipital interconnections within the same hemisphere. The uncinate fasciculus is the association bundle for frontal-temporal interconnections. The cingulum lies internal to the cingulate gyrus and continues into the parahippocampal gyrus. The forceps major is commissural, carrying callosal fibers between the two occipital lobes through the splenium. The superior longitudinal fasciculus lies in the core of the hemisphere and interconnects frontal, parietal, and occipital cortices.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Long association bundles include the cingulum, superior and inferior longitudinal fasciculi, uncinate fasciculus, arcuate fasciculus, and inferior frontooccipital fasciculus; all stay within one hemisphere. COMLEX: Disconnection of association bundles explains syndromes such as conduction aphasia without any loss of primary motor or sensory function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — association, commissural, and projection white matter classification, tested here as a board-style fiber-identification vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":3,"stem":"A small lacunar infarct is confined to the anterior limb of the internal capsule, between the head of the caudate nucleus and the lenticular nucleus. Which fiber population is most directly interrupted?","choices":{"A":"Corticospinal fibers to the limbs","B":"Corticonuclear fibers to CN VII","C":"Optic radiations to the occipital lobe","D":"Auditory radiations to Heschl gyri","E":"Anterior thalamic radiation fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anterior limb of the internal capsule, insinuated between the head of the caudate and the lenticular nucleus, contains thalamocortical-corticothalamic fibers, the anterior thalamic radiations, that interconnect the dorsomedial and anterior thalamic nuclei with the frontal lobe and cingulate gyrus, along with frontopontine fibers. Corticospinal fibers occupy the caudal half of the posterior limb. Corticonuclear fibers to cranial nerve motor nuclei run in the genu. Optic radiations from the lateral geniculate body pass through the retrolenticular limb. Auditory radiations from the medial geniculate nucleus pass through the sublenticular limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior limb = anterior thalamic radiations and frontopontine fibers; genu = corticonuclear; posterior limb = corticospinal and central thalamic radiations. COMLEX: An anterior limb lacune causes little or no weakness, so a capsular stroke with dense hemiplegia points to the posterior limb.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — association, commissural, and projection white matter classification, tested here as a board-style fiber-identification vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Classify telencephalic white matter as association, commissural, or projection fibers and explain why the internal capsule is clinically important.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops dense contralateral face, arm, and leg weakness from a small lacunar infarct affecting a compact bundle of descending motor fibers situated between the thalamus and lentiform nucleus. Which internal capsule subdivision was most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"Genu","B":"Anterior limb","C":"Posterior limb","D":"Retrolenticular part","E":"External capsule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The posterior limb of the internal capsule, situated between the thalamus and lentiform nucleus, densely packs the corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers for the face, arm, and leg, so a small infarct here produces equally dense weakness across all three regions. The anterior limb instead carries frontopontine and thalamocortical fibers to prefrontal cortex, the genu carries corticobulbar fibers alone, the retrolenticular part carries optic radiation fibers, and the external capsule is a separate association fiber sheet lateral to the lentiform nucleus rather than a projection pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: posterior limb equals face/arm/leg corticospinal fibers, the single most commonly tested internal capsule subdivision for pure motor lacunar stroke. COMLEX: this compact bottleneck is why even a few millimeters of ischemia here produces such a severe, dense hemiparesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — association, commissural, and projection white matter classification, tested here as a board-style fiber-identification vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":3,"stem":"Loss of dopaminergic input to the striatum preferentially disinhibits the indirect pathway, increasing excitatory subthalamic drive and net inhibitory output to the thalamus. Which movement disorder results from this net physiologic effect?","choices":{"A":"Chorea with dystonic posturing","B":"Hemiballismus without rigidity","C":"Resting tremor without rigidity","D":"Bradykinesia and rigidity","E":"Repetitive involuntary motor tics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dopamine loss shifts the balance of the basal ganglia circuit toward the indirect pathway, increasing net inhibitory output from the globus pallidus internus to the thalamus and thereby suppressing movement, producing the bradykinesia and rigidity of Parkinson disease. Chorea and dystonia instead reflect excessive disinhibition of movement from striatal or pallidal dysfunction, hemiballismus alone reflects isolated subthalamic nucleus loss rather than global dopamine depletion, and resting tremor and tics are separate phenomena not directly explained by this specific net inhibitory shift, though resting tremor commonly co-occurs with the bradykinesia described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine loss equals more inhibition of the thalamus equals bradykinesia, the core physiologic logic behind Parkinson disease and the rationale for levodopa replacement. COMLEX: understanding this net inhibitory shift also explains why dopamine agonists and MAO-B inhibitors are mechanistically rational adjuncts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 6 — basal nuclei circuitry and its movement disorder correlations, tested here as a board-style mechanism vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":3,"stem":"Degeneration of GABAergic striatal neurons projecting to the external globus pallidus disinhibits that structure, ultimately increasing thalamocortical output. Which movement disorder results, classically accompanied by caudate atrophy on imaging?","choices":{"A":"Choreiform movement disorder","B":"Hemiballismus in one limb","C":"Resting tremor at baseline","D":"Widespread multifocal myoclonic jerks","E":"Bradykinesia and rigidity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of the striatal GABAergic neurons that normally inhibit the external globus pallidus disinhibits that structure, which then over-suppresses the subthalamic nucleus and ultimately increases excitatory thalamocortical output, producing the excessive, unwanted choreiform movements characteristic of Huntington disease and its accompanying caudate atrophy. Bradykinesia instead reflects excessive rather than reduced inhibition of the thalamus, hemiballismus reflects isolated subthalamic nucleus loss rather than striatal degeneration, and resting tremor and myoclonic jerks are distinct phenomena not produced by this specific striatal-to-external-pallidum circuit disruption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: striatal, especially caudate, degeneration with disinhibition of movement is the physiologic signature of Huntington disease chorea, the mirror image of the Parkinson disease mechanism. COMLEX: genetic counseling and CAG repeat testing are central to Huntington disease management given its autosomal dominant inheritance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 6 — basal nuclei circuitry and its movement disorder correlations, tested here as a board-style mechanism vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize basal nuclei anatomy and common clinical movement disorder correlations.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a small vascular lesion of the subthalamic nucleus develops violent, involuntary flinging movements of the contralateral limbs. Loss of excitatory drive through which basal ganglia pathway best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Indirect pathway","B":"Direct pathway","C":"Dentatothalamic pathway","D":"Nigrostriatal pathway","E":"Corticospinal pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus is the key excitatory relay of the indirect pathway, and its destruction removes this normal braking influence on movement, producing disinhibited, violent flinging movements, or hemiballismus. The direct pathway does not route through the subthalamic nucleus and instead facilitates movement directly, the dentatothalamic pathway carries cerebellar nuclear output to the ventral lateral thalamus and does not pass through the subthalamic nucleus, the nigrostriatal pathway carries dopamine rather than the glutamatergic subthalamic signal, and the corticospinal pathway is an unrelated descending motor tract rather than a basal ganglia circuit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemiballismus equals subthalamic nucleus equals indirect pathway loss, a tightly linked trio worth memorizing together. COMLEX: symptoms often improve spontaneously over weeks to months as the basal ganglia circuit partially compensates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 6 — basal nuclei circuitry and its movement disorder correlations, tested here as a board-style mechanism vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops profound anterograde amnesia after bilateral temporal lobe surgery removed structures deep to the parahippocampal gyrus that are essential for consolidating new declarative memories, while emotional responses and fear conditioning remain intact. Which structure was most likely resected?","choices":{"A":"Amygdala","B":"Cingulate gyrus","C":"Hippocampus","D":"Insular cortex","E":"Mammillary bodies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hippocampus is essential for consolidating new declarative memories into long-term storage, so its bilateral removal produces profound anterograde amnesia for facts and events while sparing emotional processing, which depends on the separate amygdala. The amygdala instead governs fear conditioning and emotional salience and would impair those functions if damaged rather than sparing them, the cingulate gyrus contributes to emotional and attentional processing, the insular cortex governs interoception and visceral sensation, and the mammillary bodies are a downstream relay of the memory circuit but are not themselves deep to the parahippocampal gyrus in the way the hippocampus is.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral hippocampal damage sparing emotional processing is the classic anterograde amnesia vignette, historically illustrated by the patient H.M. COMLEX: procedural and motor skill learning remain intact in this syndrome since they depend on the cerebellum and basal nuclei rather than the hippocampus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal function, tested here as a board-style memory/emotion/seizure discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops blunted fear responses and difficulty recognizing threatening facial expressions after bilateral temporal lobe damage, while memory for new facts and events remains fully intact. Which structure was most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Insular cortex","B":"Hippocampal formation","C":"Cingulate gyrus","D":"Parahippocampal gyrus","E":"Amygdaloid complex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The amygdala governs fear conditioning and the emotional salience of stimuli, including threat recognition from facial expressions, so its bilateral damage produces exactly this blunted fear response while sparing declarative memory, which depends on the separate hippocampus. The hippocampus instead governs new memory consolidation and would impair that function if damaged rather than sparing it, the cingulate gyrus contributes more broadly to emotional regulation and attention rather than this specific fear-recognition deficit, the parahippocampal gyrus supports memory encoding and scene recognition, and the insular cortex governs interoceptive and visceral processing rather than fear conditioning specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral amygdala damage sparing memory is the classic blunted-fear, impaired-threat-recognition vignette, the mirror image of the isolated hippocampal amnesia pattern. COMLEX: bilateral amygdala calcification is seen in Urbach-Wiethe disease, a rare cause of this exact clinical picture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal function, tested here as a board-style memory/emotion/seizure discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal regions to memory, emotion, seizure, and behavior.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has seizures beginning with a rising epigastric sensation, followed by lip-smacking automatisms and impaired awareness, without any preceding motor jerking. Which region is the most likely seizure focus?","choices":{"A":"Frontal lobe convexity","B":"Occipital lobe cortex","C":"Parietal lobe cortex","D":"Mesial temporal lobe","E":"Primary motor cortex strip"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An epigastric rising sensation followed by oral automatisms such as lip smacking and impaired awareness is the classic semiology of a mesial temporal lobe seizure arising from the hippocampus, amygdala, and adjacent structures. Frontal lobe seizures instead typically produce prominent motor activity such as bicycling movements or forced head/eye deviation, occipital lobe seizures produce visual phenomena such as flashing lights, parietal lobe seizures produce sensory symptoms such as tingling, and primary motor cortex seizures produce a Jacksonian motor march rather than this autonomic-and-automatism pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rising epigastric aura plus oral automatisms plus impaired awareness is the single most tested seizure semiology, localizing reliably to the mesial temporal lobe. COMLEX: mesial temporal sclerosis, often from prior febrile seizures, is the classic underlying pathology in medically refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 7 — hippocampus, amygdala, and cingulate/parahippocampal function, tested here as a board-style memory/emotion/seizure discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has fluent, grammatically intact but paraphasic speech, impaired repetition, and preserved comprehension. Which type of aphasia and lesion location best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Broca aphasia, inferior frontal lobe","B":"Wernicke aphasia, superior temporal lobe","C":"Global aphasia, large MCA territory","D":"Transcortical motor aphasia, watershed","E":"Conduction aphasia, arcuate fasciculus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conduction aphasia results from damage to the arcuate fasciculus, the association tract connecting Broca and Wernicke areas, producing this exact pattern of fluent, paraphasic speech with disproportionately impaired repetition despite preserved comprehension, since the two language areas can no longer communicate directly. Broca aphasia instead produces nonfluent, effortful speech with intact comprehension, Wernicke aphasia produces fluent but comprehension-impaired speech, global aphasia impairs fluency, comprehension, and repetition together from a large territory lesion, and transcortical motor aphasia spares repetition specifically, the opposite of what is described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: disproportionately impaired repetition with fluent speech and preserved comprehension is the signature of conduction aphasia, localizing to the arcuate fasciculus connecting Broca and Wernicke areas. COMLEX: testing repetition specifically is the fastest bedside way to distinguish conduction aphasia from the transcortical aphasias, which spare it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — aphasia, neglect, and higher cortical sign localization, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a large right parietal stroke fails to attend to or acknowledge stimuli on the left side of space, despite intact primary visual pathways and no visual field cut on formal perimetry. Which clinical sign and lesion side best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Right-sided neglect, dominant left parietal lobe","B":"Simultanagnosia, bilateral parieto-occipital cortex damage","C":"Left homonymous hemianopia, right occipital lobe","D":"Left-sided neglect, nondominant right parietal lobe","E":"Cortical blindness, bilateral occipital cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemispatial neglect for the left side of space, with intact primary visual pathways and no formal field cut, is the classic presentation of a nondominant, typically right, parietal lobe lesion, since the right parietal lobe uniquely attends to both sides of space while the left attends only to the right. Right-sided neglect from a dominant left parietal lesion is far less common and less severe because of this bilateral right-hemisphere attentional coverage, left homonymous hemianopia reflects a primary visual pathway lesion rather than an attentional deficit, simultanagnosia reflects bilateral parieto-occipital damage as part of Balint syndrome, and cortical blindness reflects bilateral occipital cortex destruction rather than a unilateral attentional deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neglect is classically left-sided and from a right, nondominant parietal lesion, since only the right hemisphere attends to both sides of space. COMLEX: neglect can be more functionally disabling than an equivalent visual field cut because the patient is often unaware of the deficit itself.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — aphasia, neglect, and higher cortical sign localization, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl28_bloom_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use aphasia, neglect, visual field loss, seizure semiology, and higher cortical signs to localize telencephalic lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"After a large left middle cerebral artery stroke, a right-handed man produces only sparse, effortful speech and cannot follow simple spoken commands or point to named objects. Which type of aphasia does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Broca aphasia","B":"Wernicke aphasia","C":"Global aphasia","D":"Conduction aphasia","E":"Anomic aphasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Global aphasia results when both Broca and Wernicke areas are affected, producing nonfluent speech together with impaired language comprehension, as after a large dominant middle cerebral artery infarct. Broca aphasia is nonfluent but with intact comprehension. Wernicke aphasia has impaired comprehension but fluent speech. Conduction aphasia, from arcuate fasciculus damage, impairs repetition while fluency and comprehension are relatively preserved. Anomic aphasia is fluent with good comprehension and mainly word-finding difficulty.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sort aphasias by fluency and comprehension: nonfluent with poor comprehension is global aphasia, involving both Broca and Wernicke areas. COMLEX: Global aphasia after a large left MCA stroke points to a proximal occlusion and a poorer prognosis for language recovery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — aphasia, neglect, and higher cortical sign localization, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":1,"stem":"The posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway carries which three submodalities of sensation from the body to the cerebral cortex?","choices":{"A":"Pain, temperature, and crude touch","B":"Discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception","C":"Taste, smell, and visceral nociceptive sensations","D":"Hearing, vision, and vestibular balance sensations","E":"Itch, tickle, and affective pleasant touch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The PCML pathway subserves discriminative touch, flutter-vibration, and conscious position sense, or proprioception and kinesthesia, using large-diameter fast-conducting fibers with limited convergence for high spatial and temporal resolution. Pain, temperature, and crude touch instead travel in the anterolateral system, a separate pathway. Taste, smell, and visceral nociception, hearing, vision, and vestibular sense, and itch, tickle, and affective touch are all carried by entirely different cranial nerve or special sensory systems unrelated to the PCML.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing exactly which submodalities travel in the PCML versus the anterolateral system is the foundation for predicting dissociated sensory loss patterns. COMLEX: This submodality split explains why a single spinal cord lesion can spare one sensory quality while abolishing another.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — the same DCML pathway revisited here at the spinal-cord comparison level, now traced in full from primary afferent to cortex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":1,"stem":"Information about limb position, joint angles, and muscle length and tension is used to regulate muscle tone, posture, and coordination without ever reaching conscious awareness. Which pathways carry this nonconscious proprioceptive information?","choices":{"A":"The posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway to SI","B":"The anterolateral system to the ventral posterolateral nucleus","C":"The trigeminothalamic tracts to the ventral posteromedial nucleus","D":"The spinocerebellar tracts to the cerebellar cortex","E":"The lateral corticospinal tract to the anterior horn"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Four spinocerebellar pathways transmit proprioceptive information, with limited cutaneous signals, to the cerebellum, including limb position, joint angles, and muscle tension and length, and this input guides cerebellar control of tone, movement, and posture without conscious perception. The posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway carries the conscious form of proprioception, along with discriminative touch and vibration, to the primary somatosensory cortex. The anterolateral system carries nociception, thermal sense, and nondiscriminative touch. The trigeminothalamic tracts relay sensation from the head to the ventral posteromedial nucleus. The lateral corticospinal tract is a descending motor pathway, not an ascending sensory one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conscious proprioception travels in the dorsal columns, while nonconscious proprioception travels in the spinocerebellar tracts; Friedreich ataxia degenerates both. COMLEX: Sensory ataxia from dorsal column loss worsens with eyes closed (positive Romberg), whereas spinocerebellar degeneration produces cerebellar ataxia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — the cerebellum as a coordination and error-correction system fed by spinocerebellar input","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":1,"stem":"Third-order PCML neurons in the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus project through which structure to reach the primary somatosensory cortex?","choices":{"A":"The anterior limb region of the internal capsule","B":"The genu region of the internal capsule","C":"The superior cerebellar peduncle tract","D":"The medial lemniscus tract","E":"The posterior limb of the internal capsule"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Third-order thalamocortical neurons in the VPL send axons through the posterior limb of the internal capsule to terminate in the primary somatosensory cortex of the postcentral and posterior paracentral gyri. The anterior limb carries frontopontine and thalamocortical fibers to the frontal lobe, not sensory fibers. The genu carries corticobulbar fibers. The superior cerebellar peduncle is the primary outflow tract of the cerebellum, unrelated to thalamocortical sensory projections. The medial lemniscus itself terminates in the thalamus and does not continue past it to the cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A stroke isolated to the posterior limb of the internal capsule can produce a pure sensory or pure motor lacunar syndrome depending on exactly which fibers are affected. COMLEX: This internal capsule bottleneck concentrates the entire body's discriminative sensory information into a very small, vascularly vulnerable space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — the same DCML pathway revisited here at the spinal-cord comparison level, now traced in full from primary afferent to cortex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cutaneous mechanoreceptor is a rapidly adapting receptor specialized for detecting deep vibration in the range of 60 to 300 Hz?","choices":{"A":"Pacinian corpuscle","B":"Merkel cell-neurite complex","C":"Ruffini corpuscle","D":"Free nerve ending","E":"Meissner corpuscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Pacinian corpuscle is a rapidly adapting, deep encapsulated receptor tuned to high-frequency vibration between 60 and 300 Hz, responding only to the onset and offset of a stimulus. The Merkel cell is a slowly adapting receptor signaling sustained pressure and fine texture, not vibration. The Ruffini corpuscle is a slowly adapting receptor detecting skin stretch. Free nerve endings mediate pain and temperature, not discriminative vibration. Meissner corpuscles are rapidly adapting but tuned to lower-frequency flutter around 5 to 40 Hz rather than high-frequency vibration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pairing each named receptor with its adaptation rate and frequency tuning is a frequent single-best-answer format on physiology exams. COMLEX: Testing vibration sense with a 128-Hz tuning fork at the bedside directly probes Pacinian corpuscle-dependent pathways.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — channel-based generation of the primary afferent action potential, now triggered by mechanically gated Piezo channels in cutaneous and proprioceptive receptors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":1,"stem":"Which proprioceptor is innervated by group Ib afferent fibers and signals muscle tension by monitoring force generated at the muscle-tendon junction?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear bag muscle spindle fiber","B":"Nuclear chain muscle spindle fiber","C":"Ruffini joint corpuscle","D":"Pacinian corpuscle","E":"Golgi tendon organ"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Golgi tendon organ, innervated by group Ib afferents, is positioned in series with extrafusal muscle fibers at the musculotendinous junction and signals muscle tension or force rather than length. Nuclear bag fibers, via group Ia afferents, signal dynamic length and velocity of stretch. Nuclear chain fibers, via group II afferents, signal static muscle length. Ruffini joint corpuscles signal joint position and movement rather than muscle tension. Pacinian corpuscles detect deep vibration, not tension at a tendon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The muscle spindle versus Golgi tendon organ distinction, length and velocity versus tension, is a classic proprioceptor pairing question. COMLEX: Golgi tendon organ activation underlies the protective inverse myotatic reflex that relaxes an overloaded muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — channel-based generation of the primary afferent action potential, now triggered by mechanically gated Piezo channels in cutaneous and proprioceptive receptors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":1,"stem":"Merkel cells are classified as slowly adapting receptors and are considered especially important for which specific tactile skill?","choices":{"A":"Detecting high-frequency vibration through a handheld tool","B":"Sensing gentle affective stroking of hairy skin","C":"Signaling limb position during passive joint movement","D":"Reading Braille through sustained fine pressure sensing","E":"Detecting rapid stretch velocity of an activated muscle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Merkel cells are slowly adapting receptors that remain active throughout sustained skin indentation, allowing them to encode fine spatial detail and stimulus duration, which makes them essential for tasks like reading Braille. High-frequency vibration through a tool is a Pacinian corpuscle function. Gentle affective stroking of hairy skin is mediated by low-threshold C tactile fibers, not Merkel cells. Signaling limb position during passive movement is a joint and muscle spindle proprioceptor function. Detecting rapid stretch velocity is the specialty of the nuclear bag muscle spindle fiber.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Merkel cells being slowly adapting is the key fact that explains their role in sustained fine-texture and pressure tasks like Braille reading. COMLEX: Merkel cell density is highest at the fingertips, correlating with the fingertip's disproportionately large cortical representation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — channel-based generation of the primary afferent action potential, now triggered by mechanically gated Piezo channels in cutaneous and proprioceptive receptors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"A myelinated peripheral motor fiber 2 to 8 micrometers in diameter conducts at 12 to 48 meters per second. Rather than driving extrafusal skeletal muscle, it ends on fibers inside the muscle spindle. To which fiber class does it belong?","choices":{"A":"A-beta","B":"A-delta","C":"C fiber","D":"B fiber","E":"A-gamma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Among motor fibers, A-gamma fibers are 2 to 8 micrometers in diameter, conduct at 12 to 48 meters per second, and innervate intrafusal muscle fibers, whereas A-alpha motor fibers (12 to 20 micrometers, 72 to 120 meters per second) innervate extrafusal skeletal muscle. A-beta fibers (6 to 12 micrometers, 35 to 75 meters per second) are sensory fibers to skin mechanoreceptors and secondary spindle endings. A-delta fibers (1 to 5 micrometers, 5 to 30 meters per second) are sensory fibers for thermal sensation and first pain. C fibers are unmyelinated (0.2 to 2 micrometers). B fibers (1 to 3 micrometers, 6 to 18 meters per second) are preganglionic autonomic fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The spindle has an afferent side (Ia and II) and an efferent side (A-gamma); gamma drive sets spindle sensitivity and contributes to spasticity after upper motor neuron lesions. COMLEX: Know the motor fiber row of the classification table as well as the sensory one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — gamma motor neurons and muscle spindle sensitivity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"Low-threshold C fibers in hairy skin respond best to gentle stroking rather than to painful or thermal stimuli. Which aspect of touch are these C tactile fibers thought to signal?","choices":{"A":"The affective component of gentle stroking touch","B":"Fine spatial detail for two-point discrimination","C":"High-frequency vibration from a tuning fork","D":"Static joint position with the limb at rest","E":"Sharp, well-localized first pain after injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low-threshold C tactile fibers in hairy skin respond to gentle stroking, a stimulus thought to signal the affective component of touch rather than its discriminative features. Fine spatial detail for two-point discrimination depends on densely packed receptors with small receptive fields, such as Meissner corpuscles and Merkel cells, carried by large myelinated fibers. High-frequency vibration of 60 to 300 Hz is signaled by Pacinian corpuscles. Static joint position depends on slowly adapting proprioceptors such as muscle spindles and Ruffini-type joint receptors. Sharp, well-localized first pain is carried by thinly myelinated A-delta fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Not every C fiber is a nociceptor; C fibers also carry thermal signals and, in hairy skin, gentle-stroke affective touch. COMLEX: Patients with selective large-fiber loss may still report vague, poorly localized touch through these preserved small fibers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 6 — the same Aα/Aδ/C peripheral fiber classification scheme, now expanded with the Aβ class and mapped onto specific PCML modalities","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"A-beta fibers, corresponding to class II afferents, are medium-diameter myelinated fibers that primarily supply which two structures?","choices":{"A":"Nociceptors and thermal free nerve endings","B":"Primary muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs","C":"Skin mechanoreceptors and secondary spindle endings","D":"Postganglionic autonomic effector organ synapses","E":"Preganglionic autonomic ganglion cell bodies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A-beta, or class II, fibers are 6 to 12 micrometers in diameter with conduction velocities of 35 to 75 meters per second, supplying skin mechanoreceptors for discriminative touch and secondary muscle spindle endings for static proprioceptive information. Nociceptors and thermal endings are supplied mainly by A-delta and C fibers. Primary muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs are supplied by the larger, faster A-alpha class instead. Postganglionic autonomic effectors are innervated by unmyelinated C fibers, and preganglionic autonomic ganglion cells are innervated by myelinated B fibers, neither of which is a somatic sensory class.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A-beta fibers are the workhorse of everyday discriminative touch, making them the fibers most relevant to two-point discrimination testing. COMLEX: A-beta fiber loss in diabetic neuropathy impairs vibration and light touch, which is why monofilament and vibration testing screen for it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 6 — the same Aα/Aδ/C peripheral fiber classification scheme, now expanded with the Aβ class and mapped onto specific PCML modalities","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the posterior columns of the spinal cord, fibers from the sacral, lumbar, and lower thoracic levels form which fasciculus, and in what position?","choices":{"A":"The cuneate fasciculus, positioned medially","B":"The gracile fasciculus, positioned laterally","C":"The cuneate fasciculus, positioned laterally","D":"The gracile fasciculus, positioned medially","E":"The spinocerebellar fasciculus, positioned centrally"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sacral fibers enter most medially, and progressively more rostral fibers up through T6 are added laterally to build the gracile fasciculus, which therefore carries lower extremity and lower trunk information in the most medial part of the posterior column. The cuneate fasciculus instead carries upper thoracic and cervical, or upper extremity, fibers and lies laterally, not medially. Reversing the fasciculus-to-position pairing or invoking a spinocerebellar fasciculus, which is not a posterior column structure, misassigns this somatotopy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Sacral is medial\" for the posterior columns is a high-yield mnemonic that also explains why sacral sparing patterns differ between intramedullary and extramedullary cord lesions. First Aid frames the same gracile-medial, cuneate-lateral split as grass on the ground, clouds in the sky at the dorsal column nuclei, and separately notes the dorsal columns are organized as you are, with arms outside and legs inside. COMLEX: This medial-lateral gradient is preserved all the way up into the gracile and cuneate nuclei of the medulla.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — thalamic nuclei and their sensory relay function, now focused specifically on VPL somatotopy within the PCML pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"As the medial lemniscus ascends from the medulla through the pons and midbrain, how does its somatotopic organization change?","choices":{"A":"It rotates so upper limb fibers lie medially and lower limb fibers lie laterally","B":"It stays vertically oriented and undergoes no rotation at any brainstem level","C":"It rotates the opposite way, moving lower extremity fibers medially","D":"It permanently splits apart into two separate, functionally unrelated fiber tracts","E":"It progressively loses all somatotopic organization above the medulla level"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the medulla, cuneate, upper extremity, fibers lie superior and gracile, lower extremity, fibers lie inferior within a vertically oriented medial lemniscus; as it ascends into the pons and midbrain, the tract rotates so that upper extremity fibers come to lie medially and lower extremity fibers laterally, a rotation driven partly by the appearance of medial structures like the red nucleus. It does not remain unrotated, and the rotation direction is not reversed toward a medial lower extremity. The tract does not split into separate unrelated bundles, and its somatotopy is preserved, not lost, all the way to the thalamus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This medulla-to-midbrain rotation is a favorite localizing detail, since a focal brainstem lesion's exact deficit pattern depends on which level and which rotated position is affected. COMLEX: The final medial-to-lateral thalamic arrangement this rotation produces directly sets up VPL somatotopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — thalamic nuclei and their sensory relay function, now focused specifically on VPL somatotopy within the PCML pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus, how is the body represented relative to the medial-lateral axis?","choices":{"A":"The upper extremity is represented laterally and the lower extremity medially","B":"The face is represented within the VPL nucleus alongside the trunk region","C":"The upper extremity is represented medially and the lower extremity laterally","D":"Both extremities are represented together in one identical central zone","E":"Representation within the VPL is not somatotopically organized"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Within the VPL, medial lemniscal fibers from the cuneate nucleus terminate medially and those from the gracile nucleus terminate laterally, so the upper extremity is represented medially and the lower extremity laterally, mirroring the arrangement established by the medial lemniscus rotation in the brainstem. Reversing this arrangement is incorrect. The face is represented in the separate ventral posteromedial nucleus, not within the VPL itself. The extremities occupy distinct, not identical, zones, and the VPL is in fact precisely somatotopically organized as a C-shaped lamina.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VPL somatotopy directly predicts which body part is affected by a thalamic lacunar stroke in thalamogeniculate artery territory. COMLEX: This same medial-upper, lateral-lower arrangement continues into the internal capsule and cortical projections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — thalamic nuclei and their sensory relay function, now focused specifically on VPL somatotopy within the PCML pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":1,"stem":"At which specific location does the PCML pathway decussate as it ascends from the spinal cord to the thalamus?","choices":{"A":"The anterior white commissure at the segmental level of entry","B":"The internal arcuate fibers, crossing at the sensory decussation","C":"The corpus callosum, crossing at the level of the thalamus","D":"The optic chiasm, crossing at the base of the diencephalon","E":"The pyramidal decussation at the medulla-spinal cord junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Second-order PCML axons, called internal arcuate fibers, sweep anteromedially and cross the midline at the sensory decussation in the caudal medulla before ascending as the medial lemniscus, meaning the pathway remains ipsilateral from the periphery up through the medulla and only becomes contralateral above this point. The anterior white commissure is where anterolateral system fibers cross at each spinal segment, an entirely different pathway and level. The corpus callosum interconnects the two hemispheres, not sensory relay nuclei. The optic chiasm belongs to the visual system. The pyramidal decussation is a separate, purely motor crossing point just caudal to the sensory decussation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The location of the sensory decussation is the single most important fact for predicting whether a given lesion produces ipsilateral or contralateral sensory loss. COMLEX: This decussation site sits immediately adjacent to but distinct from the motor pyramidal decussation, so the two can be dissociated by a small, precisely placed lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — predicting deficits from dorsal column lesions at the spinal-cord level, now extended to the full-pathway ipsilateral-versus-contralateral crossing rule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion of the posterior funiculus at the T8 spinal cord segment would be expected to produce PCML sensory loss on which side of the body?","choices":{"A":"The contralateral side, below the level of the lesion","B":"The ipsilateral side, above the level of the lesion","C":"Bilaterally, both above and below the lesion","D":"The ipsilateral side, below the level of the lesion","E":"The contralateral side, above the level of the lesion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the PCML pathway ascends ipsilaterally within the spinal cord and does not cross until the sensory decussation in the medulla, a lesion caudal to that decussation, such as one affecting the posterior funiculus at T8, produces ipsilateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception below the segmental level of injury. A contralateral pattern would instead indicate a lesion above the decussation. Sensory loss above the level of a cord lesion, or bilateral loss from a unilateral cord lesion, is not the expected pattern for an isolated posterior column injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Below the decussation, ipsilateral\" is the rule tested in Brown-Sequard vignettes, where PCML loss is ipsilateral while pain and temperature loss is contralateral. COMLEX: This same rule underlies why posterior spinal artery infarcts cause ipsilateral, not contralateral, position sense loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — predicting deficits from dorsal column lesions at the spinal-cord level, now extended to the full-pathway ipsilateral-versus-contralateral crossing rule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a small vascular lesion confined to the medial lemniscus in the rostral pons. On which side of the body would discriminative touch and proprioceptive loss be expected?","choices":{"A":"The ipsilateral side, since the lesion is caudal to the sensory decussation","B":"Bilaterally, since the medial lemniscus carries fibers from both sides","C":"The contralateral side, since the lesion is rostral to the decussation","D":"The ipsilateral face only, sparing the entire ipsilateral body","E":"Neither side, since the medial lemniscus carries no PCML fibers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The sensory decussation occurs in the caudal medulla, so any lesion at or above this point, including one in the pontine medial lemniscus, affects already-crossed fibers and produces contralateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception. Describing this pons lesion as caudal to the decussation is anatomically incorrect, since the pons lies rostral to the medulla. The medial lemniscus at this level carries only contralateral body fibers, not bilateral input, so bilateral loss would not occur from a unilateral lesion. Facial sensation travels separately via the trigeminal system and VPM, not the body medial lemniscus. The medial lemniscus is, by definition, the central continuation of the PCML pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Once a lesion is above the medullary sensory decussation, every subsequent structure, medial lemniscus, thalamus, internal capsule, or cortex, produces purely contralateral PCML loss. COMLEX: This rule allows precise brainstem-level localization purely from the laterality of the sensory exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — predicting deficits from dorsal column lesions at the spinal-cord level, now extended to the full-pathway ipsilateral-versus-contralateral crossing rule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":1,"stem":"Discriminative touch information from the face is relayed to second-order neurons in which trigeminal brainstem nucleus, the functional analog of the posterior column nuclei?","choices":{"A":"The spinal trigeminal nucleus, located caudally","B":"The trigeminal mesencephalic nucleus","C":"The trigeminal motor nucleus","D":"The principal, or chief, sensory nucleus","E":"The solitary nucleus of the medulla"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The principal sensory nucleus, located in the middle pons, receives large-diameter primary afferent fibers carrying discriminative touch and proprioception from the face and is considered the trigeminal homologue of the posterior column nuclei. The spinal trigeminal nucleus primarily processes pain, temperature, and crude touch from the face, analogous to the anterolateral system rather than the PCML. The trigeminal mesencephalic nucleus houses unique pseudounipolar proprioceptive cell bodies for jaw muscle spindles, not a relay nucleus for discriminative touch. The trigeminal motor nucleus is purely motor, innervating muscles of mastication. The solitary nucleus relays taste and visceral sensory information, unrelated to facial touch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Matching the principal sensory nucleus to the gracile and cuneate nuclei conceptually is the key insight for understanding facial discriminative touch. COMLEX: Lesions of the principal sensory nucleus can selectively impair fine facial touch while sparing facial pain and temperature sensation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — the general sensory functional component of the trigeminal nerve, now traced as its own discriminative touch pathway for the face","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":1,"stem":"Second-order neurons in the ventrolateral principal sensory nucleus give rise to axons that decussate and ascend as which tract to reach the thalamus?","choices":{"A":"The posterior, or dorsal, trigeminothalamic tract","B":"The spinothalamic tract carrying body pain","C":"The anterior, or ventral, trigeminothalamic tract","D":"The mesencephalic tract of the trigeminal nerve","E":"The medial lemniscus tract of the body"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Axons from the ventrolateral principal sensory nucleus cross the midline and ascend as the anterior trigeminothalamic tract, coursing close to the medial lemniscus before terminating in the contralateral VPM. The posterior trigeminothalamic tract instead arises from the dorsomedial division and ascends ipsilaterally without crossing. The spinothalamic tract carries body pain and temperature information, an entirely separate pathway. The mesencephalic tract carries proprioceptive afferents from jaw muscle spindles rather than discriminative touch relay fibers. The medial lemniscus itself carries body, not facial, PCML information and remains a distinct tract running alongside the trigeminothalamic pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dual crossed-and-uncrossed trigeminothalamic tracts, anterior crossed and posterior uncrossed, are a frequently missed anatomic detail on brainstem questions. COMLEX: Because both tracts converge on the VPM, a single thalamic lesion can abolish facial discriminative touch entirely regardless of which specific tract was affected more proximally.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — the general sensory functional component of the trigeminal nerve, now traced as its own discriminative touch pathway for the face","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":1,"stem":"Third-order trigeminothalamic neurons in the ventral posteromedial nucleus project to which region of the primary somatosensory cortex?","choices":{"A":"The posterior paracentral gyrus representing the leg","B":"The lateral-most face area of the postcentral gyrus","C":"The medial postcentral gyrus representing the trunk","D":"The secondary somatosensory cortex","E":"The frontal eye field anterior to the precentral gyrus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPM neurons project through the posterior limb of the internal capsule to the lateral-most portion of the postcentral gyrus, where the face has its largest cortical representation because of its high receptor density, mirroring how the VPL projects body information more medially. The posterior paracentral gyrus and medial postcentral gyrus represent the leg and trunk, which are VPL, not VPM, territory. Projection to SII exclusively ignores the primary SI projection that VPM neurons make first. The frontal eye field is a motor-related area anterior to the precentral gyrus, unrelated to somatosensory thalamocortical projections.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The face's large cortical representation, driven by high perioral and lip receptor density, is the classic explanation for the disproportionate size of the face on the sensory homunculus. COMLEX: This lateral face representation lies adjacent to the region supplied by the middle cerebral artery, explaining why MCA strokes affect face and hand sensation together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — the general sensory functional component of the trigeminal nerve, now traced as its own discriminative touch pathway for the face","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with long-standing untreated neurosyphilis develops a wide-based gait, absent muscle stretch reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense in both legs. This presentation is most consistent with degeneration of which structure?","choices":{"A":"The lateral corticospinal tracts, affected bilaterally","B":"The anterior horn cells of the lumbar spinal cord","C":"The spinothalamic tracts, affected bilaterally","D":"The cerebellar vermis and paravermal cortex","E":"The posterior columns bilaterally, as in tabes dorsalis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tabes dorsalis, a late manifestation of neurosyphilis, causes bilateral degeneration of the posterior columns, producing sensory ataxia from loss of proprioceptive feedback, absent stretch reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense, classically producing a wide-based, foot-slapping gait. Corticospinal tract degeneration would instead produce spastic weakness and hyperreflexia, not areflexia. Anterior horn cell loss produces lower motor neuron weakness and fasciculations, not a pure sensory syndrome. Spinothalamic tract involvement would impair pain and temperature rather than vibration and proprioception. Cerebellar vermis disease causes truncal ataxia without the sensory and reflex findings described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tabes dorsalis is a classic posterior-column-only syndrome pairing sensory ataxia with areflexia, a favorite Step 1 vignette. COMLEX: Argyll Robertson pupils, which accommodate but do not react to light, are a frequently paired finding with tabes dorsalis in neurosyphilis vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 Matching sensory deficits to dorsal column, cortical, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns, now applied to specific PCML-pathway disease states","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has a small cortical infarct in the postcentral gyrus of the left hemisphere. With eyes closed, she cannot distinguish sandpaper from silk or tell the size and shape of a key placed in her right hand, but pain, temperature, and joint position sense are intact. Which cytoarchitectural area is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Area 1 of the postcentral gyrus","B":"Area 3b of the postcentral gyrus","C":"Area 2 of the postcentral gyrus","D":"Area 4 of the precentral gyrus","E":"Area 3a in the depth of the central sulcus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Injury to area 3b has a more profound effect than damage to area 1 or 2 alone, producing deficits in both texture discrimination and size and shape discrimination, which suggests that area 3b performs the initial processing and distributes tactile information to areas 1 and 2. A lesion of area 1 impairs texture discrimination, with size and shape spared. A lesion of area 2 produces loss of size and shape discrimination, astereognosis, with texture spared. Area 4 is primary motor cortex, so its loss would cause weakness rather than a tactile deficit. Area 3a mainly receives muscle spindle proprioceptive input, so its loss would not explain the combined cutaneous deficits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary somatosensory cortex runs 3a, 3b, 1, 2 from anterior to posterior; 3b is the first cutaneous processing stage feeding 1 (texture) and 2 (size and shape). COMLEX: Most cortical strokes involve larger areas and produce global contralateral sensory loss, so isolated texture or shape deficits point to a very small lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — stereognosis and graphesthesia as bedside tests of cortical sensory integration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops persistent burning contralateral pain and paresthesias weeks after a thalamic stroke, occurring in a region that initially had numbness. This delayed pain syndrome is known by which name?","choices":{"A":"Dejerine-Roussy, or thalamic pain, syndrome","B":"Brown-Sequard hemicord injury syndrome","C":"Wallenberg lateral medullary infarct syndrome","D":"Anterior spinal artery occlusion syndrome","E":"Central cord contusion syndrome pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dejerine-Roussy syndrome, or thalamic pain syndrome, follows a thalamic stroke, most often involving the VPL or VPM, and produces delayed, often burning and treatment-resistant contralateral pain and paresthesias in a region that initially presented with numbness. Brown-Sequard syndrome results from hemicord injury producing ipsilateral PCML loss with contralateral pain and temperature loss, not a delayed central pain syndrome. Wallenberg syndrome is a lateral medullary infarct producing crossed sensory findings and cranial nerve deficits, not a thalamic pain syndrome. Anterior spinal artery syndrome spares the posterior columns while causing bilateral corticospinal and spinothalamic loss. Central cord syndrome classically causes cape-like upper extremity sensory loss and weakness from central gray matter injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thalamic pain syndrome is a classic example of central poststroke pain and is frequently paired with the phrase \"delayed onset\" in vignettes. COMLEX: This syndrome is notoriously resistant to standard analgesics, often requiring neuropathic pain agents instead.","crossRef":"📎 Matching sensory deficits to dorsal column, cortical, radicular, and peripheral nerve patterns, now applied to specific PCML-pathway disease states","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":1,"stem":"Which bedside sensory test is performed by applying an oscillating 128-Hz tuning fork to a bony prominence and asking the patient to report the buzzing sensation?","choices":{"A":"Two-point discrimination","B":"Graphesthesia testing","C":"Vibration testing","D":"Joint position testing","E":"Romberg testing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vibration is assessed by placing an oscillating 128-Hz tuning fork on a bony prominence, such as the malleolus or the interphalangeal joint, and asking the patient to report when the buzzing sensation is felt and when it stops, directly probing the Pacinian corpuscle-dependent PCML pathway. Two-point discrimination applies two simultaneous skin stimuli at variable distances rather than a tuning fork. Graphesthesia involves tracing numbers or letters on the skin. Joint position testing involves passively moving a digit without visual feedback. Romberg testing assesses standing balance with eyes open versus closed rather than a tuning fork sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 128-Hz tuning fork is the standard instrument for testing vibration sense and is a common bedside-exam image-based question. COMLEX: Loss of vibration sense at the toes before more proximal joints is an early, sensitive sign of a length-dependent peripheral neuropathy.","crossRef":"📎 The sensory system component of the complete neurologic examination, now detailed into specific PCML bedside maneuvers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician passively moves a patient's great toe up or down without allowing the patient to look, and asks the patient to state the direction of movement. Which sensory modality is being tested?","choices":{"A":"Joint position sense, or proprioception","B":"Two-point discrimination distance testing","C":"Graphesthesia traced-symbol identification","D":"Vibration sense tuning-fork testing","E":"Stereognosis object shape identification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Passively moving a digit without visual feedback and asking the patient to identify the direction of movement directly tests joint position sense, or proprioception, which relies on muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ, and joint receptor input relayed through the PCML pathway. Two-point discrimination instead uses two simultaneous skin contacts. Graphesthesia involves identifying traced numbers or letters on the skin surface. Vibration sense is tested with an oscillating tuning fork rather than passive joint movement. Stereognosis requires identifying a three-dimensional object placed in the hand, not passive joint movement of a digit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Impaired joint position sense at the great toe, tested with small-amplitude passive movements, is often the earliest bedside clue to posterior column disease. COMLEX: A positive Romberg sign frequently accompanies impaired joint position sense, since both reflect proprioceptive loss.","crossRef":"📎 The sensory system component of the complete neurologic examination, now detailed into specific PCML bedside maneuvers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient stands steadily with eyes open but sways markedly and nearly falls when asked to close the eyes during a standing balance test. This finding, a positive result on which test, indicates loss of which sensory input?","choices":{"A":"Finger-to-nose test, indicating loss of cerebellar input","B":"Romberg test, indicating loss of proprioceptive input","C":"Romberg test, indicating loss of corticospinal input","D":"Two-point discrimination, indicating loss of cutaneous input","E":"Graphesthesia test, indicating loss of cortical integration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Romberg test isolates proprioceptive contribution to balance by removing visual compensation; a patient who sways or falls only after closing the eyes has a positive Romberg sign, indicating that balance was being maintained through vision compensating for lost proprioceptive input from posterior column disease. The finger-to-nose test evaluates cerebellar coordination, not standing balance with eye closure. Corticospinal loss causes weakness and spasticity rather than imbalance unmasked only by eye closure; vestibular loss can also make the test positive, but its classic target is posterior column proprioception. Two-point discrimination and graphesthesia are separate cutaneous and cortical integration tests unrelated to standing balance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A positive Romberg sign localizes to a proprioceptive or posterior column deficit rather than to cerebellar disease, since cerebellar ataxia is present with eyes open as well. COMLEX: Distinguishing sensory ataxia, worsened by eye closure, from cerebellar ataxia, unaffected by eye closure, is a classic osteopathic and neurologic exam teaching point.","crossRef":"📎 The sensory system component of the complete neurologic examination, now detailed into specific PCML bedside maneuvers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man is asked to close his eyes and identify a set of keys placed in his hand. He correctly reports the size, shape, and texture within seconds. A neurologist explains that this ability, called stereognosis, depends primarily on the intact function of which pathway?","choices":{"A":"The anterolateral pain and temperature pathway","B":"The spinocerebellar proprioceptive pathways","C":"The posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway","D":"The corticospinal descending motor tract","E":"The vestibulospinal balance pathway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Stereognosis, the ability to identify a three-dimensional object by touch alone, depends on the PCML pathway's high-fidelity transmission of discriminative touch, texture, and joint position information from the hand to the somatosensory cortex, where areas 3b, 1, and 2 integrate this input. The anterolateral system carries pain, temperature, and crude touch, which cannot support fine object recognition. The spinocerebellar pathways relay proprioceptive information to the cerebellum for motor coordination, not to conscious cortical perception. The corticospinal tract is a purely descending motor pathway. The vestibulospinal pathway governs balance and posture, unrelated to hand-based object recognition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Astereognosis from a parietal lobe or PCML lesion is a classic finding used to localize sensory cortical or pathway dysfunction on Step 1 vignettes. COMLEX: Testing stereognosis at the bedside with common objects, like a key or coin, is a quick way to screen for PCML or parietal integrity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — the same DCML pathway compared at the spinal cord level against the anterolateral, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar tracts, now traced through its complete course to explain a specific cortical sensory skill","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a small pontine stroke has isolated loss of discriminative touch and vibration on the left side of her body, with fully preserved pain and temperature sensation on both sides. Which structure was most likely affected in isolation?","choices":{"A":"The spinothalamic tract","B":"The posterior columns of the spinal cord","C":"The dorsal root ganglion","D":"The trigeminal spinal tract","E":"The medial lemniscus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A pontine lesion causing isolated loss of discriminative touch and vibration with preserved pain and temperature localizes to the medial lemniscus, since it carries PCML fibers exclusively while running near, but anatomically separate from, the spinothalamic tract at that level, allowing a small vascular lesion to selectively spare one and affect the other. The spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature, which are preserved here, arguing against its involvement. The posterior columns are a spinal cord structure, not a pontine one. The dorsal root ganglion is a peripheral structure well below the level of a pontine stroke. The trigeminal spinal tract carries facial pain and temperature information, not body discriminative touch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A dissociated sensory loss sparing pain and temperature but abolishing discriminative touch and vibration is the hallmark of an isolated medial lemniscus lesion. COMLEX: This dissociation pattern is a favorite way to test whether students understand that the medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract are anatomically separate despite adjacent brainstem locations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — the DCML-versus-anterolateral comparison first drawn at the spinal cord level, now used to explain a dissociated brainstem sensory loss pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man reports difficulty telling the difference between a nickel and a dime by feel alone since a recent illness, although he can still feel light touch and pinprick normally. This deficit in fine two-dimensional shape and texture discrimination most directly implicates dysfunction of which pathway?","choices":{"A":"The anterolateral pain and temperature pathway","B":"The posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway","C":"The trigeminal motor pathway for mastication","D":"The olfactory pathway for smell perception","E":"The auditory pathway for sound perception"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fine coin discrimination by texture and edge detail requires the high spatial and temporal resolution of the PCML pathway, which underlies size, shape, and texture discrimination through its limited convergence and precise somatotopic organization, distinct from the coarser localization provided by simple light touch and pinprick alone. The anterolateral system supports pain, temperature, and only crude, poorly localized touch, insufficient for this level of tactile discrimination. The trigeminal motor pathway governs jaw muscle control, unrelated to hand sensation. The olfactory and auditory pathways are entirely separate special sensory systems with no role in tactile coin discrimination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Coin discrimination tasks in vignettes are a classic way to test understanding of the PCML pathway's role in fine discriminative touch beyond simple touch detection. COMLEX: This kind of deficit, preserved crude touch with lost fine discrimination, can be an early sign of a partial PCML lesion before other modalities are affected.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — the DCML pathway's role in discriminative touch, now applied to a specific everyday fine-discrimination task","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old violinist notices that her fingertips are remarkably sensitive to fine texture changes in the strings, allowing rapid, precise bow adjustments. This heightened tactile acuity is best explained by which anatomic feature of her fingertip receptors?","choices":{"A":"Low receptor density producing large receptive fields","B":"Predominance of slow-conducting C fibers at the fingertip","C":"Absence of encapsulated mechanoreceptors at the fingertip","D":"High receptor density producing small receptive fields","E":"Reduced central cortical representation of the fingertip"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fingertip skin has an especially high density of cutaneous mechanoreceptors, particularly Meissner and Merkel receptors, producing small receptive fields per afferent fiber and therefore high spatial resolution, which is why the fingertips excel at fine texture discrimination. Low receptor density with large receptive fields describes regions like the back, which have poor discriminative ability, the opposite of this scenario. Fingertip discriminative touch depends on large, fast Aβ fibers, not slow C fibers. Fingertips are rich in, not absent of, encapsulated receptors such as Meissner and Pacinian corpuscles. Far from reduced, the fingertip has one of the largest cortical representations in the somatosensory homunculus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The inverse relationship between receptive field size and cortical representation size is a core physiologic principle explaining why fingertips and lips dominate the sensory homunculus. COMLEX: This receptor-density principle also explains why the back is a poor site for testing fine two-point discrimination clinically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — channel-based generation of the primary afferent signal, now applied to why densely packed fingertip mechanoreceptors yield especially high spatial resolution","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with a rare inherited disorder has a complete loss of light touch and proprioception from birth despite normal pain, temperature, and motor function, and genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in a mechanically gated ion channel expressed in sensory neurons. Which channel is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Piezo2 mechanotransduction channel","B":"Voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.7","C":"TRPV1 capsaicin receptor","D":"Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor","E":"GABA-A receptor chloride channel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Piezo2 is the mechanically gated ion channel expressed in primary afferent fibers and Merkel cells that transduces touch and proprioceptive stimuli into depolarizing signals, and loss-of-function mutations produce profound touch and proprioceptive deficits while sparing pain, temperature, and motor pathways that depend on different channels. Nav1.7 loss-of-function mutations instead cause congenital insensitivity to pain, not touch loss. TRPV1 mediates capsaicin- and heat-induced nociception, unrelated to mechanotransduction. Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors mediate fast synaptic transmission at the neuromuscular junction and autonomic ganglia, not sensory transduction. GABA-A receptors mediate inhibitory synaptic transmission, not peripheral mechanotransduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Piezo2 is an emerging high-yield channel for questions on the molecular basis of touch and proprioception transduction. COMLEX: Recognizing Piezo2 loss as a cause of isolated touch and proprioceptive deficit helps distinguish channelopathies from acquired demyelinating or vascular sensory syndromes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — the channel-based framework for generating primary afferent signals, now extended specifically to the mechanically gated Piezo2 channel underlying touch and proprioceptive transduction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman with longstanding poorly controlled diabetes has reduced sensitivity to fine touch and vibration in her feet on examination, with relatively preserved pain and temperature sensation early in the disease course. This early pattern is best explained by preferential vulnerability of which class of receptor-associated afferents?","choices":{"A":"Small-diameter unmyelinated nociceptor afferent fibers","B":"Large-diameter myelinated mechanoreceptor and proprioceptor afferents","C":"Free nerve ending afferents carrying only thermal information","D":"Postganglionic sympathetic autonomic afferent fibers","E":"Preganglionic parasympathetic autonomic afferent fibers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Large-diameter myelinated afferents from cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors carry fine touch, vibration, and position sense, so their loss produces this pattern, while pain and temperature, carried by smaller A-delta and C fibers, remain relatively preserved. Diabetic neuropathy can begin with either large-fiber or small-fiber predominance; this patient's pattern localizes to the large fibers. Small unmyelinated nociceptor afferents and thermal free nerve endings carry the preserved modalities in this vignette, not the affected ones. Autonomic afferents, sympathetic or parasympathetic, relate to visceral and vasomotor function rather than the cutaneous sensory loss described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetic neuropathy vignettes testing early large-fiber-predominant loss reinforce the diameter-based vulnerability differences among peripheral nerve fiber classes. COMLEX: Monofilament and vibration testing are standard diabetic foot screening tools precisely because they probe these vulnerable large-fiber mechanoreceptor pathways.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — action potential generation across differently sized afferent fibers, now applied to why large mechanoreceptor and proprioceptor afferents are preferentially vulnerable early in diabetic neuropathy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"During a sensory nerve conduction study, a technician stimulates a cutaneous nerve at two sites 30 cm apart and records a response from the same afferent population. The latency at the distal site is 6 ms shorter than at the proximal site. To which fiber class does this conduction velocity correspond?","choices":{"A":"A-delta fibers","B":"C fibers","C":"B fibers","D":"A-beta fibers","E":"A-alpha fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Conduction velocity equals distance divided by the latency difference, so 0.30 m divided by 0.006 s gives 50 meters per second, which falls within the A-beta (group II) range of 35 to 75 meters per second for fibers 6 to 12 micrometers in diameter that supply skin mechanoreceptors. A-delta fibers conduct at 5 to 30 meters per second. C fibers conduct at 0.5 to 2 meters per second. B fibers are preganglionic autonomic fibers conducting at 6 to 18 meters per second. A-alpha fibers conduct at 80 to 120 meters per second and supply primary muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs, which a purely cutaneous nerve lacks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Velocity = distance / latency difference; memorize the ranges A-alpha 80-120, A-beta 35-75, A-delta 5-30, C 0.5-2 meters per second. COMLEX: Nerve conduction studies measure large myelinated fibers, so they detect demyelinating and large-fiber neuropathies but can miss small-fiber disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 6 — the peripheral nerve fiber classification scheme by diameter and conduction velocity, now applied to interpreting an actual compound action potential recording","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with Guillain-Barré syndrome has segmental demyelination of large myelinated sensory fibers in a peripheral nerve. Nerve conduction studies show markedly slowed conduction velocity in the affected segment. Which physiologic principle best explains why demyelination selectively slows conduction in these large fibers?","choices":{"A":"Loss of saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier","B":"Increased density of voltage-gated sodium channels along the axon","C":"Conversion of the fiber from myelinated to a faster unmyelinated state","D":"Selective loss of the fiber's mechanoreceptor connections","E":"Increased fiber diameter compensating for myelin loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myelin normally enables fast saltatory conduction by restricting depolarization to the nodes of Ranvier; demyelination disrupts this insulation, forcing slower, continuous conduction along the previously myelinated segment and markedly reducing conduction velocity, a change most dramatic in large myelinated fibers that depend heavily on saltatory conduction for their speed. Demyelination does not increase sodium channel density in a way that would speed conduction; in fact, channel distribution becomes disorganized. Fibers do not convert to an unmyelinated state that would somehow be faster, since unmyelinated C fibers are inherently the slowest class. Demyelination does not selectively remove mechanoreceptor connections, and fiber diameter does not increase to compensate for lost myelin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of saltatory conduction is the core mechanism tested whenever a vignette describes demyelinating disease causing conduction slowing rather than conduction block. COMLEX: The same loss of saltatory conduction in central white matter explains why multiple sclerosis disrupts myelin-dependent modalities such as vibration and proprioception.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 6 — the fiber diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity relationships, now applied to explaining demyelination's disproportionate effect on large myelinated fibers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoes a nerve conduction study of the sural nerve, a purely cutaneous sensory nerve. The study shows a compound action potential dominated by small-diameter fibers with an absence of the fastest A-alpha wave component seen in mixed nerves. Which explanation best accounts for this finding?","choices":{"A":"The sural nerve has been completely demyelinated along its course","B":"The sural nerve contains only preganglionic autonomic B fibers","C":"The sural nerve conducts exclusively at slow C fiber velocities","D":"The sural nerve lacks any myelinated fibers","E":"The sural nerve is cutaneous, lacking spindle and tendon organ afferents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sural nerve is a purely cutaneous sensory nerve and therefore does not contain the Ia and Ib muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ afferents that generate the A-alpha wave component in a mixed nerve like the sciatic nerve; it instead shows a compound action potential dominated by smaller A-beta, A-delta, and C fiber contributions from skin mechanoreceptors, thermal receptors, and nociceptors. This is a normal anatomic feature of a cutaneous nerve, not evidence of demyelination. It is not composed exclusively of autonomic B fibers, which are preganglionic and distinct from cutaneous sensory afferents. It does not conduct exclusively at C fiber velocities, since it still contains faster A-beta and A-delta fibers. It is not devoid of myelinated fibers, since A-beta and A-delta fibers are myelinated, just smaller than A-alpha.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that a purely cutaneous nerve normally lacks the A-alpha component is a classic \"normal variant, not pathology\" trap in nerve conduction study interpretation. COMLEX: Sural nerve conduction studies are a standard clinical tool for evaluating distal sensory peripheral neuropathies precisely because of this predictable fiber composition.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 6 — the fiber classification scheme's link between receptor type and fiber class, now applied to interpreting a purely cutaneous nerve's compound action potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a small ischemic stroke affecting the thalamogeniculate branch of the posterior cerebral artery involving the lateral portion of the ventral posterior nucleus. Which body region would be expected to show the most severe contralateral sensory loss?","choices":{"A":"The lower extremity, the laterally placed VPL","B":"The upper extremity, the medially placed VPL","C":"The face, represented in the separate VPM","D":"The trunk only, sparing both limbs","E":"No sensory loss, since this nucleus is purely motor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Within the VPL, gracile nucleus fibers carrying lower extremity information terminate laterally, so a lesion involving the lateral VPL preferentially affects lower extremity sensory representation on the contralateral side. The upper extremity is represented more medially within the VPL, so it would be relatively less affected by a purely lateral lesion. The face is represented in the separate VPM, not within the VPL at all. Sensory loss would not be confined to the trunk exclusively while sparing limbs, since trunk fibers occupy an intermediate position rather than being isolated from the lateral limb representation. The ventral posterior nucleus is a purely sensory relay, not a motor structure, so it does supply sensory, not motor, fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Predicting which limb is most affected from the exact thalamic subregion involved is a high-yield localization skill tested through vascular territory vignettes. COMLEX: Thalamogeniculate artery infarcts are a classic cause of pure sensory stroke syndromes affecting the VPL and VPM together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — thalamic nuclei and their sensory relay functions, now applied to predicting the exact body-part distribution of a focal VPL infarct","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has a small cortical infarct isolated to the medial aspect of the postcentral gyrus extending into the posterior paracentral gyrus. Which sensory deficit is most likely to result?","choices":{"A":"Loss of discriminative sensation in the contralateral face","B":"Loss of discriminative sensation in the contralateral hand","C":"Loss of discriminative sensation in the ipsilateral lower extremity","D":"Loss of discriminative sensation in the contralateral leg","E":"Complete bilateral loss of all sensory modalities"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The posterior paracentral gyrus, extending medially from the postcentral gyrus along the interhemispheric surface, represents the lower extremity in the cortical homunculus, so a lesion here produces contralateral lower extremity sensory loss, consistent with anterior cerebral artery territory involvement. The face and hand are represented laterally on the convexity of the postcentral gyrus, not medially, so they would be relatively spared by a purely medial lesion. Cortical sensory deficits from a unilateral lesion are contralateral, not ipsilateral, given that the pathway has already crossed at the medullary decussation. A small, isolated cortical lesion would not be expected to cause complete bilateral loss of all modalities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The medial-to-lateral leg-to-face-and-hand progression along the postcentral gyrus is essential for predicting deficits from focal cortical lesions and for correlating them with ACA versus MCA vascular territory. COMLEX: This same medial leg representation explains the classic ACA-territory stroke pattern of leg-predominant sensorimotor loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — thalamic somatotopy carried forward into its cortical projection target, now applied to a focal postcentral gyrus lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has a penetrating injury isolated to the superior portion of the medial lemniscus at the level of the medulla. Which body region's discriminative sensation is most likely to be preferentially impaired on the contralateral side?","choices":{"A":"The lower extremity, the inferior gracile fibers","B":"The face, carried by the separate trigeminal pathway","C":"The upper extremity, the superior cuneate fibers","D":"The trunk only, an intermediate fiber position","E":"No region, since fibers are not yet organized at this level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At the medullary level, medial lemniscal fibers from the cuneate nucleus, carrying upper extremity information, are located superiorly, while fibers from the gracile nucleus, carrying lower extremity information, lie inferiorly, so a lesion restricted to the superior medial lemniscus at this level preferentially affects contralateral upper extremity discriminative sensation. The lower extremity would be relatively spared, since its fibers occupy the inferior portion instead. The face is not represented in the medial lemniscus at all, traveling instead via the separate trigeminothalamic tracts. Trunk fibers occupy an intermediate position, not the exclusive focus of a purely superior lesion. The medial lemniscus is, in fact, already precisely somatotopically organized at the medullary level, which is exactly why this selective deficit pattern occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superior versus inferior medial lemniscus somatotopy at the medullary level is a fine anatomic detail that distinguishes a well-prepared answer from a merely adequate one. COMLEX: This vertical somatotopy at the medulla later becomes a medial-lateral arrangement more rostrally, so the exact brainstem level of a lesion changes which orientation applies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — thalamic and pathway-level sensory somatotopy, now traced back to its medullary origin to explain a level-specific dissociated limb deficit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man sustains a stab wound causing a right-sided hemisection of the spinal cord at T10. On examination three days later, which sensory pattern would best confirm this level of injury?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral loss of vibration and proprioception, ipsilateral pain and temperature loss","B":"Ipsilateral loss of vibration and proprioception, contralateral pain and temperature loss","C":"Bilateral loss of all sensory modalities, both proprioceptive and pain-related","D":"Ipsilateral loss of all sensory modalities, both proprioceptive and pain-related","E":"Contralateral loss of all sensory modalities, both proprioceptive and pain-related"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In Brown-Sequard syndrome from cord hemisection, the PCML pathway has not yet crossed at the spinal cord level, so vibration and proprioceptive loss occurs ipsilaterally below the lesion, while the anterolateral system has already crossed at each segmental level via the anterior white commissure, producing contralateral pain and temperature loss below the lesion, a dissociated pattern unique to hemicord injury. Reversing this pattern, contralateral PCML loss with ipsilateral pain loss, does not match the differing crossing levels of the two pathways. Bilateral loss of all modalities would suggest a complete cord transection rather than a hemisection. Ipsilateral loss of all modalities ignores that the anterolateral system has already crossed before reaching this level. Contralateral loss of all modalities similarly ignores the ipsilateral PCML pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brown-Sequard syndrome's dissociated ipsilateral PCML and contralateral pain-temperature pattern is one of the most heavily tested spinal cord syndromes on Step 1. COMLEX: This same dissociation is the key teaching point for why hemicord lesions produce a mixed, rather than uniform, sensory examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — predicting deficits from a dorsal column lesion at the spinal cord level, now extended to the full ipsilateral-versus-contralateral crossing-level comparison in Brown-Sequard syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman presents with sudden-onset numbness affecting the left side of her face, arm, and leg together, along with mild left-sided weakness. Imaging shows a small lacunar infarct in the right internal capsule. Given that this lesion is well above the sensory decussation, which laterality pattern is expected, and why?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral loss, since the capsule lies caudal to the decussation","B":"Bilateral loss, since capsule fibers represent both body sides","C":"No loss expected, since the capsule carries only motor fibers","D":"Ipsilateral face, contralateral limb loss, a split pattern","E":"Contralateral loss, since fibers already crossed at the medulla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the sensory decussation occurs in the caudal medulla, any lesion above that point, including the internal capsule, involves fibers that have already crossed, producing purely contralateral sensory loss, matching this patient's left-sided deficit from a right internal capsule lesion. Describing the internal capsule as caudal to the decussation is anatomically backward, since it lies well rostral to the medulla. Internal capsule fibers on one side represent only the contralateral body, not both sides, so bilateral loss would not occur from a unilateral lesion. The posterior limb of the internal capsule carries thalamocortical sensory fibers in addition to corticospinal motor fibers, so sensory loss is expected. A split ipsilateral-face, contralateral-limb pattern is not a recognized internal capsule syndrome, since both VPL and VPM projections have already crossed by this level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Confirming that a lesion's laterality of deficit correctly matches its position relative to the sensory decussation is a key integrative skill for lacunar stroke localization. COMLEX: Pure sensory lacunar stroke is a well-described posterior limb internal capsule or thalamic lacunar syndrome, useful for distinguishing small-vessel from large-vessel stroke presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — predicting deficits from spinal-level lesions relative to a crossing point, now applied to confirming the contralateral pattern expected from a supratentorial internal capsule lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man develops sudden left-sided loss of vibration, proprioception, and discriminative touch over the entire body, sparing the face, after an infarct of the left dorsal caudal medulla involving the gracile and cuneate nuclei, caudal to the sensory decussation. Which explanation best accounts for the ipsilateral, rather than contralateral, pattern?","choices":{"A":"The lesion involves the medial lemniscus, already crossed","B":"The lesion involves the VPL of the thalamus, well rostral","C":"The lesion is caudal to the decussation, not yet crossed","D":"The lesion involves the postcentral gyrus, well rostral","E":"Medullary lesions always produce contralateral findings"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A lesion caudal to the sensory decussation, such as one affecting the gracile and cuneate nuclei or the posterior columns before the internal arcuate fibers cross, produces ipsilateral sensory loss, because the affected fibers have not yet decussated; this explains why this patient's left-sided medullary lesion produced deficits on the same, left, side, whereas a lesion truly rostral to the decussation would instead produce a contralateral, right-sided deficit. Involvement of the medial lemniscus after crossing would instead produce a contralateral pattern, the opposite of what is described. The VPL and postcentral gyrus are both well rostral to the medullary decussation entirely and would also produce contralateral, not ipsilateral, deficits. Medullary lesions do not always produce contralateral findings; the outcome depends entirely on whether the lesion is caudal or rostral to the sensory decussation within the medulla itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medullary lesions are a classic trap because the medulla contains both pre-decussation and post-decussation PCML fibers, so laterality depends on the precise level within the medulla, not just \"medulla versus not medulla.\" COMLEX: This precise sub-medullary localization is why detailed brainstem anatomy knowledge, not just general regions, matters for board-style localization questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — spinal-level lesion prediction, now extended to the more nuanced sub-medullary distinction between pre- and post-decussation PCML lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has a brainstem lesion producing contralateral loss of discriminative touch over the body but fully preserved discriminative touch over the face. Which brainstem structure was most likely spared, explaining the preserved facial sensation?","choices":{"A":"The principal sensory nucleus and trigeminothalamic tract","B":"The gracile and cuneate nuclei of the caudal medulla","C":"The medial lemniscus carrying body PCML information","D":"The internal arcuate fibers crossing at the decussation","E":"The sensory decussation point of the body PCML pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Facial discriminative touch depends on a parallel but anatomically distinct trigeminal pathway, relayed through the principal sensory nucleus and its trigeminothalamic projections to the VPM, so if this structure and its output remain intact, facial sensation is preserved even when the body's medial lemniscus is damaged, since the two pathways run separately through the brainstem despite both converging on the thalamus. The gracile and cuneate nuclei, medial lemniscus, internal arcuate fibers, and body sensory decussation are all components of the body PCML pathway, and their involvement would explain the body deficit described but do not account for why the face is spared, since sparing requires that the separate facial pathway remain untouched.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that face and body discriminative touch travel via anatomically separate, only partially overlapping brainstem pathways explains many face-sparing or body-sparing brainstem stroke patterns. COMLEX: This anatomic separation is why a single small brainstem lesion can selectively affect body or face sensation without necessarily affecting both together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — the general sensory functional component of the trigeminal nerve, now applied to explaining a face-sparing pattern in a body-affecting brainstem lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has facial discriminative touch loss on the left side after a pontine lesion. A resident notes that, unlike body PCML fibers, some facial trigeminothalamic fibers ascend without ever crossing the midline. Which projection best illustrates this uncrossed exception?","choices":{"A":"The anterior trigeminothalamic tract, arising from the ventrolateral principal sensory nucleus and always crossing","B":"The medial lemniscus, arising from the gracile and cuneate nuclei and always crossing","C":"The spinothalamic tract, arising from posterior horn neurons and always crossing","D":"The posterior trigeminothalamic tract, from the dorsomedial nucleus, ascending uncrossed","E":"The corticospinal tract, arising from motor cortex and always crossing at the pyramids"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The posterior, or dorsal, trigeminothalamic tract arises from the dorsomedial division of the principal sensory nucleus and ascends ipsilaterally to the VPM without crossing, in contrast to the anterior trigeminothalamic tract, which does cross, making this uncrossed pathway the key exception that distinguishes trigeminal touch organization from the exclusively crossed body PCML system. The anterior trigeminothalamic tract, medial lemniscus, and spinothalamic tract all cross the midline and therefore do not illustrate an uncrossed exception. The corticospinal tract is a descending motor pathway, unrelated to ascending sensory organization, and largely crosses at the pyramidal decussation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The existence of an uncrossed posterior trigeminothalamic tract is a subtle but testable exception to the general rule that discriminative touch pathways always cross before reaching the thalamus. COMLEX: This partial ipsilateral component can complicate the expected laterality of facial sensory findings after a pontine lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — trigeminal functional component organization, now extended to the specific crossed-versus-uncrossed anatomy that distinguishes it from the body PCML pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a tiny thalamic infarct. He now has impaired discriminative touch and two-point discrimination on the tongue and inner cheeks, while discriminative touch over the external face, arm, and leg is preserved. Which part of the thalamus was most likely infarcted?","choices":{"A":"The lateral part of the ventral posterolateral nucleus","B":"The medial part of the ventral posterolateral nucleus","C":"The medial part of the ventral posteromedial nucleus","D":"The pulvinar and lateral posterior nuclear group","E":"The ventral posterior inferior nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Projections from the principal trigeminal sensory nucleus terminate somatotopically within the ventral posteromedial nucleus so that the oral cavity is represented medially and the external facial structures more laterally, so a small medial lesion selectively impairs intraoral discriminative touch. The lateral part of the ventral posterolateral nucleus represents the lower extremity and the medial part represents the upper extremity, both preserved. The pulvinar and lateral posterior group receive some lemniscal input but project to parietal association areas 5 and 7 rather than serving as the primary intraoral relay. The ventral posterior inferior nucleus projects to secondary somatosensory cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thalamic somatotopy runs from lateral to medial: leg, arm (VPL), external face, then oral cavity (VPM). COMLEX: Intraoral numbness with a normal external face can be thalamic, so do not assume a dental or peripheral trigeminal cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — trigeminal general sensory and proprioceptive components","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with poorly controlled diabetes reports numbness that began in both feet and has progressively ascended to just below her knees, now also beginning to affect her fingertips, in a symmetric pattern. Which clinical pattern best describes this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A single dermatomal radiculopathy pattern","B":"An asymmetric mononeuropathy of one peripheral nerve","C":"A sharply demarcated spinal cord sensory level finding","D":"A hemibody cortical sensory deficit pattern","E":"A distal-to-proximal stocking-glove neuropathy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Diabetic peripheral neuropathy classically produces a symmetric, length-dependent, distal-to-proximal dying-back pattern, beginning in the feet and ascending, with the hands becoming involved once leg symptoms reach roughly knee level, producing the characteristic stocking-glove distribution. A single dermatomal radiculopathy would produce a band-like deficit in one nerve root's territory rather than a symmetric distal ascending pattern. An asymmetric mononeuropathy would affect only one specific peripheral nerve's territory, not both feet symmetrically. A sharply demarcated spinal cord sensory level implies a discrete transverse lesion, not the gradual, symmetric peripheral gradient described. A hemibody cortical deficit would affect one entire side of the body from a single hemisphere lesion, not a distal, symmetric, bilateral pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stocking-glove pattern is the classic description tested for length-dependent peripheral neuropathies such as diabetic and chemotherapy-induced neuropathy. COMLEX: Recognizing this distal-symmetric pattern clinically helps distinguish peripheral neuropathy from central causes of numbness, which is an important gait and fall-risk consideration in elderly patients.","crossRef":"📖 Matching sensory deficit patterns to dorsal column, cortical, radicular, and peripheral nerve categories, now applied to confirming a stocking-glove peripheral neuropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man presents with sudden contralateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception affecting the face, arm, and leg equally and simultaneously, without any cranial nerve findings. Imaging shows a small lacunar infarct. Which level of the neuraxis best explains this equal, combined pattern?","choices":{"A":"The brainstem, since cranial nerve findings would be expected","B":"The spinal cord, since a sensory level would be expected","C":"The thalamus or internal capsule, since no cranial nerves are involved","D":"A single peripheral nerve, since one territory would be affected","E":"The cerebellum, since ataxia rather than sensory loss would result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because thalamic and internal capsule sensory fibers for the face, arm, and leg have already converged from their separate VPL and VPM origins into a compact, unified bundle, a small lacunar lesion here can affect all three body regions equally and simultaneously without any cranial nerve involvement, unlike a brainstem lesion, which would be expected to also affect a cranial nerve given the close proximity of cranial nerve nuclei. A spinal cord lesion would instead produce a discrete sensory level sparing the head. A single peripheral nerve lesion would affect only its own specific territory, not the face, arm, and leg together. The cerebellum governs coordination rather than discriminative sensory perception, so a cerebellar lesion would produce ataxia, not this sensory pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Equal simultaneous face-arm-leg sensory loss without cranial nerve findings is the classic localizing clue that points above the brainstem, to the thalamus or internal capsule. COMLEX: This localization ladder, using the presence or absence of cranial nerve findings to place a lesion above or within the brainstem, is a core lesion-localization strategy.","crossRef":"📖 Sensory deficit pattern matching, now applied to using the presence or absence of cranial nerve findings to localize a lesion to the thalamus or internal capsule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a stroke and, on examination, has lost the ability to recognize her own contralateral arm as part of her body despite intact strength and only mildly reduced basic touch sensation in that limb. Which cortical region is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"The primary somatosensory cortex, areas 3b and 1","B":"The parietal association cortex, areas 5 and 7","C":"The primary motor cortex, area 4","D":"The frontal eye field region","E":"The primary auditory cortex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lesions in the parietal association cortex, areas 5 and 7, which integrate somatosensory input beyond the primary sensory areas, can produce a personal body-map agnosia in which a limb is no longer recognized as belonging to the patient despite relatively preserved basic sensation and strength, distinct from a simple loss of touch sensitivity. Isolated primary somatosensory cortex, areas 3b and 1, damage alone would more typically cause reduced texture or shape discrimination rather than a failure to recognize the limb as one's own. The primary motor cortex governs voluntary movement, and damage there would cause weakness, not this sensory body-map disturbance. The frontal eye field governs voluntary eye movements. The primary auditory cortex processes sound, unrelated to this somatosensory body-ownership deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Parietal association cortex lesions producing body-part neglect or agnosia despite preserved primary sensory function highlight the hierarchical organization of somatosensory processing. COMLEX: This kind of higher-order sensory integration deficit is an important, often underrecognized, sign of non-dominant parietal lobe stroke.","crossRef":"📖 Matching sensory deficits including cortical patterns to specific disease correlates, now applied to a parietal association cortex body-agnosia presentation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with suspected posterior column disease is examined at the bedside. He can identify a key placed in his hand and can feel vibration normally at his ankles, but he cannot correctly identify the number '7' traced on his palm with a blunt object. Which specific test is abnormal, and what does it assess?","choices":{"A":"Stereognosis, the ability to identify a three-dimensional object by touch","B":"Vibration testing using a 128-Hz tuning fork","C":"Two-point discrimination using calibrated calipers","D":"Graphesthesia, identifying numbers or letters traced on skin","E":"Romberg testing of standing balance with eyes closed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Graphesthesia is specifically tested by tracing numbers or letters on the skin and asking the patient to identify them, and this patient's inability to do so despite intact stereognosis and vibration indicates a selective graphesthesia deficit, which, like astereognosis, points to PCML or parietal lobe dysfunction. Stereognosis was explicitly preserved in this patient, since he correctly identified the key by touch. Vibration testing was also explicitly normal at the ankles in this vignette. Two-point discrimination was not described as tested here at all. Romberg testing evaluates standing balance with eye closure, not the ability to identify traced skin symbols.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing graphesthesia from stereognosis, tracing symbols versus recognizing objects, is a common bedside-exam vocabulary question. COMLEX: A patient can have selectively impaired graphesthesia with preserved stereognosis, since each depends on slightly different levels of cortical sensory integration.","crossRef":"📖 The sensory system component of the neurologic exam, now used to distinguish graphesthesia from other closely related bedside PCML tests","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with a suspected early posterior column lesion is tested by simultaneously touching his fingertip with two calibrated points and gradually narrowing the distance between them until he reports feeling only a single point. Which specific tactile ability is being measured, and what physiologic mechanism underlies its precision?","choices":{"A":"Two-point discrimination, sharpened by surround inhibition","B":"Vibration sense, sharpened by rapidly adapting Pacinian corpuscle firing","C":"Graphesthesia, sharpened by cortical column integration","D":"Joint position sense, sharpened by muscle spindle feedback loops","E":"Stereognosis, sharpened by combined visual and tactile cross-modal input"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Two-point discrimination measures the minimum distance at which two simultaneously applied stimuli can be perceived as distinct, and its precision depends on surround, or lateral, inhibition present at every relay of the PCML pathway, from the posterior column nuclei through the thalamus and cortex, which sharpens the contrast between adjacent receptive fields. Vibration sense depends on Pacinian corpuscle activation but is a distinct test from two-point discrimination and is not what is being measured with two simultaneous static points. Graphesthesia depends on cortical integration of a traced pattern over time, not simultaneous two-point spatial resolution. Joint position sense depends on muscle spindle and joint afferent feedback, unrelated to cutaneous two-point spatial resolution. Stereognosis requires active exploration of a three-dimensional object and does not depend on cross-modal visual input at all, since it is tested with the eyes closed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two-point discrimination is the classic clinical correlate of surround inhibition, and understanding this mechanism helps explain why densely innervated regions like the fingertip have superior discrimination. COMLEX: Two-point discrimination thresholds vary widely by body region, so normal values must be interpreted relative to the specific site tested.","crossRef":"📖 The neurologic exam's sensory component, now paired with the underlying surround-inhibition mechanism that gives two-point discrimination its clinical precision","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman stands steadily with her feet together and eyes open, but when asked to close her eyes she immediately becomes unsteady and must be caught before falling. Her strength, cranial nerves, and cerebellar finger-to-nose testing are all entirely normal. Which underlying pathway deficit best explains this bedside finding?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar vermis dysfunction causing truncal ataxia independent of vision","B":"Impaired proprioception from posterior column disease, unmasked by eye closure","C":"Vestibular nerve dysfunction causing constant unsteadiness, regardless of vision","D":"Corticospinal tract dysfunction causing spastic gait independent of vision","E":"Isolated pain and temperature pathway dysfunction affecting standing balance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A positive Romberg sign, in which balance is maintained with eyes open but is lost immediately upon eye closure, indicates that the patient has been relying on vision to compensate for impaired proprioceptive input from posterior column disease, and removing that visual input unmasks the underlying sensory ataxia; her normal strength, cranial nerves, and finger-to-nose testing argue against cerebellar, corticospinal, or cranial nerve causes. Cerebellar vermis dysfunction produces truncal ataxia that is present even with eyes open, unlike this patient's eyes-open stability. Vestibular dysfunction typically causes unsteadiness or vertigo that is present regardless of visual input, not one that appears only with eye closure. Corticospinal dysfunction would be expected to also produce weakness or spasticity, which this patient does not have. Pain and temperature pathway dysfunction does not contribute meaningfully to standing balance in the way proprioception does.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Romberg sign's dependence on eye closure to unmask a deficit is the key feature that distinguishes sensory ataxia from cerebellar ataxia, which is present regardless of vision. COMLEX: Recognizing a positive Romberg sign as proprioceptive rather than cerebellar or vestibular in origin is essential for correctly directing further workup, such as B12 level testing or spinal imaging.","crossRef":"📖 The sensory examination component, now applied to correctly interpreting Romberg testing against cerebellar and vestibular alternative explanations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient loses vibration sense and conscious joint position sense in the right leg while pain and temperature sensation remain intact. Which pathway is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Anterolateral spinothalamic pathway","B":"Spinocerebellar pathway to the cerebellum","C":"Posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway","D":"Corticospinal descending motor pathway","E":"Trigeminothalamic facial sensory pathway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway carries vibration, conscious proprioception, and fine tactile discrimination, so its selective damage produces exactly this dissociated loss with sparing of pain and temperature, which travel instead in the anterolateral system. The anterolateral spinothalamic pathway carries pain and temperature, which are preserved here, the spinocerebellar pathway carries unconscious proprioception to the cerebellum rather than conscious sensation, the corticospinal pathway is motor rather than sensory, and the trigeminothalamic pathway carries facial rather than leg sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dissociated sensory loss, vibration/proprioception lost with pain/temperature spared, is the classic dorsal column syndrome signature, as seen in tabes dorsalis and subacute combined degeneration. COMLEX: a positive Romberg sign confirms a proprioceptive rather than cerebellar cause of unsteady gait.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — the PCML pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and position sense, tested here as a board-style pathway-tracing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":3,"stem":"Large-diameter primary afferents carrying discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception enter the spinal cord and then send branches up the posterior columns. Through which part of the posterior root do these fibers enter?","choices":{"A":"Medial division of posterior root","B":"Gray ramus communicans of the root","C":"Anterior root alongside motor axons","D":"Lissauer tract at the entry zone","E":"Anterior white commissure fibers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Large-diameter fibers serving discriminative touch, flutter-vibration, and proprioception (groups Ia, Ib, II, and A-beta) enter the spinal cord via the medial division of the posterior root and then branch, with the largest set ascending in the gracile and cuneate fasciculi. Gray rami communicantes carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the sympathetic chain to the spinal nerve. The anterior root carries motor and preganglionic autonomic efferents out of the cord. The Lissauer tract carries small-diameter A-delta and C fibers that enter laterally. The anterior white commissure is where second-order anterolateral fibers cross, not a primary afferent entry route.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At the root entry zone, large fibers enter medially to the posterior columns and small pain-temperature fibers enter laterally into the Lissauer tract. COMLEX: Selective dorsal root entry zone procedures exploit this separation of large and small fibers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 3 — dorsal horn entry zone, Lissauer tract, and anterior white commissure crossing","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the posterior column-medial lemniscal pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and conscious position sense.","batch":3,"stem":"After synapsing in the dorsal column nuclei, second-order fibers cross the midline as internal arcuate fibers and ascend as which named tract on their way to the thalamus?","choices":{"A":"Medial lemniscus","B":"Lateral lemniscus","C":"Spinothalamic tract","D":"Trigeminal lemniscus","E":"Spinocerebellar tract"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After decussating in the caudal medulla, second-order dorsal column fibers form the medial lemniscus, a ribbon-like tract that ascends through the brainstem to synapse in the ventral posterolateral thalamus. The lateral lemniscus instead carries ascending auditory information, the spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature and crosses at the spinal level rather than the medulla, the trigeminal lemniscus carries facial rather than body sensation, and the spinocerebellar tract carries unconscious proprioception to the cerebellum rather than the thalamus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal arcuate fibers decussate in the medulla to form the medial lemniscus, the key crossing point that determines every lesion's contralateral-versus-ipsilateral effect above versus below this level. COMLEX: medial lemniscus lesions in the brainstem classically pair with cranial nerve findings to produce a crossed sensory syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — the PCML pathway for tactile discrimination, vibration, and position sense, tested here as a board-style pathway-tracing vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":3,"stem":"A rapidly adapting cutaneous mechanoreceptor located in the dermal papillae detects light touch and low-frequency flutter with a small, well-defined receptive field. Which receptor is being described?","choices":{"A":"Merkel disc receptor","B":"Pacinian lamellar corpuscle","C":"Ruffini ending receptor","D":"Meissner corpuscle","E":"Muscle spindle receptor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meissner corpuscles sit within the dermal papillae just beneath the epidermis and are rapidly adapting receptors specialized for light touch and low-frequency flutter with small, precise receptive fields. Merkel discs are instead slowly adapting receptors for sustained pressure and fine spatial detail, Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting receptors for deep pressure and high-frequency vibration with large receptive fields, Ruffini endings are slowly adapting receptors for skin stretch, and muscle spindles detect muscle length changes rather than cutaneous touch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair each receptor by adaptation speed and receptive field size, Merkel and Ruffini are slow-adapting, Meissner and Pacinian are fast-adapting, a grid worth memorizing as a two-by-two table. COMLEX: two-point discrimination testing at the bedside is most sensitive over Merkel- and Meissner-dense fingertip skin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors, tested here as a board-style receptor-identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":3,"stem":"A large, onion-layered cutaneous mechanoreceptor located deep in the dermis and subcutaneous tissue is maximally sensitive to high-frequency vibration, such as that transmitted through a tuning fork. Which receptor is being described?","choices":{"A":"Meissner tactile corpuscle","B":"Pacinian corpuscle","C":"Ruffini ending receptor","D":"Free nerve ending fiber","E":"Merkel disc receptor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Pacinian corpuscle has a distinctive onion-like lamellar capsule, sits deep in the dermis and subcutaneous tissue, and is a rapidly adapting receptor tuned specifically to high-frequency vibration, exactly the stimulus a tuning fork provides at the bedside. Meissner corpuscles instead sit superficially and respond to lower-frequency flutter, Merkel discs are slowly adapting pressure receptors, Ruffini endings detect skin stretch, and free nerve endings mediate pain and temperature rather than vibration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pacinian corpuscles are the physiologic basis for bedside vibration testing with a 128 Hz tuning fork, since they are tuned to exactly that frequency range. COMLEX: loss of vibration sense before joint position sense is a common early finding in length-dependent peripheral neuropathies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors, tested here as a board-style receptor-identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors and relate them to sensory function.","batch":3,"stem":"A proprioceptor located within skeletal muscle, in parallel with extrafusal fibers, signals changes in muscle length and the velocity of stretch. Which receptor is being described?","choices":{"A":"Muscle spindle","B":"Golgi tendon organ","C":"Pacinian corpuscle","D":"Ruffini ending","E":"Free nerve ending"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscle spindle, containing intrafusal fibers arranged in parallel with the surrounding extrafusal fibers, detects both static muscle length and the dynamic velocity of stretch, forming the afferent limb of the stretch reflex. The Golgi tendon organ instead sits in series with extrafusal fibers at the musculotendinous junction and detects muscle tension rather than length, the Pacinian corpuscle detects cutaneous vibration, the Ruffini ending detects skin stretch, and free nerve endings mediate pain and temperature rather than proprioception.","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscle spindles are in parallel and sense length/stretch velocity, while Golgi tendon organs are in series and sense tension, a classic paired distinction. COMLEX: the muscle spindle's Ia afferent is the afferent limb of the monosynaptic stretch reflex tested at the bedside as a deep tendon reflex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — cutaneous mechanoreceptors and proprioceptors, tested here as a board-style receptor-identification vignette","isHighYield":false,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":3,"stem":"Postganglionic sympathetic axons leave the superior cervical ganglion to reach the sweat glands and blood vessels of the face. In the electrophysiologic classification of peripheral nerves, these axons belong to which fiber type?","choices":{"A":"A-beta fiber, medium myelinated","B":"A-delta fiber, thinly myelinated","C":"A-gamma fiber, fusimotor efferent","D":"B fiber, myelinated preganglionic","E":"C fiber, small unmyelinated axon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Postganglionic autonomic fibers are C fibers, unmyelinated axons 0.2 to 2 micrometers in diameter that conduct at 0.5 to 2 meters per second; the same class includes sensory C fibers for thermal, nociceptive, and affective touch signals. A-beta fibers are medium myelinated sensory fibers to skin mechanoreceptors. A-delta fibers are thinly myelinated sensory fibers. A-gamma fibers are motor fibers to intrafusal muscle fibers. B fibers are the myelinated preganglionic autonomic axons that run from the intermediolateral cell column to the ganglion, upstream of this segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preganglionic autonomic = B fibers; postganglionic autonomic = C fibers, which explains why sympathetic and sudomotor function are spared on routine large-fiber nerve conduction studies. COMLEX: Sweat testing assesses postganglionic sudomotor C fibers in suspected small-fiber or autonomic neuropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — visceral afferent pathways versus autonomic efferent pathways","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":3,"stem":"On examination, a patient's discriminative touch, vibration sense, and joint position sense are tested. In the Roman numeral classification of afferent fibers, which groups carry these submodalities?","choices":{"A":"Groups III and IV","B":"Groups Ia, Ib, and II","C":"Groups II, III, and IV","D":"Group III and B fibers","E":"Group IV and B fibers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Discriminative touch, vibratory sense, and position sense are transmitted by the large-diameter group Ia, Ib, and II fibers, which correspond to the A-alpha and A-beta classes and supply muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, and skin mechanoreceptors. Groups III and IV are the A-delta and C fibers that carry thermal and nociceptive signals. A combination of II, III, and IV mixes a large-fiber group with two small-fiber groups. B fibers are preganglionic autonomic efferents and are not part of the afferent group system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Large fibers (I and II) serve the dorsal column modalities; small fibers (III and IV) serve the anterolateral modalities. COMLEX: Large-fiber neuropathy presents with lost vibration and position sense and a positive Romberg sign, while small-fiber neuropathy presents with burning pain and lost temperature sense.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 2 — A-delta and C fibers by myelination, conduction velocity, and pain quality","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish A-alpha, A-beta, A-delta, and C fibers by modality, diameter, myelination, and conduction velocity.","batch":3,"stem":"A topical local anesthetic is applied to a patch of skin. Touch and pressure sensation remain intact while pain sensation is markedly reduced. This selective effect is best explained by preferential blockade of which fiber type?","choices":{"A":"A-alpha fiber, large myelinated","B":"A-beta fiber, medium myelinated","C":"Golgi tendon organ afferent fiber","D":"B fiber, autonomic preganglionic","E":"C fiber, small unmyelinated"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Local anesthetics preferentially block smaller-diameter, unmyelinated C fibers before larger myelinated fibers, since smaller fibers require a shorter length of blocked membrane to prevent action potential propagation, so pain and temperature carried by C fibers are lost first while touch and pressure carried by the larger A-beta fibers are relatively spared. A-alpha fibers carry proprioception and are the most resistant to blockade given their large diameter, A-beta fibers carry the touch sensation that remains intact here, B fibers are autonomic rather than cutaneous sensory fibers, and Golgi tendon afferents carry tension information from muscle rather than cutaneous pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: local anesthetics block small unmyelinated C fibers first, then A-delta, then the larger myelinated fibers last, the differential blockade order tested on boards. COMLEX: this same size-dependent sensitivity explains the clinical sequence of anesthesia onset, pain and temperature loss first, followed later by motor block.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — peripheral fiber classification by modality, diameter, and conduction velocity, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has loss of vibration and proprioception in the leg but not the arm, following an isolated dorsal column lesion. Given the somatotopic organization of the dorsal columns, which specific tract was most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Fasciculus cuneatus","B":"Medial lemniscus","C":"Lateral corticospinal tract","D":"Fasciculus gracilis","E":"Spinocerebellar tract"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The fasciculus gracilis carries lower-body, including leg, dorsal column information and lies medially within the dorsal columns, while the fasciculus cuneatus carries upper-body, including arm, information more laterally, so an isolated leg deficit localizes to the fasciculus gracilis. The fasciculus cuneatus would instead produce an arm-predominant deficit, the medial lemniscus is a brainstem structure carrying both body regions together after they have already merged, the lateral corticospinal tract is motor rather than sensory, and the spinocerebellar tract carries unconscious rather than conscious proprioception.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gracilis is medial and carries the leg; cuneatus is lateral, carries the arm, and is present only at and above about T6. COMLEX: the medial lemniscus rotates as it ascends, so in VPL the arm is medial and the leg lateral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — PCML somatotopic organization, tested here as a board-style localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"Within the primary somatosensory cortex, neurons in the shell region of the ventral posterolateral nucleus relay muscle spindle input. Which cytoarchitectural area receives this muscle spindle input predominantly?","choices":{"A":"Area 3a","B":"Area 3b","C":"Area 1","D":"Area 5","E":"Area 7"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Areas 3a and 2 are targeted mainly by neurons in the shell region of the ventral posterolateral nucleus and receive proprioceptive input, muscle spindle afferents going mainly to area 3a, located in the depths of the central sulcus next to motor area 4, and Golgi tendon organ and joint afferents mainly to area 2. Areas 3b and 1 are targeted by core neurons and receive cutaneous input from Meissner corpuscles and Merkel cells. Areas 5 and 7 are posterior parietal association regions that receive input from SI and the pulvinar-lateral posterior group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SI is organized by submodality as well as by body part: 3a = spindles, 3b and 1 = cutaneous, 2 = joints, tendons, and size and shape. COMLEX: Area 3a's position beside motor cortex supports the tight sensorimotor coupling needed for posture and fine movement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — PCML somatotopic organization, tested here as a board-style localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the somatotopic organization of the fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus, medial lemniscus, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex.","batch":3,"stem":"As the medial lemniscus ascends through the midbrain toward the thalamus, it is shifted laterally and posteriorly by the appearance of a large medial structure. Which structure displaces it?","choices":{"A":"Substantia nigra","B":"Red nucleus","C":"Superior colliculus","D":"Cerebral aqueduct","E":"Oculomotor nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The medial lemniscus rotates in the pons so that the upper extremity lies medially and the lower extremity laterally, and as it traverses the midbrain it is shifted laterally and posteriorly by the appearance of medial structures such as the red nucleus, keeping its somatotopy until it ends in the ventral posterolateral nucleus. The substantia nigra lies internal to the crus cerebri, anterior and lateral to the tegmental lemniscus. The superior colliculus forms the midbrain tectum, posterior to the aqueduct. The cerebral aqueduct is a midline channel surrounded by periaqueductal gray. The oculomotor nucleus lies near the midline beneath the aqueduct and is too small to displace the lemniscus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Track the medial lemniscus by level: anteromedial (vertical) in the medulla, horizontal in the pons, and lateral in the midbrain beside the red nucleus. COMLEX: A small lateral midbrain tegmental lesion can produce isolated contralateral loss of discriminative touch and proprioception.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — midbrain tegmental structures and their positions","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":3,"stem":"A small midline lesion in the caudal medulla destroys the internal arcuate fibers exactly where they cross to form the sensory decussation, sparing the gracile and cuneate nuclei and the trigeminal nuclei. Where would loss of discriminative touch and vibration be expected?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral body below the lesion","B":"Contralateral body, sparing the face","C":"Ipsilateral face and contralateral body","D":"Bilateral body sensation, face spared","E":"Contralateral face and both arms"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Internal arcuate fibers from the gracile and cuneate nuclei of both sides loop anteromedially and cross the midline as the sensory decussation, so a midline lesion at that point interrupts crossing fibers from both sides and causes bilateral loss of discriminative touch, vibration, and position sense over the body, while facial sensation, relayed through the spared trigeminal nuclei, is preserved. Ipsilateral loss would follow a posterior column or posterior column nucleus lesion caudal to the crossing. Contralateral loss would follow a unilateral medial lemniscus lesion rostral to the crossing. Ipsilateral face with contralateral body is the crossed pattern of a lateral medullary lesion for pain and temperature. Contralateral face with both arms has no anatomic basis here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crossing points are vulnerable to midline lesions that produce bilateral deficits: sensory decussation (dorsal columns), anterior white commissure (pain-temperature), and optic chiasm (vision). COMLEX: Bilateral body proprioceptive loss with a spared face points to the cervicomedullary junction or the dorsal columns.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 5 — ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing, tested here as a board-style localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops loss of vibration and proprioception on the left side of the body below the neck, sparing the face, following a lesion above the level of the medullary decussation. Which side of the lesion is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Left side, above the decussation","B":"Right side, below the decussation","C":"Right side, above the decussation","D":"Left side, below the decussation","E":"Bilateral, at the decussation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dorsal column fibers remain ipsilateral until they decussate as internal arcuate fibers in the caudal medulla, so a lesion above this crossing point, such as in the upper medulla, pons, midbrain, thalamus, or cortex, produces contralateral sensory loss, meaning a right-sided lesion explains this left-sided deficit. A left-sided lesion above the decussation would instead produce left-sided rather than right-sided deficits, a lesion below the decussation on either side would produce ipsilateral rather than contralateral loss, and a bilateral lesion at the decussation itself would be expected to produce bilateral rather than strictly unilateral findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: below the medullary decussation, dorsal column deficits are ipsilateral to the lesion; above it, they become contralateral, the single most tested crossing-level localization rule for this pathway. COMLEX: always identify whether a lesion is above or below this specific medullary landmark before predicting which side will be affected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 5 — ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing, tested here as a board-style localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Localize lesions based on ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops loss of vibration and proprioception on the same side as a spinal cord lesion, below the level of injury. This ipsilateral pattern confirms the lesion is located at which position relative to the decussation?","choices":{"A":"Above the medullary decussation","B":"At the level of the thalamus","C":"At the level of the primary cortex","D":"At the level of the pons","E":"Below the medullary decussation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Any dorsal column lesion located below the medullary decussation, including the entire spinal cord, produces ipsilateral sensory loss below the lesion, since the fibers have not yet crossed at that level. A lesion above the decussation, at the thalamus, cortex, or pons, would instead produce contralateral rather than ipsilateral findings, since the fibers have already crossed by those levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spinal cord dorsal column lesions are always ipsilateral, a direct consequence of the crossing occurring only in the medulla, well above the spinal cord. COMLEX: this rule is a fast way to distinguish a spinal cord lesion from a brainstem, thalamic, or cortical one purely from the side of the deficit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 5 — ipsilateral versus contralateral sensory loss and the level of crossing, tested here as a board-style localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has loss of fine touch discrimination over the left leg and trunk but normal fine touch discrimination over the left face. Which structure carries facial discriminative touch separately from the body pathway, explaining this sparing?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal column gracile and cuneate nuclei","B":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus of the brainstem","C":"Ventral posterolateral thalamic nucleus","D":"Main sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve","E":"Nucleus solitarius of the medulla"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Facial discriminative touch travels via the trigeminal nerve to the main, or chief, sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve in the pons, an analog of the dorsal column nuclei but entirely separate from the body pathway, so a lesion affecting the body dorsal columns spares the face whenever this trigeminal relay remains intact. The dorsal column nuclei relay body rather than facial touch, the ventral posterolateral nucleus relays body rather than facial sensation, the spinal trigeminal nucleus carries facial pain and temperature rather than discriminative touch, and the nucleus solitarius carries visceral and taste information rather than somatosensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main sensory nucleus of CN V is the facial equivalent of the dorsal column nuclei, carrying discriminative touch for the face just as the gracile and cuneate nuclei do for the body. COMLEX: this parallel organization means facial and body discriminative touch can be selectively spared or lost independently depending on lesion location.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — body versus trigeminal discriminative touch pathways, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":3,"stem":"Second-order fibers carrying facial discriminative touch from the main sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve cross the midline and ascend to the thalamus as which named tract, the facial counterpart of the medial lemniscus?","choices":{"A":"Medial lemniscus","B":"Lateral lemniscus","C":"Spinal trigeminal tract","D":"Trigeminothalamic tract, uncrossed","E":"Trigeminal lemniscus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The trigeminal, or trigeminothalamic, lemniscus carries crossed facial discriminative touch from the main sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve to the ventral posteromedial thalamic nucleus, serving as the direct facial analog of the medial lemniscus for the body. The medial lemniscus carries body rather than facial touch, the lateral lemniscus carries ascending auditory information, the spinal trigeminal tract carries facial pain and temperature rather than discriminative touch, and an uncrossed trigeminothalamic tract does not correctly describe this pathway, since these second-order fibers do cross the midline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the trigeminal lemniscus is the facial mirror of the medial lemniscus, both crossing and both terminating in a body-appropriate VPL/VPM thalamic nucleus. COMLEX: recognizing this parallel pathway structure resolves most questions comparing body and facial discriminative touch side by side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — body versus trigeminal discriminative touch pathways, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate body dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathways from trigeminal discriminative touch pathways for the face.","batch":3,"stem":"Two-point discrimination on the lips and perioral skin is as precise as on the fingertips, and the perioral region has the largest representation along the postcentral gyrus. Which peripheral feature best explains this acuity?","choices":{"A":"Dense Pacinian corpuscles within the deep dermis","B":"Uncrossed posterior trigeminothalamic fibers","C":"Mesencephalic nucleus proprioceptive endings","D":"Numerous Meissner corpuscles beneath the lips","E":"Small-diameter C fibers in the perioral skin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A large number of encapsulated receptors, particularly Meissner corpuscles, lie beneath the lips and perioral skin, and this high innervation density with small receptive fields makes perioral two-point discrimination comparable with that of the fingertips and gives the region its large cortical representation. Pacinian corpuscles have large receptive fields and signal high-frequency vibration. Uncrossed posterior trigeminothalamic fibers carry oral cavity input from the dorsomedial principal nucleus to the ipsilateral thalamus, a central route that does not create peripheral acuity. Mesencephalic nucleus neurons innervate jaw muscle spindles and periodontal receptors rather than lip skin. C fibers carry thermal, nociceptive, and affective touch signals, not fine spatial detail.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acuity follows receptor density and receptive field size, which is why fingertips and lips have the finest two-point discrimination and the largest homunculus areas. COMLEX: Test facial discriminative touch on the lips and perioral skin, where deficits are easiest to detect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — cutaneous mechanoreceptor density and receptive field size","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with longstanding, poorly controlled diabetes develops symmetric, length-dependent loss of vibration and light touch beginning in the toes and gradually ascending. Which level of the sensory pathway is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal column of the spinal cord","B":"Medial lemniscus of the brainstem","C":"Peripheral sensory nerve","D":"Ventral posterolateral thalamus","E":"Primary somatosensory cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diabetic peripheral neuropathy is a length-dependent process affecting the longest peripheral sensory axons first, producing this classic symmetric, distal-to-proximal, stocking-glove pattern beginning in the toes, distinguishing it from a discrete central nervous system lesion. A dorsal column, medial lemniscus, thalamic, or cortical lesion would instead typically produce a more abrupt, level-based, or hemibody pattern rather than this gradual, symmetric, length-dependent distal progression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a symmetric, length-dependent, distal-predominant sensory loss pattern is the classic peripheral polyneuropathy signature, most commonly from diabetes in this population. COMLEX: annual monofilament and vibration testing is a standard diabetic foot care screening tool for exactly this complication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 7 — dorsal column, medial lemniscus, thalamic, cortical, and peripheral nerve correlation, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A large middle cerebral artery infarct destroys the hand area of the primary somatosensory cortex. A student suggests the secondary somatosensory cortex will take over tactile processing for that hand. Why is this unlikely?","choices":{"A":"SII also depends on the middle cerebral artery","B":"SII lacks any somatotopic body representation","C":"SII receives no input from the thalamus","D":"SII lies within the occipital lobe cortex","E":"SII is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The secondary somatosensory cortex lies deep in the upper bank of the lateral sulcus, has its own somatotopic map, and receives input from the ipsilateral SI and the ventral posterior inferior nucleus of the thalamus, but it is also supplied primarily by the middle cerebral artery, so it cannot substitute functionally for SI after compromise of that artery. SII does contain a somatotopic representation of the body surface. It receives thalamic input from the ventral posterior inferior nucleus. It lies in the parietal operculum along the lateral sulcus, not in the occipital lobe. Its supply is from the middle cerebral artery rather than the posterior cerebral artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SI receives its blood from the ACA (leg) and MCA (face and arm), and SII from the MCA; MCA strokes cause contralateral face and arm tactile loss. COMLEX: Expect limited recovery of discriminative touch after large MCA infarcts because both SI and SII share that supply.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — ACA/MCA/PCA cortical territory correlation, tested here as a board-style stroke-localization vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate dorsal column disease, medial lemniscus lesions, thalamic lesions, cortical sensory lesions, and peripheral neuropathies with clinical findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops sudden, severe, burning contralateral hemibody pain and allodynia weeks after an initially painless sensory stroke affecting vibration, proprioception, and light touch. Which level of the sensory pathway was most likely originally infarcted?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral sensory nerve","B":"Dorsal column of the spinal cord","C":"Ventral posterolateral thalamus","D":"Medial lemniscus of the brainstem","E":"Primary somatosensory cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A thalamic stroke affecting the ventral posterolateral nucleus produces this classic evolution, an initial painless sensory deficit followed weeks later by delayed, severe, central neuropathic pain known as Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome. A peripheral nerve, dorsal column, or medial lemniscus lesion would not classically produce this delayed central pain syndrome, and a primary somatosensory cortex lesion, while it can rarely cause central pain, is a far less classic and less frequently tested localization for this specific delayed-pain evolution than the thalamus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed-onset burning central pain following a sensory stroke is the classic Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome vignette, localizing specifically to the ventral posterolateral thalamus. COMLEX: this pain is often refractory to standard analgesics and instead requires neuropathic pain-targeted agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 7 — dorsal column, medial lemniscus, thalamic, cortical, and peripheral nerve correlation, tested here as a board-style discrimination vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient stands with feet together and eyes open without difficulty, but becomes markedly unsteady and sways as soon as the eyes are closed. Which bedside test result and underlying deficit does this describe?","choices":{"A":"Negative Romberg sign, cerebellar ataxia","B":"Positive Romberg sign, cerebellar ataxia","C":"Negative Romberg sign, proprioceptive nerve loss","D":"Positive Romberg sign, spinothalamic loss","E":"Positive Romberg sign, proprioceptive loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A positive Romberg sign, worsened balance specifically with eye closure, reflects loss of proprioceptive input that was previously being compensated for by visual feedback, so removing vision unmasks the underlying dorsal column deficit. Cerebellar ataxia instead produces unsteadiness that is present with the eyes both open and closed, since it does not depend on visual compensation, making the Romberg test unhelpful for distinguishing it, and spinothalamic loss affects pain and temperature, which contribute little to standing balance, so it cannot explain a positive Romberg sign, which classically indicates a proprioceptive, dorsal-column-pattern deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a positive Romberg sign specifically indicates a proprioceptive (dorsal column or peripheral nerve) rather than cerebellar cause of imbalance, since cerebellar patients are unsteady regardless of vision. COMLEX: use the Romberg test to decide whether ataxia workup should focus on the dorsal columns and peripheral nerves versus the cerebellum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — bedside sensory testing, tested here as a board-style clinical-application vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":3,"stem":"A clinician draws a number on a patient's palm with a blunt object while the patient's eyes are closed, and asks the patient to identify the number. Which bedside sensory test and cortical pathway requirement does this describe?","choices":{"A":"Stereognosis, intact peripheral nerve","B":"Stereognosis, intact cortical sensory integration","C":"Two-point discrimination, intact peripheral nerve","D":"Graphesthesia, intact peripheral nerve","E":"Graphesthesia, intact cortical sensory integration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Graphesthesia, identifying numbers or letters traced on the skin, requires not only intact peripheral and dorsal column pathways but also intact cortical sensory integration in the primary and association somatosensory cortex, making it a useful test for detecting a parietal lobe lesion even when basic touch sensation is preserved. Stereognosis instead tests the ability to identify an object by touch and manipulation rather than tracing, and describing either test as requiring only peripheral nerve integrity is incorrect, since both critically depend on intact cortical processing to be performed correctly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: graphesthesia and stereognosis are both cortical sensory integration tests that can be abnormal even with fully intact peripheral and dorsal column pathways, localizing specifically to the parietal lobe. COMLEX: astereognosis and agraphesthesia with otherwise normal primary sensation are classic signs of a cortical rather than peripheral or spinal lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — bedside sensory testing, tested here as a board-style clinical-application vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl29_bloom_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use bedside sensory tests such as vibration, joint position, two-point discrimination, graphesthesia, stereognosis, and Romberg testing to infer pathway involvement.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient can correctly identify vibration and light touch on formal peripheral testing but is unable to identify a key placed in the hand by touch alone, with eyes closed. Which bedside finding and lesion location best explain this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Astereognosis, peripheral sensory nerve","B":"Astereognosis, parietal association cortex","C":"Agraphesthesia, dorsal column of the cord","D":"Anesthesia, ventral posterolateral thalamus","E":"Allodynia, medial lemniscus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Astereognosis, the inability to identify an object by touch despite intact primary sensory modalities such as vibration and light touch, localizes to the parietal association cortex, which is responsible for integrating basic sensory input into complex object recognition. A peripheral sensory nerve lesion would be expected to also impair the basic vibration and light touch testing that remains intact here, agraphesthesia refers specifically to number or letter identification rather than object identification, anesthesia refers to a complete loss of basic sensation rather than a selective integration deficit, and allodynia refers to pain from a normally non-painful stimulus rather than an object-identification deficit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: astereognosis with normal primary sensation is a classic parietal association cortex sign, distinct from a primary sensory pathway lesion at any lower level. COMLEX: testing stereognosis and graphesthesia together is an efficient bedside way to screen for a parietal lobe process when basic sensory testing is unremarkable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 8 — bedside sensory testing, tested here as a board-style clinical-application vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}]};
